        <p>DEBATE BETWEEN MISS MARGARET ROBERTSON AND MR A. H. GROSFIELD</p>
        <p>A ONE-SII</p>
        <p>tireat interest centred ia a public debate on the wouien'a suffrage question. between Miss Margaret Robertson, 8.A., i.nd Mr. A. H. Crosfield, which took . .</p>
        <p>place in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on U'etlnesday evening. The meeting had been arranged by Mks Pemberton and was under the auspices of the Warrington Women's Suffrage Society. The Fall wan well-filled with an audience which followed the speeches with great interest. Mr.C.l:. Parker pre- sided end in addition to the debaters, Miss Massy (president of the Women's Suffrage Society) was on the platform. Mrs. Croatiald, of Colwyn Bay. was present in die audience. The proposition for debate was :—" That the time is now ripe for giving the Pal liarnentary vote to women on the same terms as it is or may be given to men." Miss Robertson opened in the affirmative and Mr. Crosfield took the negative view. Then there was an interval during whira members of the audience were at liberty to express their views on the question. Afterwards Mr. Crodield and Bliss Robertson each made a second speech and a show of hands was taken, the result being that the resolution in favour of the extension of the franchise to women wan carried by an overwhelming majority, which conaisted chiefly of ladies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>MISS ROBERTSON LEADS OFF</head>
        <p>MISS ROBERTSON LEADS OFF</p>
        <p>MISS 11.01EIIT.ON said she would not detain them with arguing the inherent logic and justice of the case ' bat would confine herself to the urgency of the vote being granted to women. Now she would juat say in passing that the maxim that " taxation and repre- sentation go together " was a very old matim not invented by women to apply to their special es. only (Laughter.) She would also like the men to remember that every argument they hail used for freedom and self-government for which their fathers fought and died. ako applied lathe case of the women in their struggle for freedom and self government to-day. But she wanted them to consider first and foremost</p>
        <p>ENOItMOUII''fIE II I SKIN II SUE:</p>
        <p>in the position of women during the past 100 years to snake this ilocation urgent. It was not more than 100 years ago since a leanied writer could say with- out any fear of rajp.ing a storm that there were porno people who should he no share in controlling the State because they were naturally incapable of exercising a a sound judgment. Such, he aided, were criminals, lunatics and women. (Laughter.) Nearer our own time a great writ er said that infants should not vote and women were perpetual infants. (Laughter.) And at the same time a famous doctor said—and again there was no storm raised—" We like to hear a few words of benne from a woman as we do from a parrot, becatme they are no unexpected." (laughter.) Since that time women had had their opportunity. Women had claimed the right to, a, any rate, find out whether they had got brains or not. She was glad to say there were many generous men who bad stood shoulder to shoulder in the struggle to help women to get the rights of education, which they never could have got without the help of men. Since women had got the right of education she thought they had undoubtedly proved their possession of brains,and she doubted whether anyone now would dare to advance , front the platform the argument that women should not vote because they were intellectually inferior to teen. Every other argument against women's euffrage —that woman's place was the home, that physical force was the basis of Government.' that women did not want the vote—all those' had grown up since women had destroyed the only real argument that, they were oble to t.e against WOWISS'S enfranchisement. Since women had Iteen educated they had naturally claimed the right to use the brains they possensed They had entered thel professions. They found women teachers and doctors,' and did they not know that a woman doctor hieing a registered medical practitioner could give a certificate of insanity which deprived a man alias Parliamentary rate? ,Laughter.) She could also judge when a man Was in a lit state to make him capable of voting and still she Was not allowed to cast a RI rliameutary vote herself. (Laughter.) Women had not only entered the prof,sions ; they had claimed their right in other ways to use their brains and their talents for the good of the community. The cry of the women hall always been for mom: SCort: To cot: ttitiui cols-Ens</p>
        <p>for the good of the nation. The women had entered municipal politics ; they bad got the right to vote a; municipal elections; they were allowed to stand as guardians and town councillors. One seldom met anyone nowadays who denied the great and useful work worsen had lone in those positions. (Hear. bear.) And yet exactly the same outcry woe raised against giving the municipal rots to women as was taised now against giving them the Parliamentary vote.. Some arguments brought forward were that they ' would neglect their homes. When women wanted to be educated they were told that the women who learned algebra could not care for children —(laughter) that women who knew Latin could not possibly cook. I,aughtero. That wan exactly the same argument which they used against women in their present struggle. So-would like them to remember that Lord James of Hereford, who waa now a pillar of the Anti-Suffrage League, when the Bill to give the municipal vote to women was before the House, spite and voted against it. And why? He acid," If you give the municipal vote to women you can no longer withhold the Parlia- mentary vote, for the one is the logical and inevitable coneequence of the other." (Applause.) What powers, talents, or capacities were required for cast- ing the Parliamentary vote that were not required for casting the municipal vote ? The women as Coun- cillors and Guardians had to administer the laws and those who administered the lawn were generally hest able to eriticige them and hest able to recommend ways in which those lax s might possibly be amended. In Oldham they had now elected</p>
        <p>of that great town. )Applause). What qualities were required in the average voter which were not required in the Mayor? The absurdity of the pesition was pretty clear. (Applause). Though it was very important in considering that question, it A. 114 flit nearly so important as the deep wrong done to the poor, to the working wonien,lty depriving them ef the protection of the vote. Under the present industrial conditions— which was another of the great changers which had taken place in the last 100 years—it had become easential that women should have the vote at once. Some said that women ;should atop at home. One hundred years ago women could do work in their homes ; they did spinning, weaning, baking, blew- ing, candle making, FOB p making. What had happened note? The men had invented machinery ; they had taken the woutan'a work out of the house into the factory, and if the woman man to go on doing her own work she had got to go into the factory, where she worked under rules and regulations made by a Government which she was absolutely unable to touch. What was the result on the industrial conditions of women? There were 5,.100,01/0 women working for their living and they knew very well that, with the one exception of the weaving department iu the cotton industry, they Were</p>
        <p>PUT, AT A I,WEII 1,11:</p>
        <p>even in piecework than the wen who did precisely the tame work. The result was that they had a great! army of underpaid, sweated labour which was one of the greatest dangers to men as well as to women. It meant that men were knocked out and ousted on every side. The men had their unions to keep wages up. That was all very sell, but when the master could turn off his men and take on women for half the money, did they think that he was going to pay the trade union rate to the men ? She was not talking in the air; that was happening every day. Theemployera said to the men "Go home arid send your wives they will do the work for half the money." When the anti-suffragists told them that suffragists wanted to bring women into the labour ntarket, they make a great mistake. They wanted to make it possible for the women to leave the factories atui go hoer e. Half the women were in the factor ien not because they wanted logo there tart becau, their husbands had no work and tile WOMPII must work or their fantiliea would starve. Once the women could demand equal wages for equal work the men would tie drawn back and no-one would begladder of that than the suffragist. (Applause). Continuing. Miss I:obertson said that the largest employer of labour in the country as-as the Government and they set a double standard of pay for men •nzl Women. Who-, in the school inspect,. 8. the man when he started had f:.llar a year more than the woman when she reached the lop of her profession, and that applied all the way down mail it cm, to the hey and girl</p>
      </div>
