        <p>poi il te:whers in die echo As —and they could not say 1 that the I, ly pupil tescher ought t , lisve higher nay because he hail to keep a family. (Laughter). Women under the Government could be sweated to starvation, while men wo: king tinder Government must have fair wages Why ?—the men had known bow to use their Tates, for they got</p>
        <p>- --S FOR ~ WE.. c; W,E in every Government contract which made it illegal to pan the man at le,s than the trade union rates. . ;Applause , Mr. Lloyd George speaking in the Albert Hiill, was not afraid to say that the inequality of pay between men and women worl-Mg for the I lovernment would be impossible if women had the I',l rliamentary vote i.e he said) the same power to call the Govern- nnant to account as the men had. That was one side of the danger of the cheapness of women t there was another more awful side still. There were millions of women who by honest work could riot earn enough to keep body and soul together and they knew the result of that. That was one of the rea,ons why the question was urgent. Every day , LE, .I,l.tTli IN Ilt,A111: Mr rI:F, 110!1F:5T,4..</p>
        <p>It was all very well to tell women that politics were pot her business, when politico were chiefly concerned with wars and whot were at any rote considered to be men's, afters, though women had a lot to do even with those questions. Now the men told the women hoe their child,en were to sleep, whether they must hirer firegutrd or not or whether they must dteot their children in flannelette. (lawgiver.) DAI they not think it war high time that w-men were consulted in politics ? (Applause.) Bat they were tohl thud Romer, did not,want the ants and Cost if they aid they woold give it then, to-ranrrow. She was perfectly really to admit that there were thousands and thou— ands of women who did not want the vote ; but there were _</p>
        <p>T-I.I;SANIPS OF NIES WIU 1,11, N 4 or ISE THE VOTE .</p>
        <p>and many more tfiensands who would not 1100 it unle.a they not a motor ride to tie roll (Laughter.) They should aho remember that !nen had the opportunity</p>
        <p>&amp;reef °ill, London,</p>
        <p>MISS ROBERTSON.</p>
        <p>of political education—their rulers educated them bscatise the men had votes, and not until the women had votes would they take the same steps. Miss Robertson mentioned that nearly every wonien'a organisation was in favour of the vote being given to women immediately, and that out of 55:: women doctu,s,3S had declared in favour of women's suffrage. (Applause.) The question was urgent ale° because of the W,TE SM. THE terrEnsEs. CI THE Women who would otherwise be engaged in philan- thropic work were now working on behalf of the woolen's cause and would not give it up until they had won. (Applause.) It seemed to her that , was a deplorable waste of many of the ablest women. In concluding the speaker said that there were hundreds of their best women breaking their hearts to-day for that cause and she thought there was no more deplorable side. It would in future be regarded as an almost incredible page of history to see parli 'meat after parliament admitting the justice of the women's claim and vet refusing it. and the women with a tragic, pitiful, futile resentment. very often hurling themselves against it and suffering terribly. while many hundreds of women were spend- ing their hies in irksorne,toilsonm work for the cause, all the time being told that it was just and right but the time was not met ripe. There was only one way in which they could bring that painful struggle tons end. The women were pot going to rest till they hail got the vote. They all knew that. (Applause./ They might not like it but it was inevitable. Women's Suffrage was hound to mine. Why not do the just and straight thong. put their principles into practice and admit that " the time is now ripe for giving the Parliamentary vote to WOMPU on the saline terms as it is or may be given to men." (Loud applause).</p>
