      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>MISS ROBERTSON'S -REPLY.</head>
        <p>MISS ROBERTSON'S -REPLY.</p>
        <p>Miun iiolllot,ON, in winding nip the debate, con-1 traded that Mr. Crostield had not produced grounds for his statement that the majority of women di 1 net want the vote. He could not rightly draw that infer- race from the fact that • certain proportion of the municipal voters, cumbering only 30,000 of the millions of women in this country, had declared against it. Tee anti-suffragists made a canvass of six wards in Man- chester and they stated that only 192 women, including half-symputhisers, wanted the vote. Without. moment's deley the suffragists started Bowman of the same wards their object being to piece the results being the Priods Minister on • depntstion which had been arranged. Hewing less then • week at their diaposal they could only canvass two of the sin wards, and in those two they found 420 in favour of the suffrage, which would hare worked out at 1,200 for the six wards. (Applause.) Srrely it we.. it figure of speech for Mr. Crostleld to talk übout passing the vote in (be teeth 411 the vast majority of worn.. Municipal voters might all in pretteerthi, but WHAT AtiIIATION WAS /IMRE lv Tilt t'OUSTItt Put INer Studio ?</p>
        <p>Did they really think that if the vote were given to women who paid nstea and totes to-morrow, there would be any great ilistree• or oonfusion Amongst the women of the country who now maid they did not want it? There was nothing comparable in intensity in the feeling of the women who lid not want the vote to the feeling of those who did. (applause.) They generally found that the women who did not want tbe vote were thoee who had ,gvE{, ~NoWN WHAT Jr"An TO WANT ANYTHINO IN 1,11,</p>
        <p>(Applause.) Astor the working class women who were indifferent to the question, abe contended that they hail not had full Opportunities of knowing what the power of the Parliamentary vote was. .firey knew their grievances and wanted to famed) them, but they bad I never hod the chance of understanding the Item ing of !political power on those grievances. Warr not the inlet- don to re decided whetner women needed the rote rather than whether they wanted it ? If, as Mr. Crab. field admitted, the extension of the franchise might do something to hotter the torr inf. condi tone under which ...fly women worked, was not that sufficient joatiflca- tion for it ? Mr. Croatleld said he faltered in , an wooded sphere of usefulness and a wider horizon for women, but we were to decide in what channels their activity might beat be developed. Ilow had it been decided in what channels the activities of men were best developed? By leaving them tree scope, by giving thorn the opportunity to find out in what channels their acti•ities might beat be developed. !Applause!. There were things that men were not fitted for, but there was n•i low w forbid them engaging in fowls occupations. Men were not for- bidden by law to be nurse maids--I laughter —ar to look after children, hut they left that work to the women who were better suited for it. But when it mime to women, we tbonght it necesalry to make boas fornidding them to d•i things of which we said they were incapable—just as if we yawed slant FoRISIDOINO TO SW ISt.</p>
        <p>(Laughter.) The tostenoe which Mr. Croatian bed quoted of the refusal of women to go on a certain committee et Bank Quny Mission, while they were willing to on others, only went to prove thet they knew what work they could fittingly do end whet work wee "tore suitable for the men. And so they could trust til,t if ever women got into Parliament— : they were not working for that, but Mr. Crustleld ...rued to hope it would conm—(laughter)—they would have the sense to go on the committees concerning the Children'. Bill. houtiug, o,llcation. end so forth, end leave the No•y and Army committees to the turn. Applause / Mr. Crosfield claimed the right to make</p>
        <p>suggestions in the inaorigement of his owo bonne bold. Lsughter. The woolen cisimed the right t make suggeetions in natinual affairs. They did not claim the right to do the detail business they</p>
        <p>MO NOT 0,0 TO 111 I'itIiNI&amp;NCNT AND OFFICIAIN</p>
