        <p>NEW BREEDS</p>
        <p>ontintr •el</p>
        <p>Altlicugh the Orpington family is already a large rue, it continues to inereme ; for we now have the Cock. and Blue Orpingtons tw swell the the list. At present.these new varieties are in no few halld, that little can be said about them. I consider new varieties of the Orpington • superfluity Already the [UT and White varieties come very near embodying.' all the es.ential.t of an all-round general purpose fowl. Taking that fart into consideration w ith the ; good taints of the rest of the Orpington family lean- not a 9 why unearth we should be troubled with still more co:t.utns. Owe could ut.derstand better it it was an entirely new breed which watt in the proceed of manufacture. But it is not. Rest assured of this; For a bird of the class and type ouch en the present day Orpington is, you cannot beat the Buff, Black and White. Why all this craze for no many colours of one breed Let us have On many new bred. as you like, penciling oh., that each new breed is an improvement on the old. But nave us from too mane cola ,re. It is not always good to have too much "dazzle." Further on I olisll make n plea for some of the old breeds. At present though I cannot pass without further mention of i titer now breeds. Practically speaking the</p>
        <p>110, o,lllEli MIN, 1.</p>
        <p>is a new breed. At least it is new to the general public. One prominent breeder has, however, been progressing with this variety for quite a number of years ; and shall I any with a far amount of success. The speefic object oi the orginator of the English ft,,,aeeombed Minorca was sole y for utility purpoaces. This fact in itself forces me to consider closely the new breed on its utility merits. It is not a new colour of the Minorca but the same colour. physically altered from a purely utilitarian point of view. I cannot do better than give to my readers tenni-thing of what Mr. It. W. Webster has to ely of his new breed. Writing fur the ' Feathered World " year book for 1911 he says : The history .d the origin of Black Itceecowli Minorcaa in relation to myself may be briefly told. For many years I kept the siutle- combed Minorca in large numbers, chiefly fur egg production. I stocked a farm of Faures with them, the birds being kept in colonies about the meadows Will fields. For ten years I retired annually several hunilteds, and alive)s wintered it less than 500 of these fowls. Many of the best @pectinous reared were sold either for exhibition or stock purpose., and it was necessary in the raw of these birds to protect their combs from the effects of frost and cold east winds More especially was this so with cockerels. If not adequately proteetel, their combs would become frost bitten, and ha one night a cockerel worth many pounds wood be irretrievably ruined. • With a large Immo, of birds to attend to. thin involved a great deal of additional labour. and it often occurred to me that the trouble would be entirely overcome if only a rose or double comb could be enbatituted for the single comb, while at the name time retai aim; all the remain- ing chatacteristice and good qualities of the ainglecomlmd breed. Following out my ideas. I set to work to produce, if possible, such a bird sea result of making a series of crosses, and the English Itosecomb Minutes is the outcome of tuy efforts in this direction. Further Mr. Webster states that his idea was to produce," A goad sized, hardy fowl, which should be a non-sitter, a prolific winter layer, •nil possessing a rare conalif•My rather lengthy description faith the aid of the ribose) is in no way intended to preydice any one against the single come Ninon,. Bat wit mud face facts even in the poultry world,and those of es who have bred the latter variety know well how the cold winter weather affects their combs. In conse pience •Iso that it aft ems the general cor.daion of the birds we must welcome any improve- inset in the breed. As far as I can see the Rolecumb Minorca is built almost exactly on the lines a the old breed. Therefore, nw can naturally i :pert just as many of those large, white shelled eggs no well known as the product of the good old Minorca. Might I say in passing that if the Minorca fanciers of the old variety had not practically :mined the old breed, in the craze for abnormal comb, no inducement would hove offered itself tor the production of a new varlet,. This error can hardly happen with the ratecomb Minorca. Endeavours will be made to breed speci- mens with a small neat comb. 'lbis again will ina,erisllv add to the comfort (and the usefulness also) of •the f del. Good luck then to the English lioeecomb Minorcal have now dealt with all the most popular new breeds of p ultry up-to-date. Shotrld Providence spare us until another season we may then have more of them to talk about and good-naturedly criticise. Now as to making a plea fur dome of the</p>
        <p>of poultry. I am glad that there are signs of a revival among these, and I am perfectly certain that our poultry breeders can profitably turn their attention thitherwaid. There are many of the old varieties which, if judiciously bred, would not only eve, but far supersede many of the newer varieties. Take for instance the good old</p>
        <p>IkORKIN,</p>
        <p>. . The very name of florklng spells rurality, and especially on with the rs.d. This breed, however, has hecome scarce. It is chiefly owing to fads and a er.7.e furs. mething new. Nevertheless. we shall not have</p>
