        <p>to wait long. ere we sec the Red ',wit ing again taking its rightful place iu the poultry world. The breed is an extremely useful fariner's fowl. Mr. E. B. Fitch Hogg (secretary of the Red Dorking Club) says : " 'lust the breed is easy to rear; will stand any soil : has not been bred to excessive sire the fish is milk white with plenty of it. and. moreover. Red Dorkings are ;pod layers Surely then this is one ,hi satiety embodying most u,eful utility ponds. Mty the memories of the late Mr. Harrison Weir gl1112111•II, and inspire the chardpions of the lied Dorkini. Then, agaiu, there in yet another variety of the Dorking family which has for many years bees dispised.. the White Dorking. guicLiy, boil think most they will come again. Space forbids met,it dm further reference, let it suffice, that the very ass is a justifiable qualification. Another grunt! .dit I ee4 which is not near no extensively Iced as it dote 'sea to lie in</p>
        <p>1111: 1111. %P.</p>
        <p>Volt will, I hope excuse a personal n;ne. it is ; quite "on tne hoards " that I shook SW, of ...mid variony. when making .• ••..r • inoin-de-phoor The Role .p is epe a., or a free range. The birds certainly • ..etter on 'itch than in a confined area, and when they can I. IPP accommodate! they give an excellent return in •nags. A very speci4l point in favour of ti, Redcap is their wonderful endurance as layers. It in quite commonly underutood amongst breeders cf Redcaps that the hens lay better than pullets. A friend of mine, who has been a breeder of this variety for 40 years, had a hen laying well wlien ten years old. I quite admit that such is very exceptional, but at the same time I know well that many of his stock are well over two and three years old. Then, again, the Redcap is a hand- smne fowl, quite inure so than many of the new varieties. It breedn very true no colonr and markings To ClOne who have a free and well-wooded range I know of no more useful breed for egg-production than the Redcap. I cannot conclude witnont saying a • word in favour of still another grand old breed,</p>
        <p>I cannot undetataud why we should lose the layers of our largest eggs. Some say it is becau.e they are delicate. This is wrong. for I have known them do well on a farm of clay land. Given a free range with tile avoidanre of inbreeding the Black Spanish are almost as hardy 86 any other of the Mediterranean varieties. I hope we shall never lose Md.* taust useful fowl.</p>
        <p>" 111- /I. ITEM/ It UL! lEA It It. I' .1,</p>
        <p>..... Purina the past week I have received the" Feathered World Year Book "for 1911. It is difficult indeed, to find words wherewith to express one's appreciation of this wonderful publication. Before 1 proceed to disaect its c 'Amite, let me say that the Feathered World Year Book " in an aboolute necessity to every poultry keeper. "A perfect amateur's guide.” because it is so intensely practical. The backyarder. the utilitarian and the fancier alike, me copiously catered for. The illuetrations arid coloured plates are exquisite, nearle every breed of paltry being faith- Mily depicted Iron-. life. It contains numy useful articles, written by some of the most able men in the fancy. It further contains two clear and sell typed calendars for 1911-12. The" Breedr rs• Table" itl calendar form is invaluable for the hatching risen. Th." Foathered World Feeding Mem.- (by Mr. Proud) are an invaluable guide and a great " worry saver." That same gentleman's " Ilelpful hints t•P Poultry keepers" are concieely comp Itsl and full of rommonsenee advice. The articles by Mrs. l'ettit, I Cobb and ('operator, are all well wriden •urt . .</p>
        <p>extremely useful. Furthermore this wonderful hick contains special articles on the different breeds and verieties of poultry, turkeys, ducks, geese and bantams. Not to speak of the second portion which is solely set apart for pigeon fanciers. I ehould liko to ss3, a great deal more about this Year Book But space forklift Let me strongly recommend you to purchase a copy, for it is, " Right bang up-to-date "! And honestly worth flee times the price. This wonderful " Year Book " with its 1i35 pages can be had poet (reef, Is. 4d., abroad Is 1;.1j The pub- lishers address is "The leathered World," 9, Anmdel-street, Strand, London.</p>
