        <p>•••.- • • - cid C RCN. The anhital vi.it of the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones took place last week end whets large congregation' asnembled ,at the Earlestown Weeleyao Church. On Mouday evening a lecture on Cromwell end Puritan Weal ' woe delivered. Mr. Jones attacked vigorously the con- stitution of the House of Lords nit was formed in the Cromwell time. In the early age. there were only 59 peen end these were men who had done signal service for the Empire. Later,in neuter to defeat thedeec is- ions of these me, henry the oth made the niveher into Istu and Peerages were hought end so hooded down from CC. to age. Asked if lie would like to have Cuomwell lank est.* the lecturer said - No, because Queen Victoria •nil the late King had done eventbiog that Cromwell would have suggeated and so far our present Moneecn was following in his step.. Referring to the libel on the King he said he wa« disgusted corral of • nine going tit the country making the direr...Nl remerts ehout loir King, and he was also sorry Wet punishment had only fallen upon one man, for hundteda had made a similar statement.</p>
        <p>TO KEEP UP THE STANDARD OF PUBLIC MORALITY.</p>
        <p>Spanking at Preston on Tuesday night the Bishop of Lowlon acid that centrra of unity and brotherhood nere vitally required in evert greet city to drag people sway from coarse and lower forms of 11311 N, ment. ftright, living churches wera nweded. There teas no cause for jealously among Christian worker.. Men with strong convictions should admir•aud respect the strong conviction. of other men. Th.. qtronir•al, Churchmen • an the one who got on best with NOPCOO. formists. The standard of public morality 'cunt gradually sink onlea• there was something in the midst of thy peopl• to keep them up They ciust sneep away all that was %ensue!, and eleven Lowly such robbith aa indecent pictures, ccutoacopreliriflg statues *3 sea norrelnwhicli tired the Imegiriatica unworthily.</p>
        <p>?</p>
        <p>A HAPPY MEETING,</p>
        <p>r iui',</p>
        <p>Anti-Sonialials As to u hnt Socialism is. kir. Belton r raid " Snoiolkr, in the nationnl ination of land and capital, and nothing else in Socialism." Well, then, if nothing else It. Socinlism, Soeislicro cannot beatheism —(laughter end eppltotae)—it cannot be free love— (laoghter)—and it cannot have for its object the breaking up of family life. ( Loud laughter.) No Socialist isae no impatient, Co sanguine, no unwire or so foolish as to bolieve that it was pottaihle to DittiOMOIPP land and capital in one comprehensive Act of Parliament. They only notched to do it step by step, reform by reform. But not one of the reforms mentioned in the King's Speech could be celled Social iam,hecaose each reform was not the nationalisa• lion of land and capitol. " It in something like this," said Mr. Snowden "To get back to London from War- rington I go through (rewe, Stafford and Rugby. London in my deetinat ion ; London is my goal. But though Crewe, Stafford end fleshy are not London I never woold get there mikes I panned throogh those places. (Appinuse). And tht ugh those pieces are not London, they are neeeenary stone.. the road to London. Therefore, abreast-, tense? Name ands of reforms which we could not describe on Socialism, because they are not the goal. But they ere</p>
        <p>I STAOPS ON Tilt WAY TO THE GOAL OP SOCIALISM, end, therefore, they ire etotirled to he Ateecribed m Socialist stages towards Socielism. (hear, hear.) lie did not impute went of heart or sonpathy to their politicel opponents. Ile khew Om many of them , deplored es deeply no they -ffdi the poverty and the hardship which nbounded Ha the land. Ile knew they were equally anxious thet anything which human effort could do to meet ie rete the problem of the poor :should be done. Sympathy with the poor was not the monopoly of any intellectuel school : hut if they were going to he intelligent metal reformom they needed enmething more than sympethy or election. 'I he affection mei sympathy floret he the motive power, but intellect and bite:lnger:co must. 1,0 the wilding heed. There they hell tiro distinztion Iwtween the Socialist end the s)rs mast it: Itedical or Tory. , The latter did not know end did not , admit the cau:e of the existence of poverty, they ' did not understand i hat the private ownership of land, production and capital wax responsible for the exist- ence and the problem of poverty (Applause.) Now I the Socialists did understand that, and hy that know- lodge they wero stile to test every proposal that was brought foreard by that infallible test. Was it help- ing them on in the direction they wanted to go ; wee it 1 Crewe or tote it Preston —lleughter)—would it take them forward or backs ard ? Ile could only recall nt the moment two meareoes palmed during the hat 20 years which had really come directly horns to the people, and those were the Workmen's Compensation Act and the Old Age Pensions Act. (Applaueed How- ever, those reforms bad not a sound ecooomio heals; they simply took money out of one pocket of the worleng people in order to tranefar, not ell of it,but a pert into the other pocket of the workmen. If the taxation to pay for old-age pensions were Mitred by taxing the people es the Tories propound under Tariff Reform—(laughter)—or in other