        <p>ear.rnaiked fitr old ace pensions of a pound a week and I it could at be used for .y other purpone,he was quite sure that everybody would be in favour of pensions of a pound a week. (Laughter.) Now if old age peneions had been raised by tilting the food and other necessary erticles that would not have been social reform, for though an odd person or two might have been benefited they would have been benefited by increes- in. the poverty of the working people.. (Applause.) If social reform was not going to add to the wealth of the masses of the people than it aria nct social reform at all. (Applause.) If social reform was not going to transfer from those who had too much to those who had too little then menu bastard nocial reform and was not wnrth the effort of carrying it into law. (Applati..) The cost of the schemes for the instiranee of the infirm should he met by the taxation of the aurplus laconic. of the bloated rich. Ile wader- stood that there was a proposal on foot that the eroplo) erg of !akar had to contribute towards the ins...ee of the workmen. lie lid not think it was right to tax an employer of labour because he wan an employer, especially non matter of ordinary nicker,sa for which he was in no sense responsible. The coat should be met by the tazittion of man's wealth or inccone and whether he was an</p>
        <p>employer era man Heim(' in idleness upon the'employ- ment of othem be ought to be forced to contribute to diecharging the responsibility of the whole community. (Applause.) In conclusion Mr. Snowden Fetid that the Labour party would net as a spur to the Liberal party. In the butt lye years they did much ; hot in the next 'five ,etwe they were going to do far more. They had begnn te practic-11 solution of problems which 5 years , ago were regarded an folly and nnrealietic. They were on the eve of great changes and he believed that young people were going to see established a state of things where there would he no undeserved poverty, where no child would be deprived of opportunity through the want of its parenta to develop Its Clod-endowed gifts. (Loud applause). In a lucid addrees on the principlen of Socialiem Mr. R. C. Wellhead, of Manchester, maid that Socialism stood for the affirmation of the right of every individual to control private property. Sock Hem did not corny to wreck, it clime to mum% not to destroy but to build up, not to crush but to take off the shackles of those who had been crushed too long. (Applause).</p>
