      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>BRITISH COLUMBIA FISHERIES.</head>
        <p>BRITISH COLUMBIA FISHERIES.</p>
        <p>Sir George Doughty considern that the self-renewing of the harvest of the sea on the Pacific coast is capable of development to n point when it alitiold equal annually the value of the whole of the cereal crop of Western Canada. "There is an abundance of halibut cod, salmon, and, during the season, vast quantities of herring. The halibut fishery is the only one of the white fish school that is being worked at present. The herring industry cannot he said to hove been touched, and there le no lioubt that otlier classes of white flab, such sa plaice, haddock, turbot, grill sod sole are being found in great abundance."</p>
        <p>74:1 p. r , '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S CRIMINAL CALENDAR.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S CRIMINAL CALENDAR.</p>
        <p>CHIEF CONSTABLES ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr Martin Nicholls (chief constable of Warrington) hae issued his fourth annual report to the Watch Committee, We append the following extracts :--</p>
        <p>110WREA 11F IT] ME.</p>
        <p>......... The number of crimes (reported during the year was 228, a decrease of 33 as compared with the figures of the previous year. For these offences 176 persons were proceeded against, a decrease of 18 on the figures for 1909.</p>
        <p>PHOPENTY WFOLEN%</p>
        <p>The value of the property reported ntolen, or froadulently obtainer!, and the amount of the some recovered in an follows : —Stolen, 3:557 ss. lid. ; recovered, £3lli 9s. Bd. The figures for the previous year were : Stolen, £.1,087 Os. ; recovered, £1,129 3s. 10d.</p>
        <p>JUVENILE OFFENDERS.</p>
        <p>Twenty-two juvenile offenders (II; years of age and under) charged with theft, were apprehended during the year. These figures show a decrease of nine as compared with the figures of 1:)09.</p>
        <p>The number of persons proceeded against for nun- indictable offences, tried summarily by the magis- trates, was 1.072 of which number 987 were appre- hended and 04 summoned. As cormnrect with the figures of the previous year there is an increase of 70 in the number of persons arrested, and a decrease of eleven in the number of persons summoned.</p>
        <p>Fire hundred and eighty-fuur persons were prp- ceeded against for drunkenness during the year, thin being an increase of 99 as compared with the figures for the previous twelve months.</p>
        <p>SKI)) (EM OF THE P 01.1114</p>
        <p>. . . . In addition io their ordinary police duties the mem- bers of the Force were called upon to perform many services, amongst which might be mentioned the following, viz. :—They restored to their parents 145 children who were found wsndering in the streets, and conveyed to the pinfold 01 horses, cattle, sheep, (tc., found straying on the highways. Two hundred and two dogs were seized, of which number 106 were destroyed, 36 sold, and 60 restored to their owners,l &amp;c. In 1000, 190 dogs were seized. Ninety-eight collisions and accidents caused by runaway horses, etc., during the year were reported to the police, and assistance rendered on each occasion. Seven hundred and fourteen persons (580 males and 131 females) were conveyed to his Majesty's Prison, either at Liverpool or Manchester, the expenses incurred being recovered from the Prison Commissioners itrieg the year 330 articles were found by private persons and the police and deposited at the Police Office. Of this number 170 have been restoredto their respective owners, 79 returad to finders (owners not being traced), 13 destroyed (being worthless), and the remaining 68 are still unclaimed. INQUEST.</p>
        <p>The Coroner held 62 inquests, on increase of 23 an compared with the figures for 1902. The police aloe reported 47 cams of sudden death, in connection with which it was not found necessary for any public inquiry to he held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FTREN,TH OF THE FORCE.</head>
        <p>FTREN,TH OF THE FORCE.</p>
        <p>The authorized strength of the Pollee Force is 83 of all ranks. Their average length of service is nine yearn ten months. Their average age is 33 years six months. Their average height is 5 feet WI inches. The variations during the year were six. Two sergeants and one constable retired on pension, two constables transferred to another force, and one constable resigned.</p>
        <p>P./LICE PENSIONERM.</p>
        <p>The number of moo on the Pension Fund is seven - teen, viz —Two who were chief inspectors, two who were inspectors, and seven who were sergeants and seven who were constables. During the year the receipta for the Police Pension Fond amounted to £1,318 15s. lid. and the payments 1:1,062 Ns. Sit.</p>
        <p>COMPLIMENT TO VIE MEN.</p>
        <p>The Chief cenc-lifife;;.:-li .I;ii-ve-ie—report that the conduct of the inembere of the Force hue been eatisfactory."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WIDNES LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The Widnes Licensing Sessions were held on Toesday.—Superintendent Strickland, In his annual report, mad that there were ninety licensed houses in the di•inion. There had been two licensees proceeded against for breach°, ef the Licensing Act. The pollee had no objection to any renewal of licence except that of the Co.operritive Hall. In his opinion they had transgressed the bounds of the licence granted to them last year, which was only a music and dancing licence. Ile had ample evidence that the hall had been run on variety and manic hall lines. , The number of persons proceeded against for drunken- ness and other offences against the Licensing Act was 361 an against 122 cxi year, .%Iterations were sanctioned for the Royal Oak, the Golden Bowl, and the Rose and Crown. Mr. Madden, on behalf of the proprietor; of , the Co-operative Hall, argued that the management I had not transgressed the licence of last year. Why they should be Monied out he did not know.—The hear- ing in thin particular case wan adjourned until March 7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>PHILIP SNOWDEN M.P. AT PARk HALL THE MEANING OF SOCIALISM</head>
        <p>PHILIP SNOWDEN M.P. AT PARk HALL THE MEANING OF SOCIALISM</p>
        <p>Though the large demonstrations held under the auspices of the Wart Mgt., hranch of the Independent Labour Party are infrequent there is amass a sneaker of a national character who attends, and on Monday evening Mr. Philip Snowden was the chief speaker at the large meeting hell in the Parr Hall. Mr. Edward Horrocka presided and amongst those on the platform were Messrs. C. Dukes, E. Randalls, W. Lowe, .1. Lowe, Armstrong. W. Rolston, W. Johnson, E. Littlewood and J. Horton. Whilst the audience was assembling the choir of the local branch contributed several well rendered items. As 'meal the flouting commenced with a Socialist hymn,that on Monday being " Onward friendn of freednin." THE CHAIRMAN AND _THE TOWN CLERK'S,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SALAItV</head>
        <p>SALAItV</p>
        <p>The Con MOAN rema;Wriii;a:t. nett rule workingmen wanted to know why the Independent Labour Forty did not have more meetings of that cherecter. They could not run their meetinge absolutoly free like the other two great political organizations in the town, for the aim+, renaon that they had not wet any hig purse behind them. Each :non in their organhation woe e workingman and their propaganda had to he carried on by the contributions on working- men. They ware not in the position that night of having a local public repromentettve upon their platform. They had, however, many member/6,1.0t they never made more merit' ere then when they had notice worker, on the Town Council, such Inn Fred Stott— (applatme)—who were to tivin g for lair wages in the Corporation. They ehould have more of his type on the Warrington Town Council today. There was ahortly to come up Et the Town Council e large increase of Wray elan official of the Corporation and he (the ereakerl felt bound to admit that the men who opposed Councillor Stott would be in favour of bending over to one of the officials, not two or three shillings n week, but pounds. What they had got to do was to leave Liberal and Tory eeverely alone and send their own men to represent them. het the wealthy be represented by the wealthy, the middle close by the middle class, end the workers by the worker.. He hoped one result of that meeting would be that Warrington would soon be represented by a worker. (Applause). ME. SNOWDEN'S _SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samtvoitfi, in the 0011f10 of an eloquent address said that that meeting was a remarkable testimony to the widespread public interest in those questiona in which the Labour end Socialint Party were identified. As the Chairman had been cruel enough to remind him, he had been aseociated with the labour movement for a good many yearn and he confmaed to them that in the days when ho emote in the open air from a soap box at Bridge Foot he little anticipated that in so ahort time their party would have the powerfoil influence it now had. It wan quilts trite, the apeaker con titnied,that in recent yearn there had been a revolution of political thought in Great Britain. That change of vicar wan allowing itself not only in thoae who admitted their identity with the movement, but they found a charge,if not in thoughtat lean in speech. In both Liberal and Tory Partici,. Liberaliam was not the Liberaliam of ten or fifieen yearn ago ; the Tory programme wan not the Tory programme of ten or fifteen years ago ; and he claimed that area largely becaune of the educational work done by the labour and Socialist parties. (Applause.) He found in</p>
        <p>111 KING'S SPEECH</p>
        <p>to a rather meagre programme of legislation for the amnion, but apart from the constitutional question all the other measures dealt with the social audition of the people—them, heart—comedians that bad long been agitated for by their party. 'I be reference in the King's speech to an amendment of the Old Age Pensions Act was somewhat obscure and unnecessary. He knew of no reason for nay alteration, for the pensions of those who had received poor-law relief were juntas secure as the rest of the pensions. Though he did not know what the reference to old-age pensions was, his suspicion sonu thllt it was intended this year by legislation to carry out the idea of the Chancellor of the Exchequer calling upon Boards of Guardians and the local rates to pay to the Imperial Exchequer some part of the cost of giving old-age pensions to those who previously had been in receipt of poor-law relief. Ile could promise the Chancellor of the Exchmpter that II that he his intention a measure of that character would not pass through the House of Commons ova non. contentious and uncontroversial measure. (Applause.) There wm a great principle involved in an apparently Insignificant and trivial matter of that dem ription— (bear, heart—namely, the principle of the incidence of the cost of carrying out social reform. The real</p>
        <p>.„ TF:ST OF A MEASURE OF SOCIAL REFORM wan the finoncial basil of that measure—the source from widish the money was obtained for its cnrrying oat. The other two measures of Kleist reform men- tioned in the King's speech referred to the long promised schemes of insurance against sickness and Infirmity and unemployment. Ile welcomed the latter because it was. method of dealing with the unemploy- ment problem which he had long advocated. However, that would not solve the problem, it could not be nolved by any Act of Parliament or by work in any one session, it wee too big, and too eomplimited. It wee desirable that they should no reorganise work and iuduntry that every man would be provided with useful, honoursble and remunerative work. But they could not undo a system, which wee the growth (of ages, by one act of refornia- tion and they could not do it at once ; fer it was beyond the power of nny willing Government to immediately re-organise state indostries or municipal enterprises as to be able to find useful work for every man who wan thrown out of work by the capitalistio nyetem. ltrrt soMETIIING MOST BE DONIS AT ONCE to make provision so that no willing worker and those dependent upon him should starve through the lack of work. (Applause.) The Liberal party had admitted the State's responnibility for the existence of the unem- ployed end the Conservative party, if not so definitely, had done no at least by implication. The Tory party was committed to acing the state and the machinery and power of the stste for dealing with the unemployed problem. Eight or ten year. ago Mr. Chamberlain launched hia proposals sea means of binding the empire togethet,but now the appeal of Teri fT Reform wits made on the ground that it would deal with those who were out of work. The first time became to Bridge Foot he ventured to say that nine-tenths of those in that meeting would not have admitted that the State had any responsibility. There could he no better j.tiflentor, of the need of the I.L.P. than the text of the King's speech. Ito did not say that the teforms proposed were Socialism, they were not Socialism, but they were stepping stones in the direction of SociaPam. "Mae I appeal to Mr. Balfour once more," Mr. Snowden continued. " About two yeara ago when the Anti• Socialist crusade was waging most •igorously—(laughter)--and •illainonsly (laughter) —Mr. Balfour, as hero often does, adminis- tered a rebuke to the unscrupulots ones of those who see supposed to follow hi rn. In opening a Tory labour Club—(loud laughter)—which had a very brief and toglorious career —ilaughter)—nod a few ',mfl later landed in the Bankruptcy Court, Mr. Balfour took advantage of the occasion to give his ideae as to what Socialism was. MR. BALFOUR'S DEFINITION OF SOCIALISM 141 in odootire gnawer to most of the charges of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>L FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>L FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P.</p>
        <p>_ . THE LABOUR PARTY CONFERENCE. SHOULD WOMEN BE MAGISTRATES?</p>
        <p>The annual Conference of the Labour Party which was held in Leicester last week, we a very successful affair. No previous Con- ference of the Party has been so marked by a I spirit of quiet self-confidence, by a general unanimity, by a generous toleration, and by a practical and statesmanlike view of the questions which came before it for discus- sion. The results of the recent General Election had given the party good reason for self-satisfaction. The Parliamentary Labour Party is the only political group which goes back to Parliament increased in numbers. There is a hopeful feeling generally through- out the movement. The attack on the party through the law courts has had a stimulat- ing effect and has drawn the trade unionists into closer touch and sympathy with each other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>TWO SORTS OF CRITICISM.</head>
        <p>TWO SORTS OF CRITICISM.</p>
        <p>The Conference has clearly shown how little the carping criti( s of the Labour Party represent the general feeling of the move- ment. At no previous Conference, since the Parliamentary party was returned, has there been so little criticism of the actions of the Labour members. This is very satisfactory from every point of view. It shows that the rank and file understand the difficulties which have to be met by a new Parlia- mentary group which is still a small minority of the House of Commons. The movement realises and appreciates the work which the party has done in its apprentice days, and is tolerant of its shortcomings and mistakes. There was not an absence of criticism, and it would be a pity if the Conference had not freely spoken on matters on which opinions differ. But between honest criticism, reasonably expressed, and snarling condem- nation, spitefully uttered, there is a world of difference, and it was the almost entire absence of the latter in last week's Confer- ence which was so pleasing, so creditable to the movement, and so disappointing to the enemy. The delegates showed in a remark- able way that it is possible to differ strongly in opinions, but at the same time express the differences in a moderate way and in a friendly spirit. DEFYING THE LORDS AND THE LAW. The Conference was the largest which has yet been held. It had been feared that the effect of the Osborne judgment would be to prevent many unions from sending delegates to the Conference. Of course all the trade union delegations were there in open viola- tion of the law. The Conference itself, as a matter of fact, was an unlawful gathering. But the temper of the Trade Unions just now is such that it would be an unwise thing for the powers conferred by judges' decision to be exercised too freely. In spite of the House of Lords decision which says that trade unions must not take part in politics and which says that not a penny of trade union money must go to a Labour Party, there were over 400 delegates at this Labour Conference, who were sent there by trade unions and their expenses paid out of trade union funds. Although injunction have been given by the courts restraining a good many unions from contributing to the Labour Party, the fall in the membership of the party at the end of the year only showed a decline of 20,000. This defiance shows a spirit which will carry the agitation for a change of the law to a successful end. _ A SIGNIFICANT VOTE.</p>
        <p>The most remarkable thing about the Con- ference was the way in which it accepted the recommendations of the Executive on the question of altering the Constitution of the party. Not that the alterations proposed made much, if any, difference at all to the conditions which candidates must accept. I don't think the changes have lessened one iota the strength of the pledge which a Labour candidate must take. The signing of the pledge is to be abandoned; but that could have been done without a verbal change in the constitution, for there was nothing at all in the old constitution requiring candidates to give a written undertaking to maintain the constitution of the party. The only real alteration which has been made is the removal of the words which required a member of the Parliamentary party to abide by the decisions of the group, and in their 'place words have been inserted that he must conform to established Parliamentary prac- tice—whatever that may mean. But the sigtfificance of the vote at the Conference on these changes is not because of any value in the alterations themselves, but because the vote shows that the delegates now look Ippon the Labour Party as being so firmly established that it can afford to dispense wilt support which was necessary when it was learning to walk. The vote showed how Rae the movement believes in this talk which is being indulged in by certain critics about the Labour Party having lost its independenc&amp; and become a mere appendage of the Liberil Party. If the delegates had Hieved that, they would not have supported, by an almoAt unanimous vote, changes in the constitutiop which will generally be interpreted as awe ening of the control which the party had ov its candi- dates and members. This ovekrwhelming vote is the greatest testimony Iwhich the delegates could give to show their tilts* . the Farliamentary party and thr that the independence of the tr ov, inviolate.</p>
        <p>ir belief -tent is</p>
        <p>_ I wrote at length a week ot the proposal which was to cc Conference for imposing upo mentary party the policy of yi occasion on the merits of a one' less of .consequences." I then</p>
        <p>I' MO 1, ome bef, on the mting on %Vim" r expretu the il; M</p>
        <p>.ck on me the very</p>
        <p>belief that the' commonsense ol</p>
        <p>would reject such a proposal thought that the resolution paltry .amount -of support</p>
        <p>iC:CO;ded—iO"-</p>
        <p>In this ril'atti•</p>
        <p>it was shown that it is pos---ie! I</p>
        <p>, minority to make a great ado thing, and to create the impres represent a large volume of op a matter of fact they have all the fighting line. It is only fa</p>
        <p>the refusal of the Conferenjc</p>
        <p>resolution was probably not to hostility to such a policy</p>
        <p>unwillingness .to fetter he p</p>
        <p>in their Parliamentary woi may arise when the Labour</p>
        <p>vote on the merits of .a climatic</p>
        <p>of the consequences" to the • • - - -</p>
        <p>but there is a world of diffe</p>
        <p>being free to decide to do tl question and being compelled to do it on a paltry issue.</p>
        <p>THE WOMEN'S CONFI The ordinary Conference o Party was preceded by two</p>
        <p>ino -of a cognate character—oi</p>
        <p>ICOlfimenceOithe Women's L</p>
        <p>and the other a Special</p>
        <p>the questions of armaments. Labour League is an organi- to the Labour Party, and it 3ervice to the party at many a kt its Conference last week it lumber of questions specially men, and passed resolutions It considered a matter which, I 3W at a Women's Conference; have been trying to interest ganisations in for some years ithout much success. The ques- °presentation of women on tho _ .</p>
        <p>When the Royal Commis sion ie Appointment of Justices was sitting I repeatedly pressed the women to put a claim forward for the appointment of women justices, but they seemed to be too absorbed in the agitation for the vote to do so. I think there is no scandal more grave, no injustice to women more serious than the fact that the administration of justice is wholly in the hands of men. How would men like to be tried by' a bench of women magistrates, by a woman judge, and by a jury of women? That ,is unthinkable, and the present system would equally be so if we had not become accustomed to it. Women ought to sit upon the bench. For a jury of , men, or a bench of male magistrates, to try and to condemn a woman, is an outrage upon the most elementary principles of justice. A great many of the cases which come before the courts ought to be left entirely to a female judiciary. I am glad the Women's Labour League has called attention to this matter, and I hope they will not let it rest. Under the Acts entitling women to be Chair- men of District Councils and Mayors, they are not allowed to be magistrates, as men are when occupying these positions, and that prohibition is adding insult to injury. The Special Conference on Armaments should have an effect on the rumoured intention of the Government to propose another large addition to the Navy Vote.</p>
