      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>IMPORTS OF ,MANUFACTURED GOODS FOR HOME USE. 1901-1910.</head>
        <p>IMPORTS OF ,MANUFACTURED GOODS FOR HOME USE. 1901-1910.</p>
        <p>LARGE INCREASE OF RAW MATERIALS IMPORTED Decrease of Fully Manufactured Goods.</p>
        <p>(BY C. N. PARKER-NO. 4),</p>
        <p>For the purpose of ascertaining the growth of our imports of manufactured goods ready for ultimate use or consumption, I have as below extracted from the Board of Trade Returns the figures for 1901 and 1910. To ascertain the balance for home consumption the re-exports from both years are deducted. It should be recognised that the term manu- factured goods ready for consumption is not strictly applicable. It is impossible to tell in many of the items what proportion is subject to further manufacture, and it is also difficult to determine to what class several of the items properly belong. For example in the case of silk manufactures which form nearly a third of our full manufactured imports, a large proportion of those imports will be in the form of broadstuffs which must necessarily go to our factories and workshops to be made up into articles of clothing. In a similar way machinery imports are mainly for the use of the British producer, and not for the consumer. Still on the whole I claim that the accompanying list contaffis the items in our imports which can be most appropriately designated as above. And inasmuch as com- parisons are made of the same groups for the two periods the totals show fairly accurately the progress we are making in supplying our increasing requirements in manufactured articles ready for consumption.</p>
        <p>tosr IMPORTS OF MANI. PiITURED GOODS.</p>
        <p>--1-9017 1010. .. 11789306 11168188</p>
        <p>Silk manufactures</p>
        <p>Cutlery,fiardware sod &amp;Ismail° Instruments ... . .......... 1833038 2954772 Watot and Cloaks and parts— 1060905 972646 Macklsery 4669059 5105382 3533449 1312572 875057 031541</p>
        <p>Cotton m.anufactures . Woollen manufactures</p>
        <p>4661059 5105182 3533849 1312572</p>
        <p>BoateandSboea...,</p>
        <p>flats and Bonnets Printed paper Fancy goods</p>
        <p>213656 437610 921911 466326 1235133 857223</p>
        <p>Soap. Toy! and (amps</p>
        <p>98037</p>
        <p>122725 1U1602</p>
        <p>Garinges, Cycles, Motor Cars, Motor Cycles 695557 11565'38 Glass and (floss Were 346382 2e65561 Basket and Basket Wares Leather Manufactures: Gloves and Belting Musical Instroineista Oilcloth</p>
        <p>2061264 11.139397 152821 831901 73482 11765</p>
        <p>09915791 £36721119 Docreme £2891372 EXPORTS OF SAME.</p>
        <p>We now turn to the exports of our manu- factures included in the same groups as the imports in question which have between 1901 and 1910 decreased 3 millions. We find these exports which at 1901 stood at 46 millions, in 1910 have increased to 75 millions. There is not a single trade making these products , (with the single exception of watch and clock makers) that has not largely increased its exports. These figures do not include all our manufactured goods by a long way, the comparison is, as stated, only with the groups the same as those in which we have competition by foreign imports of wholly manufactured products. There are those who are disposed to support Tariff Reform because they are genuinely concerned about the amount of tfactured articles we import into this ,ry. Many have a vague idea that our rts of this class of goods are rapidly ming. They are told that we import one hundred and fifty-seven millions in value. They overlook the fact that of this amount we re-export twenty-seven millions and that ninety-two millions although manufactured up to a certain point are not ready for con- sumption but form the raw material for further manufacture. It will be seen, there- fore,that the result of the trade of the decade when expressed in figures shows us that our imports which are decreasing at the wholly manutactured end, are increasing and this and more rapidly as we approach the ntary raw material. NET IMPORTS. 1901 1910 Per Mill. Mill. Dec. cont. manufacted Goods... 38 36 3 7.7 Increase.</p>
        <p>count] impor increa</p>
        <p>Manufactured Goods 70 92 22 31.4 !Morin" e above results puzzle the Tariff rmer. He sees the countries with h we trade attempting by tariffs to ide manufactures and to increase their rts of raw material, and at the same to export a greater proportion of highly rfactured goods. The results work out, lmost every case, the reverse of what rariff Reformer expects. This country h admits manufactures, and everything Free, is reducing relatively her imports inufactures ready for consumption more Ily than protected countries, while at ame time she is incrksing her exports ghly ni..rnufactured goods faster than countries that handicap their exports protective policy,</p>
