      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>British llomen's Temperance Association.</head>
        <p>British llomen's Temperance Association.</p>
        <p>The members of the above association were very busy last week and their efforts are being rewarded by numbers of adherents joining their cause. Several schools have won a picture and a good number of workers have qualified for a " Star of Honour." On Sunday afternoon last week a meeting was held at the Women's Circle, Mrs. Simpson presiding. Mrs. llartill gave a very interesting address on " God's light,His word being opposed to darkness, and sin." Mrs. Clatworthy and Mrs. Noble each took agollart,att.inwiantebee parnd edninegws. There memberswaster s ae enrolled. On Wednesday afternoon at three o'clock the usual monthly meeting was held in Bold-street. Mrs. Simpson presided in the absence of the president, Mr.. Garnett. A hymn was sang and Mrs. Gunnell led in prayer. Miss H. ugh sang, " Saved by grace," accompanied by Miss l'ayne. Mrs. Owen related with interest her visit to a Temperance Rally at Wigan and also the C.E.T.S. meeting at Liverpool, after which she gave a reading entitled " Jim's wife." Mrs. Handl told of her visit to a social at Ashton, and that that branch is getting up a big meeting for the sth April, when the Rev. R. J. Patterson, author of the " Catch my pal" movement,will givers address. It was suggested that a party go from Warrington. Newspaper cuttings relating to drink which any of the members think of interest will be read at the March meeting.—At eight o'clock in the even- ing the same workers held a meeting at the Adult School, Academy-street. Mrs. Simpson explained the object of their visit. Mrs. Owen gave an address and one of her splendid readings. Miss Hough sang 'ls there anyone can help us" accompanied by Mr. Johnson Barrett. Mrs. Hartill based her remarks on the motto of the Association,'" For God and home and every land," her words being very much appreciated. Mrs. Palmer recited "The Land of Providence." Reheshmenta were handed round. Mrs. Sherburn moved a vote of thanks to the British Women for coming amongst them. 'They had had a very happy time and she hoped it would not be long before they came again. Mrs. Bradley—secretary of the school for the women— said ahe was pleased to second the vote of thanks. She hoped the good advice given would be carried out. She had long been a British Woman and those who joineil the movement were an example to others. Miss Sherburn said the meeting was about one of the best they had had.</p>
