      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>BAYURDAY, FEBRUARY 11. 1911.</p>
        <p>Lord ROBERTS is</p>
        <p>THE CON- unwearying in his SCRIPTIONISTS. advocacy of compul- sory military training, and he treats all arguments on the other side with easy disdain. They are mere theories and fallacies. Sir lAN HAMILTON'S recent book on Lord ROBERTS'S special topic has been regarded by good judges as a powerful defence of a voluntary system, Lord ROBERTS, as he told his audience in London this week, finds it difficult to understand how an officer of Sir lAN HAMILTON'S experience could allow himself to advocate a system of voluntary training. But here is just the crux of the whole matter. There may be large experience on both sides. There may be equal honesty, and the same sincerity and patriotic feeling. And yet the views expressed may be diametrically opposed. Nobody doubts the sincerity of Lord ROBERTS; but that of Sir lan HAMILTON is equally unimpeachable, and we should be disposed to put the judgment of one as high as that of the other. But great questions affecting the national defence are not to be settled by any one's ipse dixit. The Army must necessarily be managed, not from the public platform and by National Service Leagues, but by those who are for the time being responsible to the nation for I its adequacy, its readiness, and its efficiency.</p>
        <p>The DEAN OF NORWICH moved, at the meeting which LORD ROBERTS addressed, that universal military training for home defence is necessary for the safety and welfare of Great Britain. If that proposition could be proved, there would be an end of the matter. However powerful might be the. objection to universal military training, it would have to give way before the necessi- ties of the case, if they were such as the DEAN OF NORFOLK declares them to be. But we suggest to the learned and venerable cleric that his proposition is the very matter that is in dispute, and that the greater weight of evidence, and the pre- ponderance of expert opinion, is on the other side. Lord ROBERTS makes light of the voluntary system generally. The Territorial force, he would have us believe as naught. The old Volunteer system was just a piece of vain display. These are opinions that Lord ROBERTS is entitled to hold, of course; but we doubt whether he will find them widely supported. If they did find wide support we should, as a matter of course, not be far from universal military training, or probably from conscription in the crude form. But Lord ROBERTS and his friends have the bulk of</p>
        <p>the nation against them at present, and there is no practical use in sneering at Mr. HALDANE and Sir lAN HAMILTON when it is the people who require to be converted. Whether this country can be invaded or not is a question that can be settled only in one way, which is by actual experiment. Lord ROBERTS stoutly maintains that invasion is possible, and by a force larger than 70,000, carried in fewer ships than the 150 that are declared by Mr. HALDANE to be necessary for the carrying of the smaller force. Admitting this for the sake of argu- ment to be true, and assuming that our vast expenditure on the Navy is so much money thrown away on fleets that would be lost in a fog when an enemy's fleet with an army on board was sailing across the North Sea, on what grounds does Lord Roberts presume that a Territorial Army of 300,000, backed by the regular Army, would be unable to deal with a foreign and newly-landed army of one-third or one-fourth of that number? Formerly we laid ourselves open to the reproach of vainglory by boasting that one Englishman was as good as three men of any nation. We are now invited to regard matters from the opposite point of view. Is one foreign soldier as good as three Englishmen? Well, frankly, we don't believe it, even when it is suggested to us by Lord Roberts, who in our view, might be more usefully and patrioti- cally engaged than in " crabbing" the Territorials.</p>
        <p>In the King's Speech it THE LORDS has been announced to AND THE both Houses of Parlia- PEOPLE'S WILL. ment that "proposals will be submitted to you without delay for settling the relations between the two Houses of Parliament, with the object of securing the most effective working of the Constitution." It has always been the contention of those who defend the House of Lords that the Lords bow to the decision of the people when that decision is clear and unambiguous. At two General Elections the people have decided that thei powers of the House of Lords must be. curbed. At the General Election the people unambiguously decided that there was only one solution of the problem which com- mended itself to them. If the Lords reject the Parliament Bill, as Mr. Balfour seems to suggest that they may. they will expose the hollowness of the defence which has repre- sented them as never obstructing the clearly expressed will of the people.</p>
        <p>- Probably the most MR. CROSFIELD'S heroic thing that Mr. HEROISM. Crosfield has ever done was to engage in public debate on Women's Suffrage with a noted exponent of the cause, to wit Miss Margaret Robertson, B.A. It needs considerable temerity for a man to wilfully enter into an argument with a woman, and especially so when he knows that she has choice of ground and a well-equipped armoury to begin with. As a rule man is content to make a few cursory remarks in a superior masculine tone and then leave the field, and the last word, to the enemy. Mr. Crosfield seemed to expect to be "laid out' by his opponent, and per- sonally we did fear that he would be some- what roughly handled. But Miss Robertson was both gracious and forbearing—and Mr. Crosfield proved anything but a weakling in the contest. He advanced some really strong arguments in a lucid way, and they were by no means demolished by his opponent, familiar as she must be with every method of assault on her position. She laid great emphasis on the need for women to have the vote in order to redress the terrible con- ditions under which so many of them have to work, and in reply we think Mr. Crosfield might have urged much more strongly the point that to enfranchise women on the same terms as men would still leave the sweated female workers, and working class women generally, without any voice or vote. The women to be enfranchised would be merely those with a property qualification—surely the worst jtistifi- cation for extending the franchise! It may be trite to say that the question is a very big one, but there remain difficulties about "drawing the line" in regard to feminine service in public offices which will continue, for a long time yet, to perplex the average man. Miss Robertson says that the granting of the suffrage to women is inevit- , able. We agree, but,contrary to the vote of the audience on Tuesday, we do not think the time is yet ripe</p>
        <p>A sympathetic curios- MR. SNOWDEN,M.P. ity was, no doubt, AT PARR HALL. behind the impulse which prompted many of those who attended the meeting addressed by Mr. Philip Snowden on Monday evening. Mr.Snowden is one of the most attractive personalities in the Socixlist-Labour group in Parliament. For ore thing he is always consistent in his advocacy of social reform, and for another thing he maintains a level- headed moderation which has prevented him from making the blunders by which some of his fellow-workers are unenviably distinguished. You do not hear it urged against Philip Snowden: " Oh, yes, he is the man who said this, or did that." He possesses the power of persuasion by logical deduction to an uncommon degree, and is one of the few men who can expound Socialism without arousing the resentment of opponents while yet giving them cause to think. There is a sincerity and a subordination of self about Philip Snowden which are impressive and com- pelling. In reading H. G. Well's new novel "The Modern Machiavelli," we came upon a particularly apt and generous allusion to the Member for Blackburn in the midst of criticisms not quite so favourable of even better known men. To the author, Mr. -Snowden is a type: "A seagreen incor- ruptible. . . . speaking on the Address; a slender twisted figure supporting itself on ' a stick and speaking with a fire that was , altogether revolutionary." The mental picture recalled by the quotation is exact in , every detail. In his address on Monday Mr. Snowden got down to the root of things in telling • fashion, and his summary of what social • reform should be based upon is worth special , note. Said he: . . .</p>
        <p>If social reform is not going to transfer from those who have too much to tho-e who have ton little, then it in a bastard social reform, and not wo,th the eff wt of csrrying.it into law." . .</p>
        <p>Here you have the Socialist view. concrete and unequivocal, and it must needs appeal with some force to the man with "too little."</p>
        <p>According to the Chief DRUNKENNESS IN Constable's report to W AftitisgroB. the Licensing Bench, Warrington stands unpleasantly high in the proportion of drunkards per 1,000 of population as com- pared with nine other great Northern towns. Our figures, based on the prosecutions for the year are 909 per 1,000, and only Middles- borough beats us for booziness with a percentage of 1368. Huddersfield is the lowest of the ten towns with 189, and both Wigan and St. Helens with 396 and 665 compare very favourably with our own town. Figures can be made to prove any- thing, and one hesitates to believe that there is so very much less drunkenness in neigh- louring boroughs than in Warrington. So much depends on the relative activity and determination of the police in each district. A man may perhaps have to be incapably drunk before he is prosecuted in Wigan, though we are not aware that slight intoxi- cation is enough to result in trouble from the police at Warrington. Taken on their face value the statistics make Warrington a very drunken place, and it would be very much more satisfactory if we were at the foot of the table instead of nearly at the top. There need be no question that there is ample scope for the energies of all the temperance societies in Warrington. Maybe an improvement would be effected right away if the magistrates treated seriously any and all offences against the licensing laws. Three licensees were proceeded against during the year on clear cases of either selling drink to a drunken person, or during Sunday, and the fines, on the average, would just about purchase a new hat!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Ancient Order of Forester s.</head>
        <p>Ancient Order of Forester s.</p>
        <p>A very successful meeting was held at the 'Ukiah Workman Refreshment Rooms, Florsemarket-otreet, there being a large attendance of members, Bro. Sergt..Major Boast, CR., presiding. The bneiners of the evening was the District report. owl the delegate* dealt with the pre geese of the Order, sad nleo its sound financial position. During the evening ten new members joined the Conrt, which was a good start for the year. Bro. Joseph Owen, P.D.C.R., Court 240, gave a short aildres.. He was perfectly sure th it success was as.ured and that the Court had taken the right step forward in bolding their meetings away from licensed premises. He congratulated the member. on the step they had taken in joining a Friendly Societe, awl trusted that the new members would attend their Court with regularity, becoming active workers in the truest sense of the word. At the present time he was prowl to say that the Officered this Court were a credit to the district. and hoped for • continued success of the Court. During the evening the district officer. Bro. :George Maybury, D CR.. and Bro. Robert Thomley, D.S., attended sod were heartily received. Bro. Maylinry, 1).C.R., congratulated the Court on its pro- greseduring the pest year, and the splendid work the members were doing to further the cams of : Forestry.</p>
        <p>• LINIIIELD COMPOWN to • with warm water is ma creel lest gargle for Sore Throat, Col, Cough', he.—Advt</p>
        <p>r -TB-A 00 LL 1i 44 ~ AT s . i-r---Ittta 11., t -s tiLtTIC ' 4 , _</p>
        <p>Notes by " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Once more Warrington have gathered in the full points from a home match. Hull usually prove fairly easy victims at the Warrington enclosure, notwithstanding the fact that they are a smart team anil frequently show more cleverness than their opponents. If I am not tnistaken. Hull have never won a match •t Warrington since the first year of the Northern Union. Their visi.• here are about as hopeleva as Hunslet's, or, say, Warringuin's at Oldham.</p>
        <p>On Saturday. HOll were cont.dered to have a good chance of breaking the record, for the Warrington team was 'ouch weakened by the absence of</p>
        <p>I,,kenson and Creevey. And the way they started, sesrm.l to bear out early prognostications. The Thiol Porters opened in brilliant style, and were soon three points up as the reknit of an injudicious Warrington pass, and a smart piece of opportunism by Francis the speedy right wing. Warrington seenled decidedly second-rate until Clarkson, the Hull custodian retired injured for a quarter of an hour. That brief period vettled the issue. Warrington buckled too with a will, and registered ten points in quick time. In the second half they had Hull beaten, though the only addition to the scorn was a dropped goal by Thomas. The victory by 12 punts to ii was a tribute to Warrington's determination rather than to the cleverness of the side. In the finer arts of the game—in combined back play—Hull were superior.</p>
        <p>Each side obtained a try through an intercepted pass. After Francis had run through from the Warring- ton defence—a speedier hack than Tilley would have stopped him—Waywell snapped up a lung pass from Cotterill, the Hull centre, nod ran right trim his own quarter to the Hull half before giving Brooks an easy journey under the posts. The other Warri•gt-n try was due to Frank Singers getting the ball near the line, and allowing nothing to stop him till he hail planted it over the rut. Thoma,'s three goals msrro very useful additions to the score, and gave Warrington the victory.</p>
        <p>Brooks was the best back on the home side.l and everyone was delighted to see him showing form reminiscent of the palmy days when he was the crack halt-hack of I...sneashire, and got Ms international cap against the Australian.. Since then be has been under a cloud, hut on current Nan he is as good as ever. Perhaps training has actor- thing to .lo with the happy result. Anyhow we wsut Brooks at his hest in the cup-ties. Waywell was smart, hut he seems to have developed a tendency to al av up and look for a colleague to pass to. This is bad form tort wing man. Pasivorth—the emergence man— was erratic, hut Daintith played better than usual.and made one tine save which prevented a certain score I. r Hull, and earned Wm a hearty round of applanes for pluck and judgment. The forwaids were decidedly better, arol the policy of changing them occasionally will clearly pay !</p>
        <p>On the Dull side there was touch clever passing and speedy running, huts notable want of finish, while the defence did not compare with that on the War- rington side. Wallace, Cotterill and Devereaux were all very clever, and against a less experienced team might have run up a big stove. Clarkson W. gOnd until his injury, but he (I'M@ not show the outstanding ability that he did when in the Leigh ranks.</p>
        <p>There were changes in the League table on Satur- day, due vi the defeat of Wakefield and Wigan Oldham and the Trini,y men ink like waging a close conte,t for the first position. Widnes are pretty sure to finish fourth and it was a fine and creditable per. fortnance on their part to heat Wigan pointless on Saturday. Youth and enthusiasm are making their mark in the chemical town. Over 1(0 spectator. went from Warrington to see the Widnes match on Satur- day, and over 1,000 visited the home of Manchester Cnited.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows : For. Agst.</p>
        <p>P. I,V. L. D. G. F. P. D. V. P. c age. Oldham .24 25 3 1...56 76 340.-32 34 166... 95.41 Wa00661dT33 19 3 I_4B 81 316-29 58 142.. 04.74 Wigan 420 4 0.82 105 439-19 $1 131... 83.33 Widnea ....23 11 6 3.-31 59 221...16 25 107_ 67.39 Honalet _25 15 10 0-52 65 299...43 70 296... 60.00 Holl 6.41..23 12 8 3...72 80 384...34 56 244... 50.69 Warringt'n23 11 7 5...38 47 217...27 49 194... 5,99 Dee ahory.23 13 10 0..23 59 233...30 43 196... 5652 Laecla 03 12 10 1.-45 57 267_32 50 214... 54.34 Balo, 04 13 11 0...34 61 251.-35 36 166_ 54.16 Halifax -.21 13 11. 0_36 51 231_31 41 195... 54.16 Keighley. 23. 11 10 1...30 42 196.-40 60 260... 52.27 Rochdale4424 12 11 1...41 rt 250 .29 411 529,l St.Helens 25 13 12 0.45 73315.30 66 274... 5200. Hulk 25 11 11 3..31 61 219-33 56 234... 50 00 Swinton 23 11 12 0...26 42 178...24 35 153... 47 92 , liudderso426 12 14 0.. 49 75 3.-35 58 46.13 Salford 23 10 12 1...39 03 255.. 38 60 236... 4663 Leigh 52 10 12 0...28 35 16'...35 48 211 4945 BradfordN24 9 14 1...25 34 153_44 55 253... 7959 Beg'too 44.23 9 14 0_27 34 159..40 55 245... 5993 Runcorn -21 7 14 3_20 42 166..40 65 275... 35.41 Ebbw Val, 21 8 16 o_l6 42 159...34 57 239... 33.33 Barrow —.23 7 16 0...24 49 195...44 76 316... 30.43 York ^I 6 15 0...31 37 173.-49 67 299... 29.57 NlertbyrT.lB 5 13 0-18 19 90...47 81 337... 37.77 CoTenvry -22 5 16 1.30 47 101_52 07 305... 25-01 Etamley 23 5 17 1.-50 23 115-.54 01 351... 2390</p>
        <p>The Warrington men are due at Broughton to-day, Ind with the Bangers at full strength there will doubtless be a keen struggle for point.. Warrington have not done well at W heater's field in reoent years, and one cannot be too hopeful of the result ti' ddy. But they should certainly enter upon the contest with an excellent chance of checking the Rangers, who are not much above moderate this season. Unfortunately neither Crone; nor Fish is quite fit for play, though both are expected to be available for the cup-Co nest week.</p>
        <p>To-day'n team is Tilley, fuu back ; Waywell. Taylor, Dickenson. and Unsworth. three-quarters : Brooks and Daintith. half hack. Thomas. Johnston, Shugars, Cheaters, Poison, and Skelhorne, forwards.</p>
        <p>The "A " team will be without their prorni,ing centre. Carter, who haft sustained a broken arm while at work. The aelected team to oppose Broughton •• A"ia an follows Mclntyre, full back Clare, Kenney, Jordan ,id Bradshaw, three-quarters . Lloyd *and Jenkins, half backs ; Naylor, A. Board ~,a;), P. Boardman, Worrell, Hesketh, and McCabe. forwards.</p>
        <p>The League rnstehes for to-day are : Broughton V. Warrington. Leigh v. Salford. Swinton V. Covestry. Oldham V. Runcorn. Widnes v. Rochdale.</p>
