        <head>Warrington beat Hull</head>
        <p>Warrington beat Hull</p>
        <p>As usual a cheat. crowd attended at Warrington for • the enrols! visit of the floll team, and there would t 3 folly ZOOO present. The visitors were strongly repro- seined, hut Warrington had to find sohatitutes for Dickenson and Creevey. Excellent weather agsin pre- vailed, and the ground ens in good order, though traces of frost made the surfer.ue a bit treacherous. The teams and officials were:—</p>
        <p>Warrington : Tilley, back ; (Ceps.ll, Taylor. Unnerorth and Clare, threegitarters ; Brookes sod Deintith, half-backs; Ski:gars, 1'01.013, Thomas, Johnston, Skelhorn and Chent&amp;ra, forwards.</p>
        <p>Hall Clarkson, book; Franc;s, !leveret's, Cntterill, and Rogers, [Wes-quarters ; Wallace end Hughes, half- back.; Bonier Holder, Connell, Galloway, Herridge and Taylor, forwards. Referee : Mr J W Whiteley, Wakefield. Once the ball hal been set in motion Hull opened rips lively attack. Clare wen weak on the home left wing and smart work by the Hull backs endanger,' the Werrin nine line. Brooks and Deintith relieved well, and the forwardv led bv Thomas and tbe</p>
        <p>Skeihorn carried pley into the Hull quarter. Clarkson was sae, end Poe passing by Francis, Hoverer,. and Cotterill looked distinctly dangerous until the big centre was grassed by Heinen!t. Hull got possession well. end another bout of penning was only ended when Way well forced Rogers iuto touch in the home half. The home tonna responded with a flee movement between the halvn and threeolosrtere. Utissvorth ran don-a to the left unit, hut timed his ' pest; to Clare badly, and it was intercepted by Francis. r. Hell wing got clear sway, and beating t Iley for pace he groended the ball at the c rrner after it run over half the length of the field. The goal kirk failed and Werriogton et once rushed the game to the other end. Johnston made a strong run, hut his pass to Wsywel was forward. Rogers nest intercepted a WArrington pen but knocked on in gathering—a lucky escape for the locals. Wallace again got his backs going, and judicious punt now the Hull men with the line at their mercy. Boylen .cured, and with three players alongside tried to dodge Tilley on the line, but wee promptly downed and missed a certvin try. Again the Warrington forwards by tine work in the loose drove Hull heck. Clarkson Wee hard pressed and in clearing sustained an injury to the knee which complied him to retire torn quarter-of-an.h our. Warrington made great efisrta after his and Way well snapped op a pas. from (-quern] in the home quarter. The wing man ran finely past the centre, end when challenged by the Hell custodian, he passed to Brooks who raced behind the posts in grand style, Thomas giving his Fide the lead with a goal. Harrington were all over the visitors at this stage, end a round of passing would have provided enother score, hut Taylor held the hall too long. Scrum- maging took place on the line, and Brooks getting bold gave the hall to Shutters, who forced himself over by sheer weight and strength. Again Thomsr goaled and the locals Were in ecstacies. The Hell men were •till aggreesive, however, and further fine work by Wallace. Devereaux and Francis penetrated the home defence. Francis ran and punted, and the for II appeared to be going dead when Devereaux raced tip and grounded amid a ceowd of stirprieed Warrington. men. '1 he goal- kick was egein wide,and the Interval arrived with War- rington lending by ten points to six. The Hell men were at full strength in the second half, but Clarkson could do little running. Play was even for son.ft lime, brit a run and punt by Francis sent the bail past Tilley. The Dell forwards seemed certain to go over in a body when Daintith clarified across and threw himself on the ball—a very fine save. Brooks changed the venue with a great run into the Hull half, where Warrington put in some strong play. Brooks dashed over from a pack, but lore the hall, and both Taylor and Chestere had hard luck in not scoring. Devereanx marlo another fin, run for the visitore but wae grassed in midfield, and near the close ThOrnall drOpped a floe penalty goal for Warrington. Final Warrington 3 goals, 2 tries (12 points) ; Hull 2 tries (6 points). WIGAN !KILLED A r WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, before G.OOO spectators. The Rest half was very feet and exciting, both sides playing the open game, Litt the tackling was keen. Luythatu and Tnornea had futile shots at goal from penalties. The lVidnea forwards, led by Johnson, executed some brilliant dribbling reovenients, end Sharrock Wrol often ; : hard pressed to clear. At half-time nothing had been scored. Early in the second half Wigan attacked. , Jones had his kick charged down, and Leytham getting possession made a dash for the line, but was pushed over the flag. Whims appeared to stay better than their opponent+, and towards the finish did moat of the attacking. holly put in a short cross kick, and O'Gara raced up and scored. Dainteth improved. Widnes retoraed to the attack, nods kick ana follow up in Swinton ended in that player scoring a seeenit try. Dainteth again improved. Result—Widnes 2 goals and 2tries (10 point.), Wigan nil. Teams Widget —Jones, hack ; Laws, Dainteth, Reid, and Swinton. three-quarter backs Lally and O'Gara, half beck. ; G. Aspoy, S. Aspey, McCarthy, Lloyd, Johnson, and Rigby, forward. Wigen—Sharrock, beck ; Miller, Todd, Jenkins, and tw” titans, three-quarter beck,; Weave and Thomas, half backs : Seeding. Ramadale, Whittaker, Shatters. rind Cheotharn. forwards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. M. M. Taylor, H nslet. Touch jud g. Messrs. Rescue, liroughton, and Johnson, Selforq.</p>
        <p>COVENTRY SCORE WELL AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>This return have game attracted a good "gate" to the St. Helena ground, and twat productive of a series of scoring incidents right to the close. Early on. Harrison opened the score with a cleverly dropped goad, and following thin Flanagan intercepted a nese from Davies to Woodburn and crooned for Turtill to improve, '1 hen Benyon front Piggott's pass made his may over, l'ewlett failing. Bungling on the Coventry line ended in Lea increasing the "Saints" acorn, and igmediately on resuming klevity crossed for Tortill to iaprove. Woodburn placed o penalty stwcassfully, and I later scored a t rv, a bleb he goaled. Chavasse scored again for St. Helena, HalLtitne score—St. If elens,ll goals 4 trim l pointel, Coventry 2 goals 2 tries (10 point.). Changing ends Coventry at once attacked. The home team were penalised for an informality, and W todburn placei a goal. St. Helens opened not, and Flanagan picking up in the loose ran in under the poste. Tortill improved.</p>
