        <p>One of the largest and roost enthusiastic organizing meetinge ever held by the Warrington Progresseise Labour League took place at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening. the rusaembly room beiog crowded. Mr A. H. Crosfield attend,' to address the member's, and hod splendid ',cep' ion. Mr. E. Wynnessionea presided. nod Woo 'supported by Councillor Locker, , Messrs. S. Alright, li. Welker, J. Morton, l Peacock, P. Ilelanev, and IS. Farrar. The Cnlpt on claimed that the League had proved !Heel, to be en elacient fighting force for the cottons of Liberalism end pates.e. They hod in the peat fought on greet questions. He believed they had killed tariff reform—ilaughtwr and appistwe)—in fact the Tories were only looking at it sidexes now, an !they wonld not look at it at ell. hefore Ice:. They were etill talking emit Itsperinl Prefer- ence, slthooch the bottom had hero knocked out 'of it. HI. believed the enektion of Preerence man. real and positive dring,r, the ultimate reoutts of which they were unable to renlice. An progressives they wmilti also ham to Ilght the wettestl to introduce tie Iteferendinn, for illt WO., }Olt into practice the people who would tr in every time would be those who could spend the most money. TL watt I.oe nf the illott studernocsasic forces in the world. Nir. Balite atated 0. Moodny night that the Refereetirn woniti not ^Pal.' to litticeta. they knew Hist if they wittmitted Tariff Reform to the electors it would have no chance, despite all the money they arimild lie able to apend upon it. There were trivetimis of social policy whims it would be the duty of the Longer to tackle. Flo believed that just as in the poet they be done something to exp... whet Mn. Churchill called "the glittering ftillncien of Tariff Reform " so in the future they wancld do notch to coyote the inaincere policy ot the Tory Party. (Appleoe.l lb. If. Ntov, of flit tomes, delivered a vigorotte addres in which he urged the noconsity of uniting their forme end prese