        <p>PROTD'TION AND MONOPOLY and for those policion which tended to aggravate instead of gradually to lesson the excessive disparities between the wealth and poverty existed in our land. Progressive principles were worth fighting for not because they moult give r.s the millennium, hot boccien they helped or steadily and surely on the road to better thing, in the life-time of the present generation. vinch great and sacred rauset were worthy of the energy and backbone 1 of the Intelligent artiesna of Lancashire, who were the admiration of the world, and it was therefore regres• table thst Warrington should leave other industrial constituencies to win the battle for RA. (Hear, hear.l We ought to take our share in it. (Appinose.) Desling with the practical Rood Vint restated from Helical administration. Mr. Crnslield first commented on in the democratic finance of Mr. Lloyd George's liolgiits which adjusited the hordes of tasatiou ac audio; to ability to beer it. 'rho fort that electors were induced to vote for the Conservatives, under the false impres- sion that small householders would be hit hr tbs. bind totes, showed the lack of information which would have to he remedied by steady educational work. With regard to the fiscal controversy, in spite of • oup,AL toI.KNDITCRE AND p, CRY ANIERP• AN</p>
        <p>which ha,l boon res vow to galling tbe</p>
        <p>electors into voting for Protection,the Fnitad kingdom had stood firm to the Free Trade policy ori which our remorkabbe commercial propriety was based. (Applause.) Tbe tariff reformer, Isere being found oat abroad •8 well a. at home. 1 here was evidence of a revolt even In those quarters shirth were thought to lo the very stronghold. of Protection. It was •lways thought that,whataiver else happened, the agriculturiats could lie" nibbled" for ' Protection. The small farmers in /tun ria and Germany, however, hail found that it was only the greet landowner, producing enormous quantities of corn which he could sell at a fancy price, who grew fat and rich by the system. I.:norm.a burden. were imposed upon the worlers and and the •nmll farmer, far from reaping the rich harvest which he anticipated from tariff., found the fries of everything he used in,. enhanced and in the end he was worse off. Ile (Mr. flrocflelilj had met well.informed people from iiroteciml countries .1 even Conserva- tives spoke almost in friar and trembling of the iiihlio hatred of such axi stem of taxation aid the iDi Int ices which it had led to. Ile had never thought the ilie• tribution of weilth in this country wan what it should be, and ODO of the great and noble ideals which pro- greasives had to work for Wan the •• •• ' "</p>
        <p>Mil, 11,1, UV our w scs, When that had lie. Ifftnhl;mluld, they cooly' let com- petition, resisted by trade unionism nod honest negotiationa, properly safeguarded by the State, betweeo capital and labour, regulate the rate of wages above the minimum. The renult of such a reform, be contended, combined with no improved land nyattes, would be to get rid of the squalor of slorndom. (A petit loin) Whet ROVIId bottle advantage of a minimum 'ince if we were widdlwi with a fiscal system which Iniseated the price of all necensaries, from boots to caps and broad to bottom., so es to reduce the purchasing power of 18e. to Inn ? Such n policy was not only fitment hut elmont criminal. (Appinuse) It was a standing M. VPI to him how any well with a fixed swoop—he he clerk or curate. atop-keeper or foremen—could vote foreplay which would mnke such 'en et tuck upon his livelihood. From 10511, when Free !Tirade got thrirounbly into the while In England, the advance ho real wages paid to the 'mimeo( and labourers of thins:notary had bean from 1,0 to FM per cent. In America, with its untold resources •nd 0110 roma real wagon bad seriously diminished for the hart fifteen yearn, under the occurred Protective system. Mr. Crotitleld ntrongly emphasis,' the necessity of co-firditerting •II bre; clips of the Liberal orgnnisation in Warrington and said the airorninaltin lunttutu which .st brought to I ft, in country districts to help the forces of reaction was all the more reason why the electors in the great industrial towns should he 'ably to fist., the 'rattle for thorn who</p>
        <p>mere weaker and worse olf. Ile deeply valued the pimin of lead•rship in which the progressives of Warrington bad pieced him, and convinced of the greatness, tuition and true humanity thereelte they tied to fight for, he we. sure the members of thi.t leap. would do their utmost to ! carry it to a successful issue whenever the neat opportunity came. Applause). Mr. .1. MORTON proposed a vote of thanks to %Ir. Crosfield for hie address. It. said Nlr. Criinaeld was • damoerat, fit to lead a labour organisation, nod they bad the utmost confi erica in him.</p>
