      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS. ANNUAL DINNER AT WARRINGTON. Speech by Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</head>
        <p>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS. ANNUAL DINNER AT WARRINGTON. Speech by Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>That genial good fellowship which is such a happy characteristic of the fraternity of commercial travellers throughout the land found expression on Saturday evening, when the fifth annual dinner in emneetion with the Warrington branch of the United Kingdom Commercial Travellers' Association was held at the Lion Hotel. The arrangements were such as to ensure a highly enjoyable evening and the company of about sixty which assembled voted the gathering a com- plete euccese. Mr. W. P. Ryland. (president of the branch) occupied the chair and amongst others present were 'rho Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), Mr. A. 11. Croslield, Onincillor J. T. locker, Mr. J. Fulton-Smith, Mr. W. F. Brooks (ex-chairman of the U.K.C.T.A ) and the following branch officials • —Mr. 11. Mercer, chair- man ; Mr. F. Plimpton, vire-chairman •, Mr. W. L. Walker, hon. assistant secretary ; Mr. W. G. Hayes, bon. secretary. Letters of apology for non-attendance were recei,ed from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Mr. I larold Smith, M.P., Aldermou W. J. Forehaw and W. Pierpoint, Mr. S. Rigby Armitage, Mr. T. Granger (chairman of the U.K.C.T.A.), the Mayor of Widnes, Mr. John (ieddea and Mr. W.l'. Ashton (hon. treasurer of the branch). In the course of his letter Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. wrote: "it in a great diaappointinent, as naturally I very much deeired to meet a representative gathering of the local membero of your great and influential union which plays no important a pert in stir corn- Mere, Portlier. I have bertillienjoyed Vie hospitality of other branches on several occasions and have always congratulated myself that I have met the best of good fellows from whom one acquires a fund of good information and good stories." Mr. W. P. Ashton was prevented from attending by the death of his mother, which occurred on Saturday morning. In a letter he expressed particular regret that he wonld not have the opportunity of proposlng the toast of "The President, which stood in his name, as be and his father hetes, him commenced business at the firm of Messrs. fly-lands liras. An excellent dinner wan served by Mr. John tree and afterwards there was the usual speech-making. interspersal with songs. rhe loyal toasts WPM proposed from the chidr and enthusiastically honoured. Mr.A. 11. CROSEIKI,o, who WAS received with cheese. proposed "The Mayor and Corporation of Warrington." I In said he wan gottefttl for their kindreception—n most encournging welcome to be received by as unhorsed , knight—flaueliter)—iimplemeuted by an echoing marl I sympathetic cheer from hie friend in the chair. tlanghter.) Itowns much "bilged to the members of that ansocintion ,for inviting aim to that gathering, because it revived very interesting old memories con- nected with the first dozen or fifteen years that he .peat at Bank (limy when a good part of his commercial career was asaociated with joint the sante Ititel of work which those present had In hand. A •ery important trannition took place in tile buainese of the ofdmint that time and they had to lay themselves out to create new olarces of trade both in the home and export market. Therefore, smut from him work at Sank Qtany he was • great deal on the road in those days—be could not exactly say from Jobreo'Groitts to lands' End, but over a good part of England and some parts of Scotland he did a fair amount of travelling. He had to tackle the merchants in the export trade and the distributors in the home trade. Ho must say be found it very interest- ing work, and a pretty tough job, too—(hear, hear)— even in olil England. But North of the border— (laughter)—well words faded him to describe his experiences. Ile had to find a good ninny word. then, when he was trying to get people to take soap at 21s. per cwt.,and they had been necustomed to liuy it at Ha. (Laughter.) Ho well remembered in the old dilys saying good-bye " to their egent in Glasgow after about three days of it, morning, noon and night. Ile was just able to whisper " good-bye " to him ; he hind ns voice left. (laughter.) "It is rather unkind of my old friend Fulton Smith to jeer at him " cononeed Mr. Cros- field—•'a nern Sassonach trying to trtckle his fellow countrymen." (Laughter.) Proceeding to the subject ' of the toast, Mr. Crosfield said he was sure he could express on behalf of all present whet he personally felt so strongly—the good fortune that belonged to them in Warrington in having es the chief officer of the borough at the present , time a Mayor who by bin winning personality and unfailing good nature, as well as by his unreniitting Industry and accomplishments, endeared himself to everybody with whom be came into contact(Applause.l "The fact that kin Worship and myself happen to be men of different political colours " added Mr.Croslield, n has only enabled ate the latter to appreciste the perfect courteny, colleted with admireble efficiency, which is possensed by the Mayor. We have some members of the Corporation, past and present, with as to-night. I was going to say there were other unhorsed knights here, beside. myself, hot that would not be correct One or two have had the discretion to enticipate circumntances." (Laughter.) However, he , was glad the "city fathers" who were engaged in such eminently importnnt public work in Warrington were facing their responsibilities so well. One could not enter upon a subject such en that sugggested by the toast, without reflecting on the renny striking thinp which had been mid by some of the wort eloquent men in England on the question of civio government , and the incronsing responsibility and dignity essociated with it, After all, one had only to think of edocetion, sanitation and the prospect ahead of as in the reform of the Poor Law and ninny other great branches of public duty, not excepting the abatement of the amoke nuittance—(latighter)—in order to realize the amount</p>
        <p>of responsibility that devolved upon the members of , the Corporation. As for the smoke nuisance, he wound venture to any in pursing that he hoped that, pertly by the precept of men like his friends Aldermen Ihmnett , and Mr. Parker, and the example which 151141 not or ; supposed to be set by a certain range of chimneys in the neighbourhood of hank Quay—(latighter)—the con- ditions in thie locality would be considerably improved. . In whatever way they looked at it, nobody could have beets in the House of Commons, as he wits for a few years, without realising the inevitable tendency to . &amp;central'co its work- -just as a large firm decentralii,ed its business—in a any that was throwing greater responsibilities upon the local statesmen who , had to carry out the work of our Corporation. If the . duties were heavy, some return watt forthcoming In the incresaieg amount of civic pride. It meet he a real source of satiefaction to those who undertook the management of Warringtoria affair. to ore the population of Warrington growing, its great Induetrial concerns; waxing in strength and power, and the true dignity of toil reflected in an increase of com- mercial capacity. (Applause). In Warrington, beaus glad that, teeth's that wonderful modern development of industrial progress, we had something else to ho prood of, because the chain if historic events carried us beet ton period prior to the beginnings of municipal affairs in the days; of the Anglo-Salons Commercially we could trace our history to the times when the great Boman@ in their iron imluetry were the pioneers and predecessors of some of the great flrms we had in Warrington to-day. (Applause./ One town them happily combinesi the aocient and morlern aspecta of life. Mr. Crosfield told several entesing atorcdotes, which were keenly relialied by the company. 'rte proverbial generoxity of the commercial traveller IPd bins to recall the experience of is parson who carer; tea new sphere of work full of beneficent ideas Ile chromed to meet a poor Old Irish woman. It .Isa winter time and she nest,,,, hard-op to buy foe!, to the tender-hesrted cleric promptly promised to used her some coals The woman expressed her gratitude in the words i "Hera your reverence. May ye never be wanting a fire yourself, either in this world ur the nest." (Loud laughter). The MAYOI3, in responding, said he hurl found the Mayoralty a heavy trek to undertake but he thought he could any, without boasting, that he never ehirled, see rule, scything that bed demanded attention. If he had not done the work woll, he had at all events, done his best. Ile had had aspirations for Hone years and 111006 he suoceeded to the honourable °nice he now held, they bad grown etronger and stronger, especiall7 an he found there were obstacles in the way. The Town , Council of Warrregtoo, be could any without any idea of flattery, would compare favortrably with any other Council in the country. It included good speakern and imenetjudgment LAI/balsam tact, who had the welfare</p>
        <p>of Warrington closely at heart. Others wlinv might tont Inn such prominent members of the Conceit were also very good workers and dinl yeoman service in corn/litter, Councillor lacuna proposed" The town and trade of Warrington."He recalled the interesting fact that his grandfather aerved with thn firm of which their proaident was none the head in the year PHI. When none considered the inetty branches of industry that had nerving from the tw n old concerns of Crogfield's and Hylands's and the way in which they had developed, i said no great dell for this grand old town. Its works I were equal almost to any in the world end ita mann- lectures enjoyed a world-wide renown. (Appinume., In his travela abroad it had been one of his greatest pleasures to find that Warrington was well-known and respected for the high-class goods it exported. Warrington practically existed on its export trade. It woo a great pride to him to think that works of such immense dimousions sprang from such smell beginnings and that the concerns gtill born the ...red names of their founders. (Applause.) Councillor Locker com- mended the efforts of the Council to make Warrington, worthy in every respect of the eminent position I which it OCCUlliffd an a commercial and ' manufacturing centre. The town had been very much Oct of date in years gone by I.nll the I improvement from the municipal point of view had not kept pace with the remarkarde development on the commercial side. hod the Council begun to effect great improvements twenty years ago, instead of livelong to begin when the town win so congested that it FM alnvret impeasible to move along some of tine main , at r.ts, we &amp;mold have had four atreeta 011.1 to Bridge- Street long ago. He was glad that there were men in the Council who were pledged to go ahead with those ' improvements which wera alreolutely necessary for the welfare of the borough. (Applause). Mr. FCLTICi SMITII, In regponding, complimented Councillor Locker upon his happy and felicitous speech in defence of his native town. Many Warrington people were only too willing to run down their OWII town, and make it .t to he the blackest place on the face of the earth. Ile did not relieve it 11,11. and he was proud that Councillor Locker had such • , high appreciation of his town .d was able to trace , its growth in the happy way in which omen who wry in buainess should look at thingv aroond him. Abroad the name of Warrington IVILN thO trade mark for optality and it was recognized as such wherever none wout. If an article had the Warrington Irrennl, it carried its own guarantee. It wan said that Warrington one backward, but it seemed to him to Ire the otlmr way. It. wan es Iprogressive that wo were almost unconscious of the , rapidity of its progress. That w.lB why people thought that in the tontter of nrchitectere and erreta the town was not as quadri-dere as it shoold he. We had been ann busy attending tin busiress that municipal matters seemed to hose taken 11 secondary piae.e in the past. , The Council had, however, awakened to the import- ' ance of tine fact that for the advantage of Warriugton as a commercial town, facilities must he provided to, meet the increming demands which the traffic wits I making upon our streets. lie understood that the widening of Butterniarket-street was to he mannered. , I with immediately, CO as 1,0 admit of through transit I from east tin west of the town. (Appian.). Mr. W. P. BY LANDS prOFIMIA "Tho u.K.c.T.A. and , the Comniercrel Travellers' Schools and Benevolent Institution." Ile pointed tont that the object .4 the .sociation had four aspects. First there wag the noels], aspect in which the good fellowship for which core- I mondial travellem were always esteemed, played nn important pert. In the second place tlrero onus union of effort to obtain concessions from railway conirenies, hotels and the like for the comfort and ronvernience of travellers. Thirdly, co-operation enabled them to provide teaching for the children of corn:remind travellers and he never passed Pottier station without looking with great adrnirstion Imre those mtgnificent schoola and relleoteel that there wits a standing monu- ment of th v sympathy of the commercial travellers with the education of the children of their member. Finally, one of the inset beautiful features of the association was that by co-operative effort the members were able to hold out n helping head to those who through mis- fortune fell into distress, (Applause). Mr. W. r. BOOOK.I (ex-chairman of the U.K.C.T.A.). In responding,coogratulated the Warr' ngton branch upon its progrres. Thad, however, was not wonderful when one considered what a magnificent commercial centre Warrington wins. Unity woo strength and every profession or calling nowadays found it necesaery to have an wooledoos to defend its own interests in the world's struggle. Pie. Brooks then gave particulars of the propene of the minociation ruirl mentioned that new officre had been opened in Icredon. He wished particularly to enlist toe interest of the Warrington members in the proposal to train the young commer- cial traveller in the knowledge of foreign languages. The Liverpool branch had taken an advance step in that direction and arrangements for the holding of classes , had been made with Liverpool education anthorities. Onnr progrom as a commercial nation depended largely noon ow foreign trade enni we would be enable to maintain our position unleas the cnimmercial travellers were conversant with the language of the countries to which they were sent. A scheme woold he presented to the next conference for the fortnatioe of a benevolent Bind which would ho oren to every member of the asnociation and they had olson pereion scheme in view. He regretted that the Werriegton branch had not yet seen its way to form a branch of the benefit society in this town. That anneiety hail accumulated funds to the arnottet of £07,000 and raid in sick benefit I.st veer L 3,700. With regard to the school. at Pinner 2,900 -children had been admitted to them Mace their fonandation and there were at present 310 children in residence; being clothed, fed and educated free. It required an annual income of £17,000 to maintain them. it was, he meintanned, the duty of all [revellers to come forward to help their less fortunate COUP/IGUPe end the nasociation enabled them to do something practical in that direction. (Applauge). Mr. F. BROOK (Winleen) in a witty and felicitious speech, propreed " The Warrington and District Branch." Ire advised the members to get sonic infinential local gentlemen to found ncholarnhips and send youug men to Paris for e yearn' training in French.</p>
