        <p>brightest and greatest days this country had ever seen woeld have begun. (Applause.) Councillor Hawthorn also addressed the meeting. lie nail he was girol to see 81.01, Is large meeting of Liberal women in Whiteurons Went and it nugured well for the I minter:Ancc of the Liberal tredi lions of the WIST,I. I (Applause). That day hail witnensel the cloning of the first Parliament elected in he reign of King George and it promised to 1-1 one of the most important Pruliaments which had ensembles! nt Westmineter. Their regret in Werrington wart that Mr. Arthur Crosfield wan not there to support the Prensier red his colleagues in abolishing the veto of elia House of horde. (Hear, hear). Fortunately although Warrington changed its political allegiance the country remeined loyal to the Liberal cause and the Government went leek quite an strong aa when they came out of ollice.(Applause). As Liberals they were pleased to nee that the first place had been given in the King's Srreech to the Parliament Rill. The veto was a smell word teeming great deal. (Hear, henr). They contended that Littered leginlation should have en equal choice with Conservative legisla- tion of becoming the law of the land, but at prewnt the Lords exercised their veto for crippling Liberal h•g isla- Hon and when their own interests were affected the horde did not scruple to three out trills regatillea of the public interest. 'rho Government were determined to remove thin anomaly, event if they were compelled to take the extreme step of obtaining the King's nano ion to create autlicient Liberal peers to pass the Hill. Reference wen made by the speaker to meanitres fore- casted in the hinge epench which affected worm n quite es much tithe omen, including the removal of the pauper disqualilication to the receipt. Id old Age pennions and provisions for the insurance of the industrial population strained sicknesn sod invalidity and for the iIIBIIMICO againin unemployment of those engaged in (Tall. e