      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Iru:,ry</head>
        <p>Iru:,ry</p>
        <p>11 1911.</p>
        <p>_ IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</p>
        <p>ANY SLICK'!') BEAT THE DONKEY,</p>
        <p>What was well described as "one of the must •11•3111IIIIMIS lx,litical dodges ever attempted " was utterly routed in a full dress debate in the House of Commons on Tuesday last. The facts of the ease may be briefly stated—for the facts are few, and melancholy enough in all truth. Mr. and Mrs. McCann, a Catholic husband soil • Presbyterian wife, of Belfast have unfortunately not found the married state as everybody would desire them to find it. The husband avows that it was " utterly impossible for him to live tinder the same roof with thin woman" who offended his every religious feeling and deprived hint of social joys. Three months ago McCann left bin wife, and the children —over whom the law of these realms given hint as their father 101 l charge and custoily—are Paid to have been since kidnapped and all the forces if Ireland's pantie have been working risen to discover theta. This miserable family equabble domentic affliction common to all times and climes formed the brief for Mr. Campbell, a prominent Unionist lawyer, for an empty tirade upon the miestion of Irish Self- ( iovernment. The Moot o11.•-l1 ye Speed) in the debate which lasted over two hours nose that of Mr. Joseph Devlin, the Nationalist f • JJJ ber for West Belfast. After the melodrarnic performances of Mr. Carophell and Sir Edward Carson, Mr. Devlin came along with the fullest knowledge of the entire facts of the case and explained how four or live days before his eleetion this paltry and wholly irrelevant, affair had been dragged mint into the political arena for the mealiest party ends. Ile challenged the Unionists to name any privet who had hero guilty of the interference they described and exhorted Mr. Campbell to take the whole medley of myth and extravagant charges to the tribunal of any court in the Kingdom. Mr. Birrell, ni terms singularly appreciative of the human flllll • pathetic side of the affair, well pointed out that if the , wealthy Unionists of Belfast 'really considered the j women they had at their hands the ordinary late of . the land, hut of course, untie said "the fact is—it is too obvious and too apparent for everybody-. the I, the interests of the children were taterificed." The</p>
        <p>production in the House by Mr. Devlin of the letter sent iy a prominent Belfeet minister, a Mr. A. WDot gall, in which he claimed he " bad found a tit- bit t last " and was confident that the mineable family fend of the McCanns would serve an an absolutely convincing argument vat&amp; Home Rule, gives the true inwardness of Vie whole matter and allows further in what sorry plight the Unionists must he when they are compelled to trot out such utterly puerile supports for their rune. What ground there can be for an anti-llome Rule argument in a irrelevant case which happened tinder the present regime, not under a Home Rule Govern- ment mark y.,u, only the distort.' mind of a Campbell or a Carson is permitted to understand. PAYMENT OF MEMBERS A SACRIFICE FOR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>IRELAND.</head>
        <p>IRELAND.</p>
        <p>....... ....... The decision of the Irish Parliamentary Party in restrict of the proposed payment of members is a wise statemanlike step. Whilst supporting the pre- pare] on its broadly democratic basis, the Party have unanimously decided in view of the peculiar condition of Ireland in the rnited K iugdom, to ask the Govern- ment to grant an exemption or the Nationaliat members and to devote the Nataliea which otherwise could go into the generally empty pockete of Irish members to some useful public purpose in Ireland. It is a splendid instance of genuine feeling for the welfare of their native land that there men, most of whom by reason of their financial position feel the burden and ntrain of attendance at Westminster more than any other scotion of the House of Commons, should forego their claim in no important a matter to ask instead that their salaries ahould be pooled by the Government and sent for the ecotioinic advance- ment of the country. Few people in tide country will In, able properly to appraise that decimioa but to theme • who know the poverty of Ireland, the sacrifice that membership of Parliament has meant for so many Irisbnien in the present and in the past, it will be abundantly clear that for true disintereetedness and the principle of putting country before self, there is probably nothing no high in recent political history no the attitude of the Irish member.; which we have just outlined. GINNELL'S GRIEVANCE.</p>
        <p>An element of strife appears to have entered into the atmosphere of the new Mune of Commons from its lint meeting onwards. And Irish name, have been asaociatml with the breezy momenta from'the Speaker's election to the McCann ..ase. Mr. I who had NORIO caustic reflections to make on the election of the Speaker, stoke entirely on his own account and not fir the Irish party. Immediately after both sides if the Howie had given teetinmny to Mr. Lowther'e popularity, Mr. Ginnell in • Nome- what determinist fashion intervened. It wan an innovation and Mr. Ginnoll showed 1.1110 perepicacity in selecting the opportunity—unique in that there cots Id be no closure and noanthority whatever to call him to order. Although all parties in the Howie tnenciat.,,l themsel, es with the motion in the Speaker's re-election, Mr. Ginnell'm individual art, we think, is not to be regarded as a mere piece nf Parliamentary dittenurtesy. Without going no far an Mn. Gionell went after what Inte reached ite in con•ereation with private members from both the Liberal and Tory rank., we feel meth, sure that Mr. Oilmen struck a eympathetie cord in many a breast when he complained of the irritating and undemocratic system by which speakers in the more important debates were confined to names given to the Speaker of the Howie by the l'arty Whips Mr. linnell, it may be mentioned, is a barrister, and woo for some time a secretary to laird Morley. when be as Mr. John Morley woe Chief Secretary for Ireland. ST. PATRICK'S DAY. The armagentent. for St. Patrick's Day relebrati, no are already well under way, and the preliminarin of m icy banquets and meetings throughout England and Ireland are completed. Mr. John Ml' , is to be the speaker at the Mans Meeting in the Fren Trade Hall, Manchentsr, en March Path. Ile will address a meeting at Halifax on the hith peon. The annual ban, inet arranged by the Irishmen "r Manchester and District, will be held on Mareh , 10th, when the trial. Parliamentary Party will be I represented by Dr. Eemonde, the new member for</p>
