        <p>Alderman PIEHMIINT said lie thought it wart wel that at that stage he should rifler genie explanation of bin position in the matter. Ile would like to inform Councillor Ashurst that it wan not neemnstary to give notice of any hominess to he nulenitted to the funeral Pianos. Committee. The course he adopted wan not illegal or improper, and he was perfectly joatilled in wubmitting the motion an he did. Ile proposed it looms° it wan nn alemlutelv necenaary one, with which the majority of the Come il weidd agree. The Town Clerk men appointed in 10110, and the completion 171 yearn' good, honeati straightforward and clever ser•ice merited 401110 substantial testimonial. (Hear, hoar.) Important changen had taken place under Mr. Whittle', regime, and the dollen of the Town Clerk, together with his responsibilities, had proportionately increased. Vet for fourteen years his salary had remained stationary. Ile admitted that. Mr. Whittle had been paid for what be had clone. At the sumo time ha did his work mimic/tidy, and in inch a manner that whatever hie salary might have heon he could not have carried ont his ditties totter than be hol done lie Might haVf• done the work in much a way aS 10 cold the Corporation a considerable aunt annually hut, en a teethes' of fect.l.llo work had been done an economically all in any other Lancashire borough, an,l they had a Town Clerk equal 0000 y other town clerk. Mn. Whittle wee the lowest paid town clerk of Roy in boroughs cif a !similar trios ao.I iMpOrilmeo to Warring- tell. AL Wigan, Southport, lit. Helens. Rotherham and liournernotith the town clerks were osch paid £l,OOO let yeer. ('heater, a city half the Clue of Warrington, paid It, town clerk .ClOO. They were only paying £7OO, salary not commensurate with the importance of the town or with the inveloahle legal wo. k which had been performed hy Mr. Whittle. The novement which had resulted in that necessary recommendation wan entirely originnted by hiruself, on his two re