        <p>Aldurman licshotrr mtspported the minutes on all the grounds that Councillor Pemberton °intoned them (inc of the beat way, of safeatimrding the highemt interests of the town and the ratepayer% was to secure the most efficient end okilftil official. The only way to do wan to treat the officiels fairly and give then. reagonahle recompenoe. The real interesta of the rate- payer. were identinsil with the good of the town in its broader aspect, and wore not a there question of ponada, shillinga and pence. 'Fhe quotation of the scavenger.' wage, liii not enter into the coomideration of the town clerk', oelary. At the same time him myrnpathy was with the aoaveneers, soil it was ocandal that no many of the working clamem of England received such poor wages. The Corporation was bound to pay for cloned .r•icer at the market price • The mimicipal life was lieoomir g hicresaingly active and its ondertakinga, departreenta and enterprise. were growing larger and more important every year. Those dwpartmenta were controlled and nupervised by the Town Clerk, who in addition attended to other ariltions ci•ic Maim. Could they find man anywhere controlling work of ouch magnitude at no low a remuneration ? U. wax pima'ee they could not. (Ile., hear.) Mr. Whittle was•ntitled to no small amount of credit for the advante,prosperity, progre.n anil high reputation of hia native town. Ile knit prominently ..ciated himself with every progromive movement in a practical end hearty manner •nd "hi, monnment was 'wooed him.' If the Town Clerk wrote in tn industrial enterprime he would be getting more than the amount recommended. Ile was not controlling merely one enterprieo but three or forth Night after night when they were enjoy- ing them-tel.e in good, bad and in di ffereet way., Mr. Whittle wes working. There had always been one idea in his mind and that w-am the advancement and promperity of the town in which he was horn. Councillor Uolll{ll, said it wan his intention to 111,1p- por