        <p>(No, no,. 'rho NI A 1,11 : Von gave rue the imprenaion that the aggregate asseaament was leas. Councillor PF.M HILIITON : That ie not what I mid...</p>
        <p>.6-h-s;illn, Irotturu I have no doubt that Mr. Councillor Pemberton thought something quite different to what he mid. I am dealing with what he said. (Laughter.) It hes been mid that thia is not the proper time to Rm.. the Town Clarke salary. When did the Council think it was the proper time? (I leer, beer.) The Town Clerk bad been in office :A yearn and had bed no increaße for IT When is the re.onehl• and proper time for an Incree.,? Councillor C USNINOTON Not at all. (Laughter). Councillor K 0111.7, Now We have it. That Is about the plain English of it. Continuing, Councillor Roberts maid that if Mr. Whittle lied been in private j.recline as a whet., if be had not been ebb to make two or three Limes more than 1:700 a year with his tindoubted ability and absolute honesty or he( Councillor Roberti.) would haws hewn extremely disappointed with the result. They had received a circular (from the Property Owner, Arisociation) opposing the incr... Ile did not resent it he respected it. Itut after all they only repre.nbel their own particular class. Ile (Councillor Roberta) spoke for another section which was for more important and he said unhesitatingly that the large menufacturers of the town, who had been brought into association with the Town Clerk and his department, were practically unanimous in aupporting the increase which wax welLeerned and well deserved. The MAIOII. remarked that he vise only 000delited in the ordinary nay. Ile knew the location of the incresee ens coming off sometime but not when and he did not know the tempt AA Mayor no one keiew better than he did whet the Town Clerk had to do. Of course they might nay that he had an able staff —(hear, haw— hut still the Town Clerk was r.ponsible, and he could corroborate all that had been said about bin hard work. Ile bad spent a great deal more time than he ought to have spent if be bad (-moulted hi, own health an ninny others would do. Ile also reminded them that the Town Clerk was the chief official and. a medical man be felt somewhat jealous that the medical officer wee second to the law. (Laughter and hear, hear . 'lle voting remilted at, followa ; For the Amendment Skelland. Ashcroft, Chaßler, Dickenson, Cunnington, Laehford, and Pemberton. --7. Against —The Mayor, Alderman Forshaw, Pierpoint. Mmethiirat, Bennett, Bolton, 'Finnion, James Evens, John Eve., Barton; Councillors Locker, Craik. Sankey, Steel, IR. McKee., Ilewthosn, Toole, Wilnoni Archer, 8i01.., Roberta, Ifellian,Jenkina, and Holm.. The minutes were therefore agreed to.</p>
        <p>How TO STRENGTHEN THE LUNGS and Breathing Sterhanism, and insure immunity against Coughs, raids and Chronic Chest, Throat and Lung Trouble..</p>
