      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>N(YIFk6- W.</head>
        <p>N(YIFk6- W.</p>
        <p>There was a lapse from Parliamentary etiquette on Tuesday at the Debating Society when one member addressed another as "My Dear Chap," instead of the usual "My honourable friend, the member for—"</p>
        <p>On the banks of the River Jordan there is a bottling works, equipped, says the "Liver- pool Post," with American bottling machinery, engaged in bottling the water of the River Jordan for pious people throughout the world.</p>
        <p>A substantial start has been made with -the demolition of the property at the rear of Buttermarket-street, to enable the proposed widening to be carried out, the houses on 'Cropper's Brow, one of the oldest parts of the town, already having disappeared.</p>
        <p>The Corporation has been proved guilty of owning property which has not been painted for 12 years, and against which a closing order has been issued by the Health Com- mittee. There is nothing like cleaning your own doorstep before you go fault-finding in other people's houses!</p>
        <p>The Academy of Sciences at Malta, says Reuter, has determined in future to call the disease hitherto designated "Malta fever," Mediterranean fever." It is undersood that the step is a result of complaints made by the Governor of Malta to Sir Edward Grey, but it will not be any easier to suffer from on -that account. _ _</p>
        <p>A pathetic interest attaches to the election of borough auditors on March Ist, as it will probably be the last to take place in War- vington. If the clause of the Corporation Bill, insisted upon by the committee of the House of Commons, passes as drafted, the Local Government Board will take charge of the audit of the accounts for the coming year.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. K. Chesterton was the lecturer before the Hull Literary and Philosophical Society on Tuesday night, and he talked for an hour on "Progressing Backwards." As he was late in reaching the hall, and had to apologise, he said he would tell the trth (which was always improbable), and say that he lost his hat in his hotel, and at after a long and vain search had to drive through the streets of this magnificent city without it. He had lost his notes, too, but as to whether he had lost his head must be left to the judgment of his audience.</p>
        <p>The chief objection to the raising of the Town Clerk's salary from £7OO to £9OO, was that a rise of £2OO in one fell swoop was too much. No doubt there is something in the contention of Alderman Tinnion that the town has saved money through Mr. Whittle not having any increase of salary for 14 years. But it would have met the general view of the ratepayers more nearly if the salary had reached its new level by more modest increments. There is no question that, as chief official of the County Borough the Town Clerk is not over-paid at £9OO a year after 21 years of most assiduius public work.</p>
        <p>If the weather does not depart from its customary ways we may expect a very cold spell, with northerly winds, beginning now. This is one of the half-dozen events which, however wayward the weather may be at other times,recur every year,or almost every year, with singular regularity. February as a whole is 20 per cent, less stormy than January, but the week from the 7th to the 13th is as bad as any week in January. Odds of about 7 to 10 represent the probabilities of a fog and a fall of snow. Thunder is the only evil weather phenomenon which scarcely ever visits us in February. • • The "Daily Mail" would appear to be 11 training for another acrobatic feat. Finding things uncomfortable on the Protectionist side of the fence it is preparing to jump back to the safer position it formerly occupied. Its leading article of Saturday last is th.: spring-board from which the leap is to be taken. In that article the "Daily Mail" completely throws over the agricultural interest in this country, which has been the chief support of the Tariff movement. "Opinion throughout the Dominion of Canada" says the "Daily Mail," "shows beyond dispute that no such preference as our negligible two shillings a quarter on wheat would be even a slight temptation to Canadian farmers. *s*</p>
        <p>What has the Tariff Reform League to say on the matter? The "Daily Chronicle, anxious to ascertain the effect on the League of the "Daily Mail's" bitter pill, sent its representative to the headquarters of that organisation. The reply he received to his enquiries was brief, but cruelly emphatic. The" Daily Mail's" article was described as being "too silly for words." The officials of the League have vouchsafed the further information that "they would just as soon think of dropping the whole scheme as of dropping the preferential taxes on corn and meat.'</p>
        <p>Professor Hewins, the secretary of the Tariff Commission, who was also interviewed gave an answer, the curtness of which suggest that he was not feeling very amiably inclined towards the "Daily Mail," or, indeed, towards newspapers in general. "I have something else to do," he said, "besides commenting on the views expressed by a newspaper. I have nothing to say about it whatever."</p>
        <p>What could he say about it, after the back-handed compliment to his labours and Those of the Tariff Reform League, with which the "Mail " concluded its leader? " Much as we may deplore the immense amount of self-sacrifice and strenuous work that has been vainly extended in the prefer- ence propaganda in the British Isles, we cannot but rejoice that the Unionist party is now freed from the grossly unfair handicap that hen been placed upon it." It is hard lines to be called a "handicap" to the party of which one has supposed one's self to be The mainstay! No wonder the Tariff Reform League and its pet professor feel sore!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>NEW AND IMPROVED BREEDS OF SEEDS.</head>
        <p>NEW AND IMPROVED BREEDS OF SEEDS.</p>
        <p>The well known Agricultural Seed Eggert., Mews. Gartons Limited of Warrington, the principals of which are, Mr. Robert Garton, Mr. Cl. P. Milo and Mr T. R. Ostton, have just issued an English edition of their catalogue of New and Improved Breeds of Farm Seeds, and they are to be congratulated upon the production of a beautifully printed and well arranged work. In the introductory part of this Catalogue, there is printed an unsolicited testimonial in favour of their guaranteed Grass and Clover Such supplied for an order of over 900 acres. 'flies. and many other testi- monies go to prove the auperior qualities of the seeds this enterprising firm sends out.</p>
        <p>JAMMED COMPOUND (Aniseed Boners, Squill,To Ago.). For Coughs sod Colds. Influence, etc.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
