      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LA VERY. MR G ON &amp; SOCIAL</head>
        <p>THE LA VERY. MR G ON &amp; SOCIAL</p>
        <p>Irrots 1</p>
        <p>Mr. 0. K. Chesterton, the distinguished author and journalist, lectured in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey street, last even- ing on The Coming Slavery." His visit, which had been arranged by the Warrington Philomathic Society, attracted widespread interest and the hall was well filled. The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) presided. The Mayor said that although the president of the society might fittingly have occupied the chair, he thought the society had done right in asking the first citizen—be said it in the abstract—to render due homage to a man whom they all knew so well by repute. (Applause). If Mr. Chesterton was anything at all he was original, and it was said that " an ounce of originality is worth a ton of routine." He had also aspirations in the direction of setting society right. (Laughter and applause). Mr. Chesterton said that broadly the sub- ject of his lecture, so far as he could see it at the start—(laughter)—was Socialism. He wanted to make it clear that he was opposed to Socialism and Socialists, but his objection to them was mild and meek compared with his oppo3ition to almost all their established enemies. (Laughter.) In that, he was almost entirely alone in the world ; he was pining away, as they might see. (Laughter.) The objection to Socialism hitherto had always been stated wrongly. He had no truck at all, as far as he was concerned, with any of the celebrated opponents of Socialism. They included some of the greatest and best men in the world—Herbert Spencer, Brad- laugh and others. Even Mr. Mallock was not a bad sort. (Laughter.) Their objections seemed to him to be nonsense. (Laughter.) For instance Herbert Spencer wrote a book called "Man versus the State." Herbert Spencer was undoubtedly a very able man, but in what extraordinary state of indiges- tion or sleep or something else he wrote such a nonsensical title as that he (Mr. Chesterton) could not imagine. (Laughter.) The words literally had no meaning at all. They might just as well talk about man versus the sunshine or man versus the atmosphere. (Laughter). Man lived in a State—that was the definition of a man. All the stories about people on desert islands were stories of people remembering that they once belonged to a State and wishing to the deuce they could get back to it —(laughter)—and all the stories of savages which represented them as running wild in the woods without any social control were manifest nonsense, because, as a matter of fact, a savage was more controlled by his state than anybody else. Anyone who knew anything about savages knew that their conventionality and respectability were carried to a greater extreme than would be possible in any civilized community. First of all, he asserted that liberty was a thing intimately bound up with an idea of a State and of an authority. There was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>_ NO LIBERTY WITHOUT _AUTHORITY</head>
        <p>_ NO LIBERTY WITHOUT _AUTHORITY</p>
        <p>It was not merely a question of balancing one thing against the other. It was not merely that they must have a reasonable amount of foou and also a reasonable amount of beer—(laughter)—and that if they had too much of either the balance was upset. That was quite true of a great many things, including food and beer—(laughter)—but that was not what he meant when he said they could not have liberty without author- ity. He meant that if there had been no authority there never would have been any liberty at all in any conceivable intellectual constitution. They would have to find out what authority they believed in before they could be per- mitted to go on believing in liberty. If there were no State the individual would not be allowed to do what he chose; he would be knocked on the head by the next individual. (Laughter.) The Mallock argument against Socialism was that there had been a certain definite number of specially clever or inspired people who had conceived all ways of conquering nature, which the ordinary labourer would not have had if those things had not been invented and that therefore a reasonable extra payment went to them for having made the whole thing possible. In that light, it was contended, they might regard the payments to the manufacturer and the landlord as payments to the inventor. It was, however, quite obvious that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MODERN RICH</head>
        <p>THE MODERN RICH</p>
        <p>man did tot- CO-ri:ZW-pond to the people who had invented wonderful things. "That. I should think, is self-evident," continued M" Chesterton. "There is a very simple meth, for anyone who believes that the very rich are really the cle:erest in the community and have got there because they are the wisest, noblest and strongest—a very simple ending of that sort of illusion is simply to look at them. (Laughter.) Look at the portraits of the late Colonel North or Sir Thomas Lipton and see if you think they are people with the brains of Julius Ciesar and the star-like eyes of the super-man, looking onward. (Laugh- ter.) In the society in which we live the supreme wealth goes not only to wicked men but also very largely to silly men. I don't propose to analyse why that is, but I think it is perfectly obviously the fact to anybody who cares to look at the people in a motor car as they go by. (Laughter.) Does anybody seriously believe that the head of a big commercial firm in the modern world is an inventor? He never invented any- thing in his life, except excuses—(laughter) generally excuse for not seeing the man who has invented anything. (Laughter). I have not the slightest doubt that if people go on relying on these sort of arguments for resist- ing Socialism, nothing in heaven or hell can prevent Socialism coming about. After read- ing five or six pages of that sort of stuff, I don't understand why anybody is not a Socialist, at least for a few minutes. aught,er). The proposition that the ividuid is alright by himself and ought do everything for himself, when as a ter of fact he could not even talk if his her had not taught him—(laughter)—or proposition that the man who gets the money in any particular RBARIC SCRAMBLE OF THE MODE.C. MONEY MARKET tually the noblest and finest fellow these things I dismiss as bosh." Con- g, Mr. Chesterton said the primary of Socialism was that it would o poverty, and take off our shoulders horrible responsibility which we elt as we walked through the —the feeling that every garment we and every meal we ate was a good tter than that which the average of mages of God around us were permitted , or wear, or eat, or talk about. If we d all the property and paid out to tie le wages which were considered fair, it very possible there might be less walk - lasphemies and obscenities of poverty</p>
        <p>than were walking the streets at this moment. On the other hand any Socialist would agree that if there was a case againbt Socialism it was the possibility of official tyranny, of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>DRAGOONING OF MANKIND</head>
        <p>DRAGOONING OF MANKIND</p>
        <p>by policemen and people in buttons which must necessarily follow any pooling and handing out of anything. The good of Socialism was that it would relieve us of all this agony of impotent com- passion. The evil of Socialism was that it might increase the power of government and order the peopleabout to an extent such as might well be the foundation of a tyranny. We had started at the wrong end, and bit ,by hit we were finding the vast maim of I the English poor in a condition of slavery. We were no nearer to the society in which the 1, majority of the people would have a little money ordecent wages. What we were nearer to was a society in which the vast mass of I the very poor could be ordered about. We had at this stage simply got the despotic command of the poor without giving them those economic advantages which Socialism originally promised. One did not need to be very cynical about human nature to see that if the upper class or the governing class found that it was getting the great mass of the people of England in its power without having given them any of the land or capital, or any of the actual property, the governing class would he very prone to keep them still in that condition. (Applause.) The tendency of legislation had not been towards giving the poor more capital. He himself believed in the peasant proprietor idea as it existed in France. Ireland and most other jolly places. (Laughter.) The tend- ency had been towards bossing poor men more and more, with a promise of capital which was never fulfilled. That had been the history of social reform up to now. There was growing up around moderately wealthy men a small circle of feudal dependents. That was the most human form of slavery and if he had to choose between all sorts of things that might happen he would chose that. He would say " Let us all become barbaric chieftains, as in the dark ages, and have people around us who will do all sorts of things for us." There was, however, coming upon us, to all appearances, a vast slavery of the lower class. It was coming, not because anybody was specially trying to tyrannise over them, but because the curious thing called social reform had taken the form not of giving the poor anything of the carrots, but only the kicks of the Socialist system. (Laughter,) There was no evidence whatever that any social reforms, good or had, nice or nasty, of the last little time, had really transferred private capital from the rich to the poor. There was nothing in the tendency of our chief philanthropists, politicians, or general directors of a better state of affairs to indi- cate that they would even warn the people that they were getting into slavery. Most social philanthropists and idealists he knew detested and loathed the idea of democracy. VISIT IF MR. ALFRED TENNYSoN DICKENS.</p>
        <p>tin-cler ihe—s-oc—ie-t;;;-a-i—te—pices--,-ii;.-Alf;ed-T;;;;;;;;; Dickens, eldest surviving son of the late Charles ticket's. will lecture jin the Parr Hell on Tuesday, February 281.13: on " My father's life end works."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WARRINGTON SWIMMER FOR CANADA.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SWIMMER FOR CANADA.</p>
        <p>Mr, Robert Allen, one of the beet known members of the Warringtott Swimming Club. sets sail from Liverpool for Canada to-day (Saturday) on the liner Latirentic," and will proceed to Hamilton, Ontario. Mr. Allen is one of the smart., exponeets of the natatory art in this di•orict and pomesses n fiLe record of competition succe-sea.lle commenced swimming in 1899 at the agent thirteenin 1902 he obtained second place in the Wart i ogton Swinitn:ng ('lab's yards handicap, and was al-o wood in the 100 yards race at Hock Savage. The earne year he omitted the Wartitigton water polo team when they won the championship of the third division of the Liverpool and District League. In 1903 Mr. Allen swam third to J. Peers in the 1-J1 yards champ'omlip of War- rington and planet with the polo team in a notable w.e.,on which ended in the:r eecotnittie champions of Or 2nd division of the Liverpool and Dietriet League.</p>
