      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>NATURAL-SWEETS.</head>
        <p>NATURAL-SWEETS.</p>
        <p>Most people lo ,k upon the." sweet ' course as merely pleasurable, but in reality it should be an important part a the meal. Sweets can be quite nutritious Its well as tasty. Those composed of fresh fruits would.be merely cleansers of the system were it not for the added Roger and cream which provide the nutriment and fatty matter no necessary for the maintenance of animal heat and physical eneru. Scientific dietists are most emphatic cu the point that food values should be considered in regard to every dish. Acting upon this hypothesis. every housewife should make a study of the wonderful properties of the Greek currant—the fruit which is all nourish- ment. Currants contain an abundance of natural grape sugar, the best form of food, and the daily sweet course can be attractively varied by the use of this marvellous little fruit.</p>
        <p>Your Grocer has a u.eful little curreot cookery book, which he will gladly hand you free on request.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
