      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>rat"-</head>
        <p>rat"-</p>
        <p>CLERGY OF HOLY</p>
        <p>rPholo, Birt tea</p>
        <p>REV. E. C. E. CARLETON, M.A. (Viv,kr).</p>
        <p>Situated in the heart of Warrington, amid the noise and bustle of the junction of the four main business thoroughfares, lloly Trinity Church has for many generations been one of the met conspicuous and interesting landmarks in the "good old town." Its fascinating historical records, extending to a remote age, have previously been dealt with in our columns, mid it will suffice to say that the Chnrch is to-day fulfilling its high traditions as one of the most , important and useful influences in local Church life. For nearly thirty-three years the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, MA., has bad charge of the work in the parish ard he occupies a warm place in the affections of his flock, amongst whom he has labouted most devotedly. Mr. Carleton is a native of the Emerald Isle and was horn in the County Roscommon in the year 1843. At the age of IS, after having been privately coached by one of the masters of the Manchester I:rammer School, he entered St. John's College. Cambridge, snd took the degree of B.A.,when ii. He became an M.A. in 1000. Henna ordained deacon in 18001 by Bishop Prince Lee, of ?danehester, and entered upon the curacy of St. John's, Over Darwen, of which the late Bev. Philip Graham was the founder and first vicar. Ile wan ordained to the priesthood in 1808. Mr. Mr. Carleton remained at Darwen until 1800 and his next emery mason Sciasett, near Huddersfield. In 1870 he was appointed by the late Archdeacon Pollock to the charge of the Chapel of Ease of St. Mark's, Dunham Massey. Work in the town,however, appealed to him more strongly, and he moved to St. Luke's,Chorlton-on-Medlock,Manchester. Sul sequently lie held the curacies of St. Philips, Salford and Holy Inn, cents, Fallowfield. In 1878 Mr. Carleton was appointed by the Rev. W. Quekett to the Vicarage if Holy Trinity, Warrington, and under his care gratify- ing progress has been made in the church and its many and varied activities. New schools have been built and an old school enlarged, the church of Si.</p>
        <p>TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>[Photo Field, Putney. REV. H. R. WILD, (Curate-in-charge of St. Luke'i).</p>
        <p>Luke erected in Liverpool-road, a minion root opened for nervier in Factory-yard, and Holy Trinity Church renovated and reaeated. Attached to the church are numerous flouriahing organizations, including a Mena Institute, Girl. Friendly Society, and Ladies' Mi.sionary Work Society. Mr. Carleton takes a eery special interest in all constitutional fires- lions affecting the Church'. law and hiatory and is always ready to defend her, both with voice and pen. He isa sturdy and uncompromising controversialist and a keen debater on political a• well as religions questions ; bat although he is shard hitter, he ban gained the friendship and regard of members of all denomination., who respect hie loyally to his con- •ictions. At one time Mr. Carleton reaps member of the Hoard of rivardiaint, and he has held several other public offices. The Rev. /I. R. Wild, M.A., hen had charge of St. Lnke's Church since it, erection in 1893. His early education was received at the Manchester Grammar School and Owen's College, Manchester, and he graduated at Merton College, Oxford, obtaining the degree of B.A. in 1857 and M.A. four years later. While an assistant master at Mansfield Grammar School, Notts. he was ordained deacon by the late Bishop Wilberforce and took up work in the parish of Tynemouth. He became a priest in 1890 arid remained at Tynemonth until his appointment to the .uracy at Holy Trinity,Warringiontwith charge of St. Luke'. Church) in 1893. Mr. Wild has done a quiet but mach appreciated work in thin p muloue, working claws district arid one of the most satiefact .ry features of the progress at St. Luke's in that the :umber of communicants is steadily increasing. Mr. Wild has always token a keen interest in athletics end played in the football team at Merton. He was also a capital sprinter and captured many college prizes. To-day his favourite recreation is gulf of whiah he is an enthusiastic and skilful opponent.</p>
        <p>, I GTOPI ch /slit _ RELIGIOUS e- SOCIAL 1111, . • .41 • • „ . . •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>New Curate of Daresbun.</head>
