      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>St. James Church, Latchford. The Church and Amusements.</head>
        <p>St. James Church, Latchford. The Church and Amusements.</p>
        <p>In the comers of his monthly letter to the pariehionera of St. Jetnee', Latchford, the *leer (Rev, H. B. Firth) writes s—During Jannary me hare had a somber of nodal gatherings which seem to have been enjoyed. How far the church should cater for the entertainment and amusement of the people ia a ques- tion frequently discussed and one on which persons whose opinions are well balanced widely differ. That ' we should meet one another as merubera of the sam• Church end get to know Our fellow-WOrShip.lll better seems eminently desirable. One would like to tb that all Churctipeople realized that, on account their Churchronnship, end were united by a bond ..1 kindred and felloeship. So fnr in social gatherings tend to promote friendliness arid sympathy they would seem to be useful. If they minister to mere excite- ment and selfish amusement It would appear difficult to justify their existence. It Is undoubtedly a reproach that may be levelled against Churchpeople Omit they ere reserved and lacking in the senee of comradeship. Surely all of us who worship in the Berne church and kneel together before the KIMe alter shoitld feel that we are members of the same family, and for member. of the same family not even to be on speaking terms with one another is anythinc but right. , During the present month Parliament will re-aesenible , and questions of the most vital imnorr•nce to the nation will be debated. Greet issue% will he raised,and the proceeding. at Westminster will he watched with interest and anxiety by all sections of the community. Day by day when Parliament is flitting we use the prayer for the " High Court of Parliament " and it is incumbent upon Corietian people who believe ia the value of prayer to pray earnestly both in Cloirch and nt home that Cio.l may bless our country, and cause the decisions that are arrived at in the great Council if the nation to tend to the advatioemeot of His Kingdom end the ultimate good of Hie people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Rev. E. C. E Carleton and Welsh</head>
        <p>Rev. E. C. E Carleton and Welsh</p>
        <p>Dlsestablishment.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. E. Corleion recently addressed a meeting of Churchmen and Nonconformists in Liver- pool on the question of Welsh disestablishment. A full report of his remarks appears in the February issue of the "Holy Trinity Pariah Magszine." Passing over the argument in favour of the Disendolvrnent of the Welsh Church derived from the numerical test,</p>
        <p>added that it WaR .01;;i011Sly 611 argument that migl be used against the very existence ot the Welch Chur in any shsics or form ; and now actually being used the purpose of taking away front the Welsh Chni eleven-twelfths of her property. Fortunately argument did not occur to the heathen advent./ . .</p>
        <p>the Church iv the primitive egesor the ettermia of tha Church might have proved • very easy to The good .bleb the Church may derive from diem m \ erect appears to he derived from the fret that it . • .</p>
        <p>apeciartonic. Of cOUrse the temporal involved in the loss of goods is good for everyone; I. quite as good for Noncoolcrmists as for Churc and quite as good for lawyers as for ministers 9 Gospel. Hut when those who prescribe tbe tonic no disposition to take the medicine themselves, il are wise they still steer clear of these very reflections. The reason ICC 2 is fined opus as the of disendowment is that it is a date tint cannot poi °unhinge. any endowments possessed by the Not fottniets while the whole of the Church endow;</p>
        <p>will disappear with the exreption of a twe One really is very mach at • loss to know wha principle is which governs these proposals for the &amp;cation of the property of the Welsh Church. A, veers ago 1705 was fixed es the date of disendownie/</p>
        <p>few years earlier 1,13 wan the date, now it is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>MI The earlier date giving the better protection, he thought, to Nonconformists' endowntents. But is there any principle in the matter, unless it he this—any Christian memorials previous to 1032, and any money given for religious purposes previous to that date are of no accoont and not worth preserving, or porusibly itins grievance to Nonconforroiats that it was Riven so early that they were not there Ito share it. All anßestry is looked tit askance in !these chit's, the reason being, b. supposed, that ancestry ' often carries inheritance along with it. But in this en. ha didn't see how our Nonconformist friends can I legitimately make cot a grievance. The situation hns erAirely been one of their own choice, and one of their own creation. It was the glory and pride of the Non- conformints to make new departure in religion nearly 300 years nen. They riiniiived to separate from their Christian brethren and take the conseirpiences. Are we to suppose that loran of them now look back upon the past with regret ; and that their disestisfaction is taking the form of trying to prize the property they were oupposei to have given up in compliment to their principles ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Church Lads Brigade</head>
