        <p>Heath</p>
        <p>The Church of England Men'. Society is doing inrslu- able service in building up an cement body of workers j for the edvenceraent of the Church, eanecially amongst the workingmen. Three branches of the society have been formed in Warrington and district, and to further interest in the work the Rev.Wallece H.Elltott,secretnry for the Northern Province visited Warrington hut week- end. He addressed a service for men on Sunday after- noon at St. Thorium. Church, Stockton Heath, end piesched et St. Paul's Church in the evening, On Mondny evening hews, the chief speaker at a crowded meet ing of men in the Church corn at Stockton Heath, the audience being draw, from all three branches. On Sundry at •rnnon nt St. Thomas' Church, he took as bin tent the words "lame debtor" (Rome. i. 14 j He pointed out that life was the stemly accumulation of a huge detit which could never he repaid. Enimuiri sting some of these sacred debts, he tlealt first with the lore if the child for the parente. The reason, he said, that the majority of men in this world kept straight was not her-twee they loved God or cared soothing for God—or the Ihisol—but 6.014111 e they loved their mothers. Mr. Elliott also tooched upon the debts which the parent owed to the child. anti which a man owed to lain friends. But the greatest debt of all was the debt which tie whole wiirld owed to Jesus Christ and Vint debt included all the other three debts of which he had spoken. It can the influent.. of Jesus Christ working through the national life end character which bud made for POOOlO the honourable position which ahe occopied in the home of to-they. If Christ hid never lived on eerth borne life such as we now enjoyed wi old newer hero been ponible. If Chriat bed not corne to reveal the Lahr rhood of God we nhould never here known the brotherhood of man, and without the brotherhool if 111.10 there mold by 110 each thing en Os sacred friendship which made life worth li ring Alluding to the debt which we owed to Christ as a Te cher, Mr. Elliott said that when we looked Mick upon the putt 19011 years every movement for social progress, the uplifting of the mos.,. and th• making of life more livenble must be traced to the marvellous teaching of Jestta Christ. We could never repay the debt which we owed to Christ, hot we could show our grittitude by eacriflce nod service. "‘</p>
        <p>At the raeet;ng. on Morality evening, Mr. Elliott out- lined the sin, of tbesociety, explaining them in a very lucid manner. The trek they had set themselves to do, he mid. woe a,ery difflattlx one. They Wyre trying to introduce principles which had leesforgotton for many years—ttiey were, in short, trying to rep's., selfishness by • spirit of sacrifice. They w•re trying to make men abandon • life of selfeervice and instead endeavour to Lerre others. They were trying to break down that greet wall of indifference within the Church as cell as outside the Church—that indifference that existed amongst Christian peoples. well as among non- Christians nod which bed prevented Christ from coming to His own. And for Ibis work they, in the C EMS.. were trying to get labourers to go out into the high- ways and bywnys end compel wren to come in, that the Church of God might be tilled. With such aims they must expect difficulties, for no great work could be considered a great work without diffiooltiea. Continu- ing, the rev, gentlemen maid then the work they were doing was the work Christ came to do. The hattl• wie thn battle Christ came to fight. The course they</p>
