        <head>Brick street Nissen</head>
        <p>Brick street Nissen</p>
        <p>At the shove place of worship on Sunday services on behalf of Foreign Minions Isere held. Mr. Anock was planned es preacher, but owing to illness he could ' not be present on this occasion, hot he stringed with Mr. Thornton. of Asliton-under.Lyne, to take the services all dey. Mr. Thornton gave niuch 'Mumble information es to how the work is carried on in foreign nations end emphasised the necessity for all the help that can he given to such n noble cerise as spreading the Gospel truth arnong.M the heathen people.</p>
        <p>Rev. Owen S. Watkins at Bold-street. One of the most intereeting lectures that has ever been delivered in Bold-street Wesleyan Cuirch was that by the Rev. Owen Spencer Watkins on Thursday even- ing. Mr Edeard Wallington presided and in the rostrum besides the lecturer nere the Rev W Seed and Mr J Holland. Mr Watkins took ea his subject" With Kitchener to Khartoum " and gave his exper:encee as chaplain in the British and Egytion expedition of 'Slit Ile described very vividly his journey from Malta to Kliertoum where Gordan's death was retrieved and through which the tyranny of the Der- •iehere won put at an end. The compaignnrnsentrem.iy difficult not only on account sf the activity end the great fighting 113I1LiOCIII of the Dervishese, but owing Ito the scorching heat which the Egyptian and British , forces had to contend with. On many occasions the temperature was no less thorn 117 degrees in the shade and if the soldiers happened to tabs their helmets off they .Iropped down unconscioue.. it etruck by ride bullets. A battle in which 20.000 Britishers were engaged with flee or six times as many of the enemy was imaginatively described. win also toe onslaught upon Kheirtourn itself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church</p>
        <p>Preaching in the Bewee)-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening, the Ile, G. H. Keeling took as his teat" That they Icy bold on the life which is life indeed." Ile said it wee natural to lose one's temper to be greedy, to be sellish and to be false. If they only got beck to the simple life and got rid of civiliration and it. erceutricites borne thought all would be right. All would not he right, it would be all wrong. t% hat would happen vroisid be just the come as when they left the best rote clone—it went back to the old will rose. There we. the some tendency in or. It was not natural to be good, noble, unsel6o. It weu not natural for roan to hare a beautiful character, the nature went the other war. Let them follow inclination blindly and they would know what would happen. "To enter in at the narrow door" there were certain preparations necessary. " Not highmlnded." There was always the tenderer if they got on well in their business enterprises or bad been well educated,l for theni to look down upon their fellows. It natural to be bed, it was alum. inevitable. Flow could they help it ? " Don't do it " said the New Testament. They should not measure by what some- body else bad done, hut by the standard of the Gospel itself. The life that was life indeed most have some- thing of the Cross in it, something of the Saviour's yoke,eornethina of the Savionit burdens and something of the Saviour a toil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S A.</p>
        <p>There was a fair attendance on Suo-kley,Mr. 0. Smiley presiding. fr.. E. Lloyd Jones gave the seldrees opera " When be carne unto himself." T:.e prodigal by degrees went triton had to wore°. He left the path of Shill affection nod aellishoess took possession of him. The world for a time fevoureel hint, but when the trouble, came, which were unsurromintable, it drought him to himself. From that moment there we• • ray of hope. He remembered home, plenty nod sunshine. To young men who woold trend and think, there was • grand opportunity tn return to all goodoosa, God and wake. The Secretary rept the story of the Prodigal Son, Hr. 1. Johnson led the prityor. Mr. P.O. &amp;Hey sang" The SOW./ went forth sowing" and "Come unto Me," which wore much eppreciste ' Mr. WM. Peyton, Mits. Sao., presided at the organ. ,nree neer members received a hearty welcome.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. On Sunday hat the pulpit at St. John's was occupied morninn and evening by the Rev. J. Fi. C. Macaulay, M.A., Moderator of Presbyterr. In the evening the church area well filled, end Mr. 'Macaulay took for his subject the biogrephy of John Mark, epeaking tram the text "And he Peter came to the Ilona° of Mary. the mother of John, whose anthems was Mark." (Acts, 12c.. 12v.. They didn't know roach, said the preacher of John Mirk, but they know enough to till in a picture of useful bionrephy, tinl there xa. no more useful study than biography. The first chapter in the life of Mark showed the kill,' of home he an brought up in. It we. wealthy home, with an atmoaphere that was spiritual. After his father's death he had resourcea of wealth at hi. disposal In him mother's housv prayer was contino• sily made for the christian church. The house became the centre of early persecution, and Mark was drawn into tho discipleship. It was a fine thing to have • spiritual ancestry, and they neede.l to bring up their soling people tea religious atmosphere in the bone, in the school and in the church. The next chapter in Merit's life was a sad one. Ile had splendid opportuni- ties of learning under Pant and Karnali., but he refused to go with Paul over the mountains Into Asia Minor It was mote than deee,tho ; it broke the leadership of the church and divided Barnithem and Paul. Ilot desertion did not mean ruin, and there was the chapter of restoratioo. It took • long time to do it. A line like Paul could not be played fast end loots with for nothing, and they could not pley fast and loot. with Christ or with the clohrcb. For Mark, the re-eetaliliali- ment of chsrecter meant hard work, patience, endurance and the reviving of that home influence end prayer, hu: he accomplished it and that smut how he became a great soul. M•rk won his battle on the field where he had loat it The last chapter war th• grecs and glory of it all. That young man who had refused to go over the mountain. with Paul, penetrated further than or Peter or any of them, and he wrote the 'Rory of th• his lard. They could not all do that but they could do better in a tient.; they could be living epistle, known •nd read of all men, and the most realistic preeentation of Christ was just the preaentation of faithful follower. and believere: . _ . .</p>
        <p>On Mo.lay even log M. Mscaulay delivered a loot on at. the church, under the 111.1,1 CPS of the St. John'. Guild. The subject max Celtic Characteriatica." l'here was a large attendance, snd the lecture Tao highly intereating end instructive. On the motion of the Rev. William Reid, oho occupied the chair. cordial vote of thanlis was accorde.l the leorscd find gifted lecturer.</p>
