      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB</p>
        <p>CANDLEMAS DAY</p>
        <p>It is very late to write about Candlemas Day because it occurs on February 2nd and we have now come to February 11th, but it would be a pity not to say a word about an interesting celebration. We have all read about Jesus being taken to the Temple by his mother. It was the custom for mothers to take their tiny babies to the Temple and when Mary carried Jesus there the aged Simeon took him in his arms and said :</p>
        <p>Wrest thou Thy servant depart in peace, according to Thy word: For mine eyes have seen Thy salvation, . .</p>
        <p>Which The'e haat prepartA --before the face of all people; A light to lighten the Gentiles, and the glory of Thy people farad." These were beautiful words, and the day has been celebrated and old Simeon's words repeated and sung in the Churches on what is called "Candlemas Day." The day was so called because the clergy used to provide candles arrl the people walked round with them, the candles being a referenceito the words "a light o lighten the gehtiles." They took home Ithe pieces of candle which were not bur Ni and in superstitious days used to believes that there was some supernatural value in t em. One of the old poets described landlernas like this: t</p>
        <p>Thin done, each man bin candle ligc.s, Where chief test eeemeth he Whose taper greatest umy be wen ; And fortunate to be, Whose candle burueth clear and bright. A wondrous force and might Doth in these candles lie, which, if Atany time they light, They sure believe that neither storm Nor tempest doth abide, Nor thunder in the skies be heard, Nor any devils spied, Nor fearful sprites that walk by night, „ Nor hurts of frost Or hall. Candlemas Day is also supposed to be the weather-maker for the year. For centuries the superstitious have believed that good weather on Candlemas Day means a long, cold winter and a bad crop; but a stormy, foul day is, on the other hand, a good omen. According to the old Scotch rhyme,</p>
        <p>"If Candlemas Day be dry and fair, The half of winter's to come anti mair ; If Candlemas Day be wet and foul, The half o' winter's gane at Yule.</p>
        <p>Many an American farmer boy watches to see whether the woodchuck leaves his hole on the second of February, believing that if he does, and the sun shines upon him so as to cast a shadow, he draws back immediately into his burrow, knowing that the winter is not nearly over ; but if he finds snow on the ground, and a dull, cloudy day, he stays out, knowing that winter is almost over. In Germany the badger takes the place of the woodchuck as a weather-foreteller on Candlemas Dayoandlin a German proverb we are told that a shepherd would rather see the wolf enter his stable on Candlemas Day than the sun.</p>
        <p>LITTLE KINDNESSES.</p>
        <p>Yo? ?nen on .the way a pleaaant mile</p>
        <p>And thought no inor-e 'about itT— R cheered a life that was sad the while That might have been wrecked without it ; And no for the mile and its fruitage fair You'll reap a crown some time—somewhere. Yon spoke one day a cheering word, And passed to other duties; It warmed a heart, new eremite stirred, And painted a life with beauties. And no for the word and its silent prayer You'll reap a palm some time—somewhere.</p>
        <p>You lent a hand to a fallen one, A lift in kindnese given; It saved a soul when help MEW none, And won a heart for heaven ; And no for the helpyou proffered there You'll reap a joy some time —somewhere. n. o.</p>
        <p>GOOD FOR THE BOYS. A crowd of boys were gathered round the entrance of a circus in a country town,trying to get a glimpse of the interior. A stranger standing near watched them for a few moments, then walking up to the doorkeeper said "Lot all these boys in and count them as they pass." "Twenty-eight, sir," said the official when the last one had been admitted. "Good," said the stranger as he turned and walked off. "I guessed the number exactly ! "</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND RUNCORN CO•OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E. Dudley presided over the balLyearly meet- ing of the shove aticietv, in Lacey-street echoolroone last Monday evening. The report mid there were now 9,500 memtiers, the trade total. £114,369, being an inorease of .C 36 on the last half-year. There wee • net return of £15,977, end. dividend of 2s. 10d. has been declared. The society was never in • better position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WARRINGTON ---5 CHAPTERS. THE BATTLE OF LAND AND SEA.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ---5 CHAPTERS. THE BATTLE OF LAND AND SEA.</p>
        <p>A CHAPTER ON LOCAL CHANGES.</p>
        <p>Among the branches of geological study there is none more easy to enter upon, or more full of interest, than the observation of our coast changes, or, in the words we have borrowed for our title, the battle between land and sea. That such observa- tion takes us to the seaside and that it may form the excuse for many pleasant holidays is of course a detail, but it is a detail that may be allowed to have its full weight in the choice of a summer hobby. In preparation for the bright (lays which will soon be here we propose to write upon some of the interesting things that may be studied within the range of a short journey from Warrington. It needs but a slight acquaintance with the coasts of Lancashire and Cheshire to discover to us that changes are in progress. Southport with a mile of sand before its doors knows this full well, and to the Mersey Docks and Harbour Board whose huge dredgers fight the sand banks day and night, the battle is a grim reality. It is still an open question whether our much-admired</p>
        <p>MAI' OF LANCASH Showing the coast lino (dark line) as ,</p>
        <p>Liverpool will be silted up with sand as were the ports of Chester, Neston, Shotwick, Formby and Parkside ; and in another Igeneration Southport may be an inland town with nothing but it name to proclaim that it I once stood at the water edge.</p>
        <p>It all depends how things go in this big battle between land and sea.</p>
        <p>The sea commonly gets the best of it, but the land makes effective answer by piling up where it can sand hills and sand plains. On the mouth of the Mersey the struggle has been more than a skirmish; it has been a pitched battle, fierce and atrong, that began before the dawn of history and continues day by day, a batttle. too, that has given so many surprises and taken so many unexpected turns that no one dare venture a prediction of the end.</p>
        <p>NE AND N. WALES.</p>
        <p>riven by Ptolemy in the second century,</p>
        <p>off the lawgh water marke, and also oute of the bodye of the said countye."</p>
        <p>, In modern parlance, that land wag "off the map." Sir Henry Halesll was able to prove that no one had mean it for eighty years and he won his case.</p>
        <p>Formby up to 1705 was s good port, and in 1700 it became a subject of discussion whether the docks contemplated for Liver- pool should he built there, but before 1740 it was left high and dry, and now even the site of the pier and harbour cannot be ascer- tained.</p>
        <p>These are but a few among many starting changes, a theory for which we will advance by and by.</p>
