      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>SUBMERGED WESTERN LANCASHIRE</head>
        <p>SUBMERGED WESTERN LANCASHIRE</p>
        <p>But before making a discussion of the Mersey, let 118 glance down the coast of Lancashire, taking note of a few familiar places. Tim was when, what is now Blackpool, was joined to Walney Island and in the grout's' inundation countless villages, some of them mentioned in the Domesday Book, have passed from sight The visitor to I</p>
        <p>MAP OF IkE Showing t</p>
        <p>Cleveleys may, at low water, walk Inspect the tree roots and other relics' forests that grew there, and under the the wrecks of old homesteads have been stumbled upon by diggers. Last year at a very low tide a party Blackpool gentlemen walked out in search' what is called the Penny Stone. The sto is a full mile out at sea, due west of TT Tom's cabin. It is the post of wayside inn, and the iron rini horses were tethered still remain</p>
        <p>Rit to pf the</p>
        <p>'hat w. to</p>
        <p>But the land at Blackpool ha rally; and from the south end s like those at Southport are slow' themselves northward. CHANGES AT SOUTHPORT Of the great changes at Southporl will need to be told, and we mention some facts which are not known.</p>
        <p>An interesting indication of th line is to be seen at Churchtown an Irish vessel was driven as wrecked. She was laden with from this fact the bit of coast I locally Sugar Hillock. This spc covered by Sunny Road, awl is a half inland from the present sea vessel, it may be mentioned, carrb potatoes which ever entered Curiosity caused the natives some of them, and incidentally w hamlet of Marsheide the honour ol first to grow potatoes. Going back a century only w</p>
        <p>[RRAL PENINSULA.</p>
        <p>Lt course of Mersey.</p>
        <p>to the period covered br human records. PTOLEMY Et MAP. The earliest known map of Britain was pre- pared in the second century by the Roman geographer Ptolemy. No original drawings of his have survived, and the copies have not always been carefully done, but they have I sufficient likeness to show in general out- line the results of Ptolemy'll survey. In the tion of the corner of England in which post interested he presents us with a puzzle. The estuary of the Dee is narked and also another estuary , but whether this is meant for the or the Ribble has been a point of keen ersy. If it be the Ribble then there is sey, if it be the Mersey there is no It will be noticed too that he 10 Island of Anglesey and that his le would leave the Great Omie's head land mountain and the Menai Straits ad fields. rn research has gone to show that r was much more accurate than his itators have supposed. It is now conclusively proved that in Roman me was no island of Anglesey, and le Orme's head looked over smiling o a distant sea. /as Ptolemy right in his coast line of ire? And it so which was the miss-</p>
        <p>Idelineat ye are it -et rate</p>
        <p>a careful survey of the evidence we nclude that he was right, and that sing estuary is that of the Mersey.</p>
