      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>MOUTH OF THE MERSEY.</head>
        <p>THE MOUTH OF THE MERSEY.</p>
        <p>Geologists have much to settle concerning the ancient history of the Mersey. They are agreed, we think, that in glacial days it ran the contrary way to which its waters now flow and joined the Trent or Severn,and there seems to be evidence of this in the fact that Wallasey, Bromborough and Ditton brooks flow in a direction contrary to that of the river they join. But we are not con- cerned here with problems HO remote as those of the glacial age.We confine ourselves</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PIPPIROMMINMMINUMNION7I</head>
        <p>PIPPIROMMINMMINUMNION7I</p>
        <p>period of great change. Fishermen caught shrimps on the site of Lord-street and up to 1849 there was a boat building yard °tithe site of Trinity Hall where boats could be launched upon a stream called the Nile. The mouth of this stream formed the " port " which gave name to the town and it is said that a century ago it enjoyed eleven fathoms of water. As the town grew and coal was ' needed it was brought up the Nile, the flats travelling from Wigan by way of the river Douglas and entering Southport from the sea. There are those still living who travelled thus as excursionists to Southport and many more who reached the sea port by a canal journey from Wigan to Ormskirk and thence by road. Lest there should be enquiries after South- port's lost river, we may record that it grew discouraged at the aggressive sand banks and in 1876 all that was left of its stream was turned into the Lord-street sewer.</p>
        <p>LOST LANDS AT BIRKDALE</p>
        <p>In rearing itself on the Lancashire coast the land is but endeavouring to lx. avenged for centuries of ravage and defeat. South of the now famous resort several villages lie submerged and the sands cover the relics of many a foros4and farm. The fifteenth century seems to have been is specially destructive one upon this coast and among the papers in the Record Office is a Court document that records an action by the King for rent of lands at Halsall, Rirkdale. The defence put in by the tenant is that he declines to pay rent for land which is not there, and which he never saw, but which was, to quote his document, "within the high see and drowned and anhichilate with the irayd see, and oute</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>mminigiuppeouli</head>
        <p>mminigiuppeouli</p>
        <p>the thapry is offered that the Mersey was originally a tributary of the Dee and that it flowed acmes the Wirral peninsular to a point below Chester. According to his belief the old city would stand upon the point where the two streams joined and be pro- tected on two sides by them. I Every discovery goes to confirm the theory. The ancient channel of the Mersey can be easily traced; it is the flat valley along which the Ellesmere Canah was made a century ago, Turning tit nomenclature the Welsh and Roman names of the Dee both bear the mean- ing "two streams." The Welsh was " dwy" or " dwfr," meaning' two waters," and the Roman Scteia means a "couple of streams." The name' Meirse " is Celtic for marsh and seems to point to a time when the stream tas associated with marshy places..; but no ffoman historian mentions it. With tMipicture in mind of the magnifi- cent stream that sweeps between Liverpool and Birkenhead it is difficult to give form to the other picture and see a far spreading forest reaching from the Dee to Southport broken by the two hills on which Everton and Birkenhead now stand. Yet such was the geography of what is now the Mersey estuary. There was only a fresh water pool round which the Liver birds hatched their young where now the " Mauritania" rides at anchor in the chief vestibule of Britain. I To-day an inland sea spreads from ,Ituncorn I to Eastliam, and its waters, as the tide falls, scour out the channel that forms the port of Liverpool.</p>
        <p>Only one theory can explain this trans- formation, and it is that this corner of the world has experienced a striking subsidence. By the falling of the land the sea has evidently met the Mersey and given it an outlet of its own, flooding inland to Runcorn. How many homes and villages lie under the Irish sea we shall probably never know, but we know of at least one fair palace that sleeps under the waves. Of this and many !other relics of the great subsidence we shall tell in another article. (The blocks illustrating this article are kindly lent by Mr. William Ashton and are taken from his valuable little book "The Battle of Land and Sea," (1/6).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>After Fred Karne'a nketch "The 0.P.0." which was at the Palace a few weeks ago his ;absurdity "The Footliall Match," which is the chief attraction this week, seems quite sedate in comparison. The turn appears to be a magnified game of " pihonting in," and it " goes down" tremendourdy with the ripening net:thin of the audience. It is certainly a little dis- appointing, after our experience of Fred Karno 11134 lon humeer, to meet with a company no loliberate ' and serious. the one which undertaken "The Foot- ball Match." All the name we have Karoo humour above the ordinary merit, and amusing incidents nil lib while the company occupy the boards. The ' fun of the sketch centres; on two teams, Midnight Wanderers and Middleton Piecans, and as the names Mini"' there ins good deal of what is known in foot- ball parlance, as ittestionable play. On the whole the fame ix quite amusing. There in another clever "turn " this week.in "I laley's Juveniles," who provide a vocal neena winch ia pretty. novel :ma exceedingly attnrelve. The jtiveniles t °Deist of twenty highly- train children who coot, Orme ramie duets, songs and dances with pleaning effect. The youngsters include " lot" and "Spot," who are ntated to be "the smallest duettists before the public." In addition to the above two turns there is an excellent programme. The Vitality Quartette nf male vide. in " tiemn of vocal hirmony " show themselves to 110 vac-abeta of no mean merit. Rosie Archer, a I 'lever mimic at the piano, impersonatee several lea,fina "edam" of the vaudeville world. Captain Kettle, coin and card comedian; Snowball and Italia, singers and dancers; and Ada liatley, in a pot-pourri speciality are all nnistee worthy of mention. " Perfec- tion " picture. are ',hewn, and the orchentm eontribute some high-duos music under the direction of Mr. Widdop.</p>
      </div>
