      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PROFESSOR SCHPSON ON PLAGUE,</head>
        <p>PROFESSOR SCHPSON ON PLAGUE,</p>
        <p>The "blank death." which bad not visited Seemed for two hundred and dfty pars, has eluded the •igileace of the sanitary authorities, and threatens to become once more endemic.' The Local Government hoard is not inactive in this emergency. It, employing certain Inscteriologistii for the examination of anima* end Professor Simpson suggests that they" 'Mould publish weekly the kind of animater...Dined and their number, the locslitiee from which the anireol. were taken, and the number in each locality which wen found infected." It in, emetically speaking, imperative that thin should I he done, otherwise no one who in taking proceeding. I against the tat will know whether he in only dealing with • common nuisance or with a possible carrier at plague infeetiers.—" Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
