      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>{ OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>{ OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>(ALL RIGHT. RUNEVIIIII I THE MYSTERY OF ROGER BULLOCK,</p>
        <p>• TOM GALLON (Asthor of " Tetterley," "Meg the lady.",</p>
        <p>CRAPTZE xin.</p>
        <p>TIM ZND OF MI GANZ.</p>
        <p>Pacing up and down in the rain of • Windy, gusty morning outside the 'nailer- man'e Arms went Lydia Pardon, patiently waiting until such time as she could have epeech with Mr. Robert Withers. In what fashion she had got through the night it is impossible to say ; but there was about her a certain air of neatness, despite the fact that she had not been to bed. This was, in a sense, the day on which all things were to . happen to her—the day on which her vengeance wan to be completed. In due course Mr. Robert Withers, from an upper window, saw her, and instantly came running down, lie took her up into a raom he had engaged for greater privacy, and there, with Mark Connaway, heard the results of her night's work, and heard, too, that amazing message she had delivered to Neal Jarrow.</p>
        <p>"Do you think he'll go ?" asked Withers quickly. "Go ?" she laughed derinivelv. "Of course he'll go. At the present time that man in enduring torments ; he doesn't know what it is that is walking the earth in tho likeness of Roger Bullock ; in his half craz- ed brain he doesn't really know even what has become of the dead man. 110 will go back to the mill, with the feeling that it is just possible he may there see again Roger Bullock—just as he left him—or the spirit of him, accusing him." "So much the better," said the little fisherman, rubbing his hands. "If he comes to that place at that time, 'ee'll see tiuch a night as even ho little expects. Sit down and have some breakfast," he went on hoepitab- ly ; "there is work before us all to-day." Fortunately for the nucceas of their plans, Neal Jarrow did not venture that day near the town of Weathergate, or if he did, no one saw him. In due course a mesnage dash- ed over the wires to Richard Bullock in Lon- don, and was answered instantly ; a little later in the day Mr. Withers went to the little railway station, to meet an incoming train, out of which stepped Richard Bullock. He was almost in a mood for laughter when he new Mr. Withers standing with a finger on his lips, regarding him mysteriously; at. moat annoyed when presently Withers in. mated on making a detour through back lanes and alleys in order to reach the Fisher- man's Arms. One of the first questions asked by Withers was about Madeline. " Madeline is oafs and well ; only anxious about that young man in whom she takee so deep an interest, • answered Richard. "I mean to interview our friend Roach to-day ; I want to get from him some account of the girl's fortune. By the way," he a ked— ' why ore you smuggling me into thojplaee in this mysterious way "Because I don't want you to be wen ; because I don't want anyone to know that the supposed Roger Bullock is walking about, alive and well," answered Withers. If only you'll put yourself into my hands to-dav, all will be well. But, once inside the Finherman's Arms, you must not stir out until dark."</p>
        <p>They came to that room wherein Mark Connaway and Lydia were ; Mark stood up. with worda of apology upon his lips that ho should be there at all.</p>
        <p>"I didn't expect or hope to see you again, Mr. Bullock," he said—' but I call you to witness that lam not here willingly. Mr. :Withers believes that something can be done towards the clearing up the mystery of your brother's death ; I came here at his request. Personally, I don't believe that anything can be done, and in any case I promise you that after to-day you will see</p>
        <p>"Don't be too sure of that, my boy," said Richard Bullock, with a laugh. "I have someone else to think of, and I don't think we can let you go quite 30 lightly as that. There—give me your hand," went on the big man, etretching out hie own—"in my own mind I know that you are an innocent man. Perhaps to-day we may be able to prove that ; but whether it in proved or not will make no difference to me." " You found Madeline ?" "Yes—l found her," answered the other, with a queer look at him. "She gave me a message for you—but I shan't deliver that message at present. It must wait." Mr. Withers, stage-managing the little drama he had prepared, arranged that they were all to meet near 06 mill at a certain hour after darkness had set in that night. In response to many questions he declined to tell them anything ; he simply urged up- on them to be at the place at the time he had stated, and to keep am much or possible out of sight of anyone who might by chance be there. Towards evening the little man disappeared, without Baying a word to any- owe as to where he was going. As soon as it grew dark, and while there W3R yet some considerable time before them. before keeping the appointment, Richard Bullock, in an agony of impatience, suggest- ed to Mark that they should have a walk through the old town. It had occurred to him that it might be well if he put in an op- pearance at the house of Sabre Roach, the better to let that gentleman understand that he was still alert, and ready to face him on that coming matter of business. So the two men strolled away together, and presently came to the house of Sebes Roach to find it. as we know, shuttered and locked up and silent. Richard Bullock tugged at the bell several times, but could get no respon. ; and he was just turning away, when from out of the shadows on the opposite side of the street the figure of a woman glided, and approached them hesitatingly. It was Susan Roach ; Mark Connaway re- cognised her at once. She looked at them in a frightened fashion, and raked in a whisper what they wanted. "I want to see Mr. Sebes Roach," said Richard Bullock. -.Where is he ?"</p>
        <p>" flee gone, said the woman. without looking at them. " Ile went—last night I'm his wife."</p>
        <p>- "Where has he gone ?" "I don't rightly know," said Susan, in the same curious whimper. "But he's not coming back." Richard Bullock gave a long whistle, and looked aticidark. "1 guessed as much," he said "I thought he wouldn't face me." "This gentleman," Mark explained to the woman, "so Miss Madeline's guardian, and he had made arrangements to talk to your husband about the fortune left in Mr. Roach's hands for the girl. I suppose you know nothing about that The woman drew a key from her pocket, and put it in the lock of the door, and led the way into the house. She beckoned to the two men to come in, without a word, and having closed the door, got a light, and, Mit without speaking, conducted them up to that disordered room on the first floor. She threw open the door of it, and pointed to the old portmanteau and the bundles of papers in it. Then she spoke for the first time in a hard voice. "Don't be no ready to blame people foe what they haven't done," she said. "Roach wasn't one of the best, and there'll be Venty to say hard thing. about him ; per. aps he didn't treat me ton well. But he didn't steal Madeline's fortune, after all. !Mere it is; look for yourself." On hia knees beside the old portmanteau Stichard pulled out one by one the bundles</p>
        <p>of securities; nodded his head over them in some amazement. "It looks almost as if he had been packing them to take with him, and had repented of his purpose," he said. "And you don't know where he's gone ?" he added, looking up at Susan. " I don't, know," she said sullenly. "If you'll put all those things together, and take them with you, I'll lock up the house. - When everything was completed and Rich- ard Bullock had stuffed his pockets with the papers, she let them out of the door into the street, and locked the door for the last time. Then for the last time she turned away, and went through the little town, and out into the open country, making for some destination known only to herself, or per- haps not known at that time at all. And so she passes out of this history. The two men returned to the inn, while Richard Bullock placed those securities, so strangely recovered, in the bag he had brought from London. It being now near- ly the time when they were to meet Withers at the mill, they bet out together to cross that dreary track of marshes that has be- tween the Fisherman's Arms and the 'Ferry House.</p>
        <p>_ "1 wonder what that amiable lithe mad- man, Robert Withers, means to do with us?" said Richard Bullock: as he strolled along smoking a cigar. "lie's one of those curious fellows who may by chance do something great, but is far more likely to do some- thing ridiculous. However, we're in his hands, and we must make the beet of it, I "Ilremycame to the old mill at last, and found the door of it swinging open. Not without some natural hesitation they wert in, and climbed the stairs to that room Mark remembered no well.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile one unhappy man had hod the mental picture of the old mill before him through a long night. Over and over again, after the woman had left him, Neal Jarrow repeated to himself that message he had heard—that mad, impossible message corn- ing from the dead—that Roger Bullock would meet him in the old mill at ten o'clock at night ! It seemed ot last to the haunted man that the whole world was filled with that message ; that the river, winding along between its banks, murmured it to him ; that it rustled in the trees ; that strange shapes sprang up at him out of the mists, whispering awfully before they van- ished that Roger Bullock would wait for him in the place where lie had died. More than once, in horror of the mere thought of meeting him, Neal Jarrow strode away, with the intention to get as far as ponsible from the mill ; but always he came back, like on uneasy ghost, until once again the old Ferry House loomed before him. The night passed, and another day, and there came the night again. By this time the man had lost count of time and place and everything el. ; his world had narrow- ed down to that gaunt half-ruined structure in which lie had killed Roger Bullock. Ile staggered like a drunken man over the marshes ; now telling himself that if he could by any lucky chance find a certain Mark Connaway, he himself was safe ; now coming, as it seemed, upon that Con- naway, and struggling with him, and losing him ; and so beginning all the horrible busi- ness over again. And all the time drifting nearer and nearer to the old mill. . .</p>
        <p>..... Ile had got to that condition that he could not remember the dates and times of events. Was it yesterday that he had come down here with Boger Bullock, with that purpose in his mind to kill him ? How long ago was it that he had met a poor starving wretch upon the road, and with a plausible ' tale had brought him into the place, and fed him, and made him drunk, and left him I drugged and helpless with a dead man ? ! Was it to-night that he had come out of the I mill—to creep back in a few minutes, with I the design to wake Mark Connaway, and show him that dead thing hidden by the couch ?</p>
        <p>He could not tell—he did not know. All that dinned through hia brain now was that message trumpet-tongued that Roger Bullock waited for him in the old mill, and was not to be denied.</p>
        <p>He came at laid to the door of the place, and stood there, hesitating a long time. A bright moon wile shining; all was very atilt and peaceful. Ile thrust open the door, with no feeling of surprise now that it should not be fastened ; with a hand against the wall to guide him, he went up in the dark. nese until he reached the door of that room in which, if the message was correct, he must find Roger Bullock. Breathing heavi- ly, he leaned for a moment or two against the lintel of it ; then at last thrust it open, and stood there, looking in. It was very strange—but the room was aa he had remembered it. On the table burn- ed a candle; beside it was a bottle overturn- ed. Cards were scattered on the floor, and at the end of the table was roughly chalked out some squares for a game they had been playing. He stood just within the room, striving to collect his thoughts—trying hard to un- derstand. He was groping nearer to the truth every minute ; he knew now that all the rest had been a dream—and that he vas back again to that night when he had xtruck down Roger Bullock. What was that figure seated at the window, with arms resting on the ledge of it, and with bead cradled on the arms ?</p>
        <p>Hest°le nearer, and touched the figure on the shoulder ; but Mark Connaway did not move. Ile looked next at the couch, where it had been dragged from its place to cover something, and had dragged the carpet with it. He would not go near that yet he knew too well what was beneath it. He was faint and tired; he should have more cour- age presently, when it came to the time to wake the man at the window, and to aCCIIBO him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>some vague fashion he saw through tea trick, will never be known; suffice it that, he sprang straight for the window, and reached it before anyone could seise him. For • moment he poised himself upon the sill outside, looking in upon Richard Bul- lock ; then, with a scream, turned round— stumbled—and fell headlong. As he fell he struck the mill wheel ; and then turning Iver and over, fell into the river. Three men rushed out of that room, and aln),,,t tumbled over each other down the ; but they were too late. Though they ran along the aide of the bank, and al- though a woman who had aprung up from nowhere, ea it seemed, out of the darkneas, ran with them, they saw nothing and heard nothing. No arm upraised out of the dark water—no cry for help borne on the night wind.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Withers cline back, hatles9 and panting, to the mill ; there he met Richard Bullock and Mark Connaway. " I had a sort of feeling that in the end he would do me ; I knew he would slip through my fingers,'' said the little fisherman. 'lt was a close thing—but he got the best of it in the end—didn't he ,"</p>
        <p>" He's gone to his punishment," RUM Richard Bullock solemnly.</p>
        <p>They walked back together to the Fisher. man's Arms. On the way Richard Bullock dropped a hand on Mark's shoulder, and spoke to him in a low tone that could not be overheard by Withers. "I go to London in the morning—and you'll come with me. I can give you her message now. She sent you her dear love— to the boy who was her sweetheart and tho man who is her lover. The three of es, Mark, will have to start the world afresh— in another country ; Roger Bullock will go a wandering. To-morrow, dear boy, we ge to London—to find Madeline."</p>
        <p>[Tsz Enn.]</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 11, 1911.</p>
        <p>'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>He got to the table, and poured some spiritinto a glum, and drank. That seemed to send fire leaping through his brain ; he was ready for anything. He had made a movement towards the couch when he stopped, and drew back with a muffled shriek. God of mercy !—the couch wad moving ! If what was beneath it should by any chance stretch a hand out, or crawl out there—alive and horrible—what should he do ?It had been fancy dead men did not stir like that.</p>
        <p>But while he looked the couch stirred again, and he saw a white hand come out slowly upon the floor. Then, while he held to the table, and wondered how 140011 this thing that was beating in his throat would burst and give him relief, the conch was overturned with a crash, and the thing be- neath it rove suddenly to its feet. Neal, Jarrow fell upon his knees, and covered h face with his hands, and bowed himself the floor.</p>
        <p>"I killed you Roger—but von have me a hundred times eince,' he im " Tell me what I shall do in penance from me the burden of this thing more than I can bear. Is there no g which you will rest ?" 'Answer toe, Neal Jarrow—as yoi one day answer your God !" said Bullock sternly. You brought ayf in here, meaning to make him a r for yourself ; had he any hand in in of me ?" 'You know he had none," said still keeping his face hidden. ". through it all ; slept while you am tied in this room before I struck ye You made a fight for your life if I meant to kill you I neve: could hi</p>
        <p>Mark, lip longer able to controli had sprung up from his pouition at dow ; Jarrow, in perlrtity, wean his feet. At the same time Richard' advanced towards him, meaning ' him ; and the door of the inner ro, cd, and Withers ran out. But whetl row believed that the spirit of the de was advancing upon hims or wile!</p>
        <p>On Thoughtlees floseim—Of the domestic life of the King and Queen, en idyll might be written. There in In the land no happier marriage, no better example of the highest ideals in family life, and no fair-minded Fenton ever gave p second thought to the disgraceful story which ha been current scandal for nearly twenty year., and was only finally despot.' of in the High Court last etwk. There have always been people ready to weave scandals or romancer shout theme on or near to the Throne, and it must he admitted that in fernier times there was ample material upon which to found such stories. To go no further back than the House of Hanover the family life of the Georges Mb far from being wholly admireble, end the lives of the lint two were openly scandal... All this was changed when Queen Victor's earns to the throne, end her marriage with Prince Albert was one of the happiest whilst her private life could have afforded no higher conception of everything that woe witimnble. Happily ere have a similar example in the home life of our present King and Queen, and the case lest week should prove a lesson to those people who thoughtlesely repeat ecandals of which they"have private information "or which they " have heard on the best authority," concerning persons in • high station. The Game. Girl—in a letter on " School., Games, and Neursetheois " • medial correspondent of the" Lancet" seems to think that the modern girl is suffering from too much athletics, 'end protests against the practice of girls in the public and high wheels, being driven compulsorily into the playing fields immediately class work is over. Lately the games girl hen come in for moch of the criticism that has been bestowed upon the" mudded corn,' and what with her indulgence in Badminton hockey, Swedish drill, and on she is said to be acquiring a certain gaucherie doe to a mannish gait and angularity of figure. Some alarmists have even gone so far as to nay that before long women will acquire such mannish figures as to look ridiculone in feminine attire. That,howerer, if it ever comes shout, is a very long way off yet, and Dr. hlery Scharlieb, in lecturing recently on this subject, recommended dancfng as the hest exercise to counter- act the resulte of enemies indulgence in gymnastics ; a remedy which meet gins find to lie an agreeable one. The Harem-scarem Skirt.—Paris has been flirting for some time peat with the ides of a divided feminine garment; but the pictures of the new harem dirt which have appeared in some of the London papers, will not help to reek° it popular. Its very some 00g. frets what it is, a sort of mongrel adaptation of the garment worn by Turkish women, and although it is described as being "sensible, hygienic, graceful, and unrivalled for comfort and elegance," it it far from preneseing all these advantages. More than anything else, it resembles the °oedema of the circus clown which is intended to look grotesque and sheen', and hy no possible stretch of the imagination could it be called a thing of beauty and elegance. Fashion has gone to many extremes from the days of the crinoline to those of the hobble skirt, but it would lo ridicule.s to speak of the fresh ass rein fashion. If it survives Its birth it will be known es the harem-ecarem skirt, but it Is doubtful if it will reach the christening stage of its brief and inglorious life.</p>
        <p>The Preairence for Blum and fireye.—There is no doubt that it is to be a year of gay colour., which will etrike a note of gladness after the prolonged season of mourning. Such an exhibition is to be expected, especially in view of the Coronation, but at the same time brilliancy will need something to modify it, end for thin porpoee the drspery trsde is anticioating a larger demand for grey than ever before. Lhis and grey are Queen Mary's favourite colours, and i m not only sore to be largely worn thin year, but are pe 'haps the safer and wiser choice inn season when arid colours will be conepicuous both in materials shd trimming.. Veiled Trirnminge—Many of the house or afternoon, frocks have the trimming Sc well an the material veiled. A cashmere doses for instance, would have the lace add embroidery with which it is trimmed, veiled with chiffon. This terminates sometimes, at the waist, at others it continues on the skirt, where it !forms draperies. Even cloth is now veiled with chiffon, in this case it Is not draped, but stretched tizhtly and forms on the bodice a bolero effect, or it may be that a tunic is nisnaged. In neckwear, e emarA novelty is the sailor collar, in pin-spotted muslin, /with drswn thread work, Mee and other effects. Peter Pan and Puritan sets ere still very popular. hluelin end lawn collar, look well with the fashionoble square back, whlNt scarves arose popular lb ever, those for spring wear being in metal effects, oxidised Miser and Persiso old gold. Hate—Ornamental soil Usefa —The meet noticeable feature of to-daye milliner% is, without doubt, the ontrich feetber,and the vogue br it bee become a cram. Milliners place it at every a laponsible and impoesibe, and it is considered not ly beautiful, but artistic. Uncurled ostrich loathe in plain, shot sod guided effecte are univemally u d, and it is said that later on marabout in conjuncti with shell-pink flowers, will be extensively employ . Never were feathers of every bind, long, short, it ' ht, and curled, more fashionable bet to the estejh muet be accorded pride of Ph.. Tta s'ar• ties include the triple limner toast, the fa y ostrich quillu nod the uncurled ostrich Plumes ith lancer effects. lo Paris the brims of hate have • full ruche of curled ostrich feathers and hie will probebly "take on " for it in found to be b owing, to both old and young. Stitched We in Bilk, shantung, and poplin, will be as popular thin summer as were timee of tweed thin winter. The trimming I most crises consiste of a single bend of glossy lest or. The stitched fancy mercerised cotton hats, dray d with metal tissue, urea novelty and more skPoneike than those mentioned above. Sports caps and hats, re etitcbed or plain in henget itmeorl poplins. , These a, most serviceable and have the further merit of being quite inexpeneiva Mod Simple but Costly.—There is alweys a tempo. ramp I I in faehions between the end of the January Mk. the influx of spring toilettes, and millinery, sad one of them periods of calm bee now net in, 'her is not much to chronicle of the fashions of the u . In those of toqnorrow, the Coronation strikes a areal note, and laces and silkn are coed, and regal- ing. In laces the new domino, are particularly d, especially those in black and white, picked out th cocoon silk. From Paris comes a rumour that "ode has decreed an era of simplicity in fernenine abut thissamesimplicity is nsually of ecostly kind. vs study of the plastic and aesthetic •rt iahowever, fr'"ng those lights in the World of Fashion, who neible for the creation of model dresses. o we relinquish our search for setorial and o ideas and biota in the long hundred past, and far more than is recognised or imagined to the k precede \\,, egos that prede our own, as we do our te end frock, to the °meters of art, who mune r our own time. It is said with regard to thst Non must go back to the Georges for a the present briskness in the tract., for never money wpent on hemlgear, yet never was rigiuslity In designs and trimming.</p>
