      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</p>
        <p>OUR MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The miscellaneous programme announced for next Wednesday's concert under the auspices of the Warrington Musical Society is to include Tschaikowsky's piano concerto in B Bat minor: It is worthy of note that in this instance the title la given quite accurately ; it is frequently advertised " B flat" simply. The Russian composer in question (born 181(1, died 1893) is notorious forhia use of the sledge- hammer, and he never did so with more effect than in this concerto. It has been complained of him that he frequently departs in detail from the accepted model; that he bad a habit of constructing movements within movements—of inserting secondary subject wholly foreign both in tempo and character, thus interrupting the main theme and hindering regular development of the main subject. Much was made of this on the publication of hisl symphony, but an eminent German critic, Dr. Rieman, appeared to serious musicians to abstain from judging that work by the standard of the Teutonic classics, and effectually pleaded the greater licence of the Slavonic school. This, however, by the way. It can hardly be said to apply to the It Ifat minor concerto with which we are to be favoured, next week. Musically, as someone has chromed, it is powerful self-assertion intermingled with sentiment not quite no plaintive as thecompoeer usually indulged in, relieved by the quiet and restful andantino. G.K.C.</p>
        <p>fast evening by arrangement with the enterprising Philomathic Society, Mr. G. K. Chesterton lectured i 3 Warrington. I always read hM Saturday article in the " Daily News," and anything else from iii, pen that I can lay tiny hands on. By-the-way P book of Chesterton quotations for each day in ate year has just been published, on that, as a reviewer fur the paper I have mentioned remarked, if Mr. Chenterton has not quite attained the ultimate apotheosis of Iteing immortaiined in a tear-off calendar, he ham come within an are of this distinction. It is added that the extracts demonntrate his extraordinary vernatility no loss than his gift for epigrammatic paradox. These characteristic, were duly displayed last night. " THE GUINEA STAMP" OR STUMP. Thursday'n visit to Bold-street of the Rev. Owen S. Watkins, a %elapse military chaplain who has seen considerable active service, remind, me of one or two thing,. Now I take it that this Mr. Watkins is son of the Rev. Owen Watkinn who achieved such dis- tinction as a missionary in South Africa. I once heard that gentleman forcefully appeal for increaned subecriptione to the funds of the eociety whose very able agent anti advocate he wan. and I have not forgotten the original way in which he drove home kin elmment idea. Ile begged his wealthy hearers to vary the monotony of the list in the annual report, page after page of which wont— one pound oan nine, one pound one, like a m with a wooden leg! That in turn reminds one of Father Phil in "Bandy Andy," and the nubscriptien lint for the repair and enlarged rent of Ballysloughgutphery Chapel, with its " Rottnd town, one pound ; rotted one town; town found, one round," and PO forth. A SAFETY VALVE.</p>
        <p>Apropoe the periodical lectures in connection with the Welsh Literary Society (one wan referred to is these columns last week), a while ago I wan in the Eye Hospital of which I have told my readers many a time and oft, and I noticed a particularly funny. beetling little body summoning to eye-dress- ing the men patients in the big ward. She was evidently in a hurry, and, as entire of the fellows did not respond with alacrity, alto gradually worked breech f into in state of excitement that caused some amueentent. As one after another slouched into the eye.kitchen, as a sort of small !surgery at the end of the ward is called, she rated them soundly for the lazy lot they were. At length one of them interposedwith—" Nr so are you Welsh ?" " Yee, was the re.monee. " Why do yen ask that ?" " Well," 4rawled the man, "I was just- thinking what an immense advantage a person of your temperames t most find it to be able to speak a language which ,we cannot understand." "What on earth's the man driving at ?" exclaimed the impatient nurse. "I me n,' added the imperturbable one. "that you can ',Gear without any of us knowing it, whereas if we ,iappened under extreme provocation to be betrayed is.a the use of language with a capital L, we should be reported and probably expelled.' "WHEN I WAS IN GAOL."</p>
        <p>last week's discussion respecting the War, ington Guardians' eputation work brought to my mind one or two little incidents which may be set down here. In former years I used regularly to accompany the members of certain Board of Guardians on their annual visits of inspection to the county asylums, and on at least four occasions of the kind we made a tour of the county prison. On the last of these our party were met by the Governor, and his glancing at the Home Office order with which our leader was armed, said "I suppose von are all members of the Board! referred to here?"" Yes," was the reply, "excepting one ,-Z4 reporter." "A reporter ! " broke in the GrAmrnor ; "but the permit says nothing of press 'representative.. I'm afraid I shall have to detain the reporter." Subsequently a subordinate official who R acting as guide carried the joke as fares to clap me into one of the underground cells formerly used for purposes of solitary confinement. To this day, when travelling, one of the guardians in question is fond of saying audibly to anyone who may happen to he with hiln--" The last time I erne in gaol," and then sinking his voice to a whisper; where- upon of course, stranger, among the passengera eye bun ank•nce. Apparently he enjoys that sort of thing. A HORRIBLE BLIP.</p>
        <p>The late Rev. Chem Clark, renowned as one of the moat eloquent and popular of lecturers of his time in the course of ore of his ['Odic deliverances incidentally remarked in my hearing —"Those of you who have been in gaol "--of course it was introduced to raise a smile ; but instead of laughing, many in the audience looked at each other in something like dis- may. The fact was that the venerable and really respected member of the church who was present as the unsuspecting lecturer's host, bad as a young man strayed front the path of rectitude, and had suffered a term of imprisonment for larceny ! In the liven of well.known men, literary characters especially, there , are for more instances, and souls of these I may ; recall on another occasion. A ROYAL TRAIN (?)</p>
        <p>'rho experience of the honoured Warriogtonian, Mr. James It. W. Comber, in esenciation with the !Julio of Connatight in Sierra Leone reminded me how, in view of an expected royal visit to the North tnany years ago, I was one of a party who went on a preliminary trial trip in the luxurious train for which the responsible railway company had made elaborate and costly preparations. Solon unsophisti- cated dwellers along the route hail aomehow imbibed the notion that the toyaltien were on hoard, no we messed to the front the moot distinguisheil-looking member of our little company and passed him of as a prince of the blood. There was tremendous cheering, and he most graciously acknowledged the abounding mani- festations of loyalty. Moreover, fortune con- tinued to favour us, for the royal party did not fulfil the engagement after all, no that the train was not used for the purpose for which it was designed. SHOPPING WEEK.</p>
        <p>As to this, I appear to have written my last note under miaapprehennion. I did not understand that an All-British display wan intended so far as Warrington VI. concerned. I had had no experience of an All-British Shopping Week. I referred merely to cases where the tradesmen of a town had, by meana of their local association, arranged to set apart a particular week for friendly rivalry in the way of window display, irrespective of origins and sources of manufacture or production. As I furiously testified, the results have without exception been profitable and satisfactory all round. BOY SCOUTS.</p>
        <p>I am strongly tempted to enlarge upon the War- rington Boy Scouts admirable demonstration, but unfortunately I am once more at the end of my letters. Of course one can have nothing but praise for the splendid discipline the movement is calculated to promote. However, having as to this said my my only the other week, I must reserve further remarks for some more convenient Beason.</p>
        <p>The King has fixed hfarch 21 for the reception of the deputation from the British and Foreign Bible Society, which will preeent a epeoially boned Bible sad address in connection with the tercentenary of the Authorized Version of the Bible,</p>
      </div>
