      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Boy's Shocking Death.</head>
        <p>A Boy's Shocking Death.</p>
        <p>INSPECTOR'S QUESTIONS AT INQUEST.</p>
        <p>An impost wa• held on Saturdey •fternrinn in the Police Station on the body of Charles Fos, 16 years of age, of 13, liesth•lane, Warrington, who mot with • ' terrible desth the previous Thunnity morning while In the employment of Mem. Pearson and Knowles. Main Ironworks.—Mr. S. Brighouse conducted the Inquiry ; Mr. Warren appeared for the Cilovernrnent Mr. F. G. Taylor (11. (freewill and Co.) for the com- pany ; end Mr. T. S. Steel for the relstives. Charles Fox, father of the deceased, was the first witness. Ile mid that he woe. horse driver, but owing to ill-health he had not follosed any occuantion for some years. Hie eon had been at Dallarn for too years, sod on the Wednesday night, en ususl, witness took his supper, finding him in good health. Thomas Millen] Webb, foreman roller In the 7-inch her mill, lining et SO, Itoome-street, mid the accident took place about twenty minuteei to live on Thursday morning. 'lle lad had only been working in the mill about 40 minutes,having changed with am.. no that he could get more experience. When the atoident happened he had alreadtpassed 60 or 70 pieces of wire through the rolls and At most base been momentary forgetful- nem that calmed him to miss catching the wire when it came through the rolls the &amp;scowl time. Ile had done the MOM sort of work !MOM of times. The wire would lle travelling shout eight miles an hoer, and deceased had to receive and pass it through the rolls twice and not once in he had to when he was in the ruin in which he usually worked, Mr. Steel It would he about 15 or 10 feet long when it came to Pon the first time ?—Yes. . . .</p>
        <p>tTi-in-e-erapees--1;t-1;er—r; o'ne—billet.ol;ing through the rolls and another 7—About half•a-minute; probably 010 f,</p>
        <p>thee. men are constantly on the move?—Certainly. This boy had been working all night ?—Yes. Ile @lofted shoot SA p.m. And out of that he would have half an boor for his supper ?—The time entice. About that at any rte?—Yen. Mr. Steel remarked that it might be between he had been working no long that Nocountwil for his lapse. Inspector Warren ?—What time did the boy have his supper?—it would be somewhere between nine o'clock and half past. . .</p>
        <p>—ili-d-h-e'r;o-Th from the supper time to the time of tho smirked without ceuiog ? No sir. What other time? Between every other best (about four minutes),</p>
        <p>he an interval of half an bowsaw:se ohm oloock ? —He had another eupper time from one o'clock to hal f peat. Somewhere about half an hoar !—About that, but. I cannot any definitely. . _ . .</p>
        <p>In reply to the roman of tho jury witnens said that the bar would be about N or tlbe. in weight. Joseph Shaw, holterdown," of 11, Dotton-ntreot, mid that about 4.40 on the morning in question be passed a red-hot iron billet through hi• rolls nod then peened it on to thong at which the deceased was work- ing. Ile easy him place it in the rolls end then he (witeese) turned aside. Almont immediately be heard a et:lrma and enw that the billet had penetrated bin leg, fully • yard of it going through. The lad fainted when witness pulled the wire out of his leg. The Coroner It in a long time for these lads to work.</p>
        <p>The Inspector (to witness): Do you know what meal times he had 7—About half an boor each for two suppers nod thre, or four minutos between heats. George Miller, 16, Phillip-street, Warrington, said that he wee on the opposite side of tho rolls to the deceased and he pawed the billet through in the ordin- ary way, and immediately afterwards he heard him scream. He ran round to the other side of the rolls sod saw the presions witness pulling the wire of his In He bad nest 50 or 60 pieces to him previously. John Hallowsy, lOC, Longford-street, Warrington said he worked in the 7in. Mill until 4 o'clock on the Thursday morning when Fox came to him and asked him to change places foes little while. In reply to the Coroner witness remarked that in the IRin, mill the Iron was heavier work and bed only to to I through the rolls once, wberess in the 71n, mill it had to go twice. Mr. Taylor : You changed pl.ces many a time?— Yes. This conclioled the evidence.</p>
        <p>Aflod by t} ho likft to say anything to the jury, M. Taylor observed thst he thooght the lad had died from .hook consequent on the lam?. The Foreman I cannot understand bim dying. The Coroner observed that the .loath was sanity explained, as the ahock must have been terrible. Nobody could blame the lad for trying to Improve himself. He thought it was • nice trait in bin character.</p>
