      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Orford.</head>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL.—A successful social was held in the school- room on alonclay evening in aid of the Sunday school, and promoted by the Sunday school teachers and the members of the Bible classes. Over a hundred young folk attended, and spent a very pleasant evening, with games, conga and ,lances. Miss E. linviour nag .11inaes" it a creditable meaner. Mr. J. Gibson wee well received for his rendering of "The Trumpeter," and Mr. A. Carter caused much laughter with his °emirs song " McNeil)." Mr. Fl. Posey proved a very able M.C. Dkotr heron Cr.REMONY.—A large congregation attended Orford Church on Sunday evening to witness the dedication eta new font cover, by the Rev. Canon Willis, Rural Dem. The choir walked in procession, beaded by the wardens and two sidemen, to the font, while the ceremony was gone through, afterwards going back to the chancel singing a hymn. The font cover hos been made, entirely by himself in bin spar« time and presented to the church by Mr. Louis Cross, one of the nideemen. It is a splendid design in the Gothic style of architecture, octagons] in form. Curved lathe base, in letters of raised character,are the words " Suffer little children to come unto me. A. 1,1911." Immediately above this, for a height of six inchetkesch panel is divided into two qcsorefoils, topped by a dentil tar old. These are in turn surmounted hy carved tapering panele fun a height of tour feet, the whole terminated by an ornamental cross to a fall height of 5 feet tt inches. The work has hem carried rilit in best selected Danoic oak nod is the design of Mr. E Warburton, architect, Bold•street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Winwick.</head>
        <p>Winwick.</p>
        <p>Coxcritr.—The Winwick Orpheus Society gave • concert, which was enjoyed by a large audience, in tbe Free Citurch. on Saturday evening. Several male voice choruses were song by the glee party, Nimiqta E Smethurst, H Morris, G Floyd, J IV l'aylor, W Jones, G Richards and Ci Gibson, Mr 0 Richard. was encored for bin gong "Sleep, comrades, sleep," and responded with "Sly a/o folk" (by request) Meseta .1 IV Taylor and II Morris gave a very good rendering of a pianoforte duet. Messrs It Morris and IV Jones made a good impression with their two duets " The Veteran's Toast." and "Two Sailors." Mite Roberta of Liverpool, was encored for both her songs, " Goodbye," and " Beautiful Garden of Rorer." reaponding with" Teach me how to Love," awl Von are going away." If, E Smethurst was swell received with his rouge, " Carnival," and " Grenadiers." Mr G Gibson kept the audience lea good humour with his humorous tales, and hie songs " Love Time "and "The Digby Club," and in response to persistent encores, gave a parody on the " Men of Harlech," nail " By tbe side of the Sen.' The duties it( accompanist ware very ably performed by Messrs II Morris and J IV Taylor, A 1.1.07 WENTY WANTY.U.—A special meeting of the Parish Council wire held on Friday, the 3rd tnut, in the schoolroom. The Rev. Canon Penrhyn presided and there were also present (—Masan IV. G. Wallace, and H. Hanibleton 'Mame), A. Allier and Joreph Shaw (Wine' ink), and K C. Cooper (clerk).—The Clerk mated that in accordance "ith instruction. given at the last meeting, he had written to Messrs. Smith, Gore, and Co., and hail received the following reply —Dear Sirs, —Allotment Gardens. The matter bee been considered, Jolt we are not able at present to State where a plot can be found. If you have eny suggestions to make, we shall Or pled to consider them." Mr. Wel Ince t.poke rather strongly about the delay, he said the allot- ments would not be of any use now for this year. The applicant, were determined to have their gerdens, and if they went en as they hail been doing, they would he made a laughing stoes. Ile proposed rent the Clerk ahould write to tbe Clerk of the County Council and ask him to lay the matter before the Allotments Committee and explain to them that they bail seven applicants, that they wanted an acre of ground, and that, they had tried to come to an arrangetnent but were unable Pi do so. The matter would then be in the handn of the County Commit. Mr. Hambleton secouded the resolu- tion, which was centred unanimously.—The Clerk read • letter (ram the Rural District Council Surveyor. thanking the Winwick Parish Council for their approval of the lighting at Orford, and mating that he would be pleased to submit the places where they proposed,to put the lamps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Liberal Activity in Lymm.</head>
        <p>Liberal Activity in Lymm.</p>
        <p>Optimistic Speeches at Social Evening.</p>
        <p>Although defeated ;at the last election, the LilTerals of Lyttro are already husy at work again preparing for . the next election when it comes along, and on Weilnen- dny even tog a ten party, concert and dance was hold in the Drill Hell, Li nut. A cheery spirit of rptimism pervaded Ike gathering, and fighting speeches were delivered by Mr. Frank Xlilne, secretary of the Central Executive of the Altrincharn Disunite, end Mr. W. E. Thompson, chairman of the Central Executive. Over 400 sat clown tote excellent tea,and afterward. , a high-el sea entertaintueut was gone through. Mist Elsie Partington eang charmingly and with great . feeling soroe attractive songs, and fully deserved the encores she received. Mr. J.H. itragendale contributed some amusing songs inn laughable manner, whilst Mr. Gorman. of AltrinchneLas an entertainer at the piano, was extremely popular, his •ernatality Leine recognised by the aurilence. trecitation entitled Booty Brid," which was written by Samuel haycock during the time of the cotton famine, gave Mr. Gorman an opportunity of showing that his powers of elocution are of no mean merit. Mr. Browne grure several recitations in a clever manner, whilst Mr. T. U. Eantham wee an efficient accompanist. During breaks in the progranuee Mr. Thompson and Mr. Miller I leli•ered speeches. Mr. J. li, Mather chairman of the Lynim Liberal Mit:mist ion, presided, and he was supported by Messrs.': hompson, Mitten, J. D. B. Smith, (hon. sec.) J. W. Brookes, 11. limokes, ft H. Gotolsed J. liedcli lie and the Heys. A. Barrett and B. W. B. Whiteway. A letter of apology for absence wns read by the hon. secretary, from Mr. (r. L. Welford, who was detained in London. Mr. Thompson,in a fighting speechdedd that although they had been defeated in the Altriechem dielaioe they must out be down-hearted. Although the election had been lost, he continued, they in Lytum had the satisfaction of knowing that they had dote their part excellently. (Hear, hear.) Vitrenever he and his friends in the Alt rincliam division had met, either at Piffled:rare or Xishcheeter, be had sheep) been able to say that as far a. L 1 tens was concerned in the great bight lent Decemher they bad done their part nobly and well. Hin only regret ens that the rest of the division bad not followed their lead,or that they in Lyrnm had not hive so numerically strong that they would hnve teen able to carry the rest of the division nlong with tbetille did not want to make out that Lynat wan an perfect RS it might be. bla this he did know they worked shoulder to shoulder for the good of the groat came of Liberalism. (Applause.) They bad had a unit from their member, Mr. Thompson continued. Of course he war their member—(latghter,—and he noticed that be had been threatening them with ell sorts of pains and petslties. There were always tales told at elections, end the tale he (the speaker) heard wad that inimeniately Mr. Keibly Fletcher heard that he war elected he wett straight tinny to Loudon by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <p>the first train end took hit west in the Home of Commons and they could not get him ost again. o Loud laughter).Mr.Fletcher came toLymm and told them that this Government would not grunt Homo Itole.Thost was rather estrange statement to make. He ( Mr. Thorn peon) did not think that at the next election Home Role would he included in the country's programme for the simple reason that it would bad been granted. Speaking en the renult of the election in the Division, Mr. Thompson mid that they had had a lesson, and he was sure that they would all make up their nsinds to wipe out the stain of the defeat they received !mt. December. Ile could cooly sn y that he was perfectly cure that they would tackle the work that was in front of them. He wea sore that they would ' make olotobly certain that at the next election they would have only one member—and boa Liberal mem- ber—for the bvision. They swore only part of a great army. They bed !suffered derma in their division, hut it was only • lOW clefest, for they had returned • Government with a big majority to pOeer, and he wee certain that the policies which they were advocating woold be carried t/ ough. (Applause. Next election, he hoped, would see a Liberal member for Altriecnano once mere. (Applativeo. Mr. Frank Milne, who had an excellent reception, game abort addrem. Although they had been defeated in Altrincham at oho lest election, he raid, they were buoyed op with the thought that et the last electim they sent in Liberal Government back Go power. Bot they meant to win an well next election. (Hear, hear.) He knew sonow.toing .0 the deterlOilletiell of the men of Louie. They bud token their defeat smiling, owl would worm op settling for the next round. • Applause.) iloot their came was wo•th fighting for, It was worth lighting if ikaen9 only that they could put their spoke in the wheel. It was worth fighting for, bemuse by no doing they could do aomething to help the came not only in .ynon and Altrinchatu hut