      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD CONCERT.</p>
        <p>A capital entertainment was held on Sa.-Jirtlnv PVPII- ing in the Lecture Hall, Crow-lane, when the St: Helm.. concert party goy° • concert. Solos wore rendered by Messrs. A. J. Wilkinson, F. J. Houghton and .1. Welsby, and Mr. Billy Cope contributed three humorous s,,ngs. Mr. J. Wel.hy art, responsible for two violin solos. The programme was concluded with a laughable eketch entitled .• Silly Ssetmy." the serious charscters being taken by Messrs. F. J Houghton, A. J. Wilkinson, J. Welsby, Billy Cope, and Mrs. F. .1. Houghton. RAILWAY SIDING FATALITY NEAR ST. HELENh.</p>
        <p>Henry Gaffney, a labourer, Burtonwood, was killed en a railway Aiding at the Havannah Colliory, at St. Helens, on 'Thursday. Gaffney was about forty-six years of age, and had worked at the colliery all his lire. After leaving work he wan walking along the siding sod stopped to speak to a man named Wilson. They were standing with their backs to an wore y waggon when a shunted engine caused the waggon to start, with the result that Gaffney was knocked down rind killed. Wilson fortunately managed to jump clear and escaped without injury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>-URBAN COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>-URBAN COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Newton District Council held its lisivtl meeting In the Town Hell, Esrlestown, on Monday. r C II F Borron presided, end there were present Messrs. Latham, Bent, (docile, Senor, Davies, Ritchie, Binliey , sod Higbee], with Mr C Cole (clerk), Mr A Bowes 1 (surveyor), end Mr R T Suttees (engineer.,</p>
        <p>1 TOWN IN.ANNING.-111(; ,WHENIF. It was resolve) that the report of the Town Planning Suh-cononittee of the 9th Jannary instant be received and adopted, the Chairman of the committee in addi• tine to the Clerk and Surveyor) being requested to meet Mr Leslie, via :</p>
        <p>An requested by the Sub-committee, the Surveyor , submitted cartoon of the district, showing thereon his smtgestions torn town planning scheme, which were rm follows, viz. The outlined scheme shown on the cartoon is one which instead of entailing much cat is calculated to save much evitenne ;n future years. It includes no proposition for demolishing property or zvideniug esitimtn roads. ft only attempts to foresee the needs of the future and to prevent any works being oarried out which will militete against those require. mnte being met at a reasonable cont. _ .</p>
        <p>It Is proposed to provide fore central open spare in Crow-lane, where the Technical School and Free Library are now. This is to be the principal point for important public buildings. Radiating from this central space would be several main roads. A new man road is chows running north- vrards,continning Gas-street until it meets Athton•road. A new !Mt' II road is shown from near the rorth end of Cross-lane to n junction with Vista-road. A road running net and west joins or tnese two last camel rends, and follows the Woodpit footpath across the Ilaydock Itailwey to Brunswick-road. Brunswick- road is shown continued towards Haydock, but of a greater width than Nlarket-street now has. Wargrave- road is shown continued on the north side of the rail- way, across Park-road to Mill-lane. A new road is shown running from about Thompson's pit in Wnrgrnver road, through the fields on the south aide of the cemetery, under the London and North-Western railway (fast line) try the existing bridge, and carried to a junction with the Warrington-road. Bradlegh•road is thown carried across the canal and Sankey Brook so as to jan the coal on the Burtonwood side. A new road in shown from VitrioLaquare, running southward across the canal and Sankey Brook to the road lending to Bortonwood. A new road is shown connecting Esrle-street directly with the Sr. Helens main road, on the westerly side of Sankey Brook. A new road is shown from Ilnyilock-street nor, ism the Haydock Railway to Cron -lane W. The positions for future work. and manufactories are shown as entirely south of the Manckeater and Liverpool line, end generally along the side, of the two railways running to Warrington, and also near the Sankey Canal. It will be advisable in drawing up a detailed scheme to , eammeth certain positions or sites for places of! worship, placw of amusement, schools, police stations, municipal buildings, newer, works, pa works, destructor,, baths, art galleries, and the like. On the cartoon are indicated the existing recreation groond in , Earle-street, the eleven acre site on the Common, and also various wooded places which should be retained es small perks or ornamental ohms. Amongst 5 hese are the Dingle, the lied Brow Wood, Bradley Woodoind the mall plot in itob-lene. Other recreation grounds should ba provided approximately on the sites shown." Resoleed—Thar, ea a preliminary, the Surveyor and the Clerk no instructed to meet Lord Newton's agent uMr. A. C. Leslie) and .0141 the suggestiora to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <p>CHANGE OF COUNCIL'S NAME.</p>
        <p>It was resolved that the following report of the General Purpose. Sub-Committee of the 9th January instant be approved and edopted, ei.• The Sub- committee carefully considered the queetion of the desirability or otherwise) of changing the name of the Crime, Dietrict so es to identify it with the two towns of Newton-le•Willow is and Esthetician, and after coo• siderable discuseion it was resolvml that in the apiniou of this nob-coramittee it is dtwireble, in the public interests, that the name of the Council and their district should be altered to • Newton-le-Willows end Earlentoern; and that It has recommendation that, ' under Section 55 (3) of the Local Government Act, 194, an application he made to the County Council for their sanction to such proposal." _</p>
        <p>IIOAT!NO rOW 'NEWTON</p>
        <p>A petition signed by a large number of ratepayers and residents in the district, with reference to the opening of Newton Lake again to the public for boat- ing during the 11.3113, mouths was submitted. It was resolved that the Ml', lI.Ct be referred for consideration to the General Purposes Sub.comniitti., and that Councillor Lootham's name be added to the in of were hers of that anb-comnoittee for the purpose. All the minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE CONFERENCE AT ST HELENS At the Central Baptist Chapel, St. Helene, on Satior day. e public conference was held under the otrapices of the Lancashire end Cheshire Band of Hope Union.— Hr. J. B. Leach, LP., president of lie union, presided, and, on welcoming the conterenr• . said that the St. Helens branch bad heen in existence over forty yearn In the °arty days children went to Sunday school. Band of Hope meetings. and then went borne and were in lied by eight o'clock, hot now there were all kinds of attractions of alight character to take them away from the Sunday schools end the churches. They had all these things to watch, and the Bends of Hope must remember their ix symnsitilities and try to impress the minds of the tong with right principles no as to atrengthen them for the battle of life, and enable them to successfully face dillicultivs that their fore- fathers never knew of.—The conference discussed papers on the question, . Is poverty the cause or result of intemperance ?” which were resd by Hr. J W. Ctoston, of Nutgrove, and Mr. A. Dodd, or Haresllnch. In the evening a temperance meeting waa held. The Rev. W. Floiroyd presided, and addresses were given by Dr. Buchan, medical °Meer of health, and the Rev. W. H. Jeffries, pastor of the Knowsley-road Congregs• Donal Church, Sr. HELENS TERRITORIALS. RETIREMENT OF A SERGF:ANT-MAJOR.</p>
        <p>At the Engineer Hall, St. Helena, on Saturday night, Sergeant-Major E. J Hannaford, RE., wan the guest of the officers of the Weet Lancashire Divisional Royal Engineers on his retirement front the position of regi- mental sargeant-major alter fifteen end a half yeers' service with the St. Helens corps. Lieutenant-Colonel IL E. Snyce, VI)., who presided. spoke of Sergeant- Major lienneford's splendid services to the corps, and on behslf of the officers and nien wished hint success. Serseant-filejor Ilenneford, who when appointed wits the youngest sergeant-major in the Army, returned thenks. Since he wee appointed in August, 15G, he had enjoyed the happiest relations with the officers, and be thanked all the rank and file for the appreciation they bad always shown of his services. Colonel Sayco presented elfitiency Territorial medals to Company Sergeant-Nini ors Joe Gill and 11. Shaw..., Corporals T. Magee and H. Chadwick, Sappers T. Twist and E. Smith and Driver Eccleston. In replying to the tenets of the officer., Colonel Sales mid that last year there seemed to be momg drawback which interfered with their recruiting. lie could not unite make out what it wee, hut the corps had not had its fell strength. This was moat deplorable when they looked at the young fellow. who were walking about the Wards with nothing to do. They must have their ! full complement or men, and he appealed to tha ! sergeants and others to use their influence to get young men 0 join, so that they might return to the pusition ' they were in a few years ago, when the inspector told them they were second to none. . .</p>
        <p>...... ..0.0. Nlator Ili...isle also i.poke, end mid thnt the backbone of the Army was th• non-commimitiond men, and he hoped they would all assiat in the matter of recruiting</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SEACOMBE SEANIAN DROWNED AT WIDNES An inquest war held at Widnes on Saturrliy into the death or Thomas Johnson (39), mate of the steamship Le Blanc, of :14, Belvieu-road, Seecomire.—Thomas Hewitt, the captain, said that Johnaon on Tuesday night went or some coal. As he did not return, fearing something was wrong. he the captain) procured a boat- hook and grappled, and after halloin•hour ecoonerrr,l the body —The Jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>MR. J. DE KNOOP Al RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MR. J. DE KNOOP Al RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Ott Monday eventomthr.Jersey de Knoop, proepective candidate for thc Northwich Division. formally opened e Conservative workingmen's club. Ile mid that the next eloctios would mark the result of twenty-flve years of bard work, for by then they would hens suceteded in instilling their principles into the majority of the voters of komhwich. Runcorn was the citadel of the Northwich Division, atil et the last election Runcorn had done better twin ever before. The town cowd put en Conservative whenever it liked. Ile bad taken up politica with rather a bad gnwe, end he hail not %muted to fight Northwich at all. Having had the fight, he would say that he one very grateful to the people who had insisted on his cowing forward. lie wantal another light —t cheers)—and if they would lot • hint hate another "go "be would be delighted t 6 stand. (Applause).</p>
        <p>-- THE FOOTBALL FRACAS. , MAGISTRATES' EXTRAORDINARY COLI 'B. At Runcorn Heroines, on Monday. a sequel WS pro- vided to the scene which occurred at the Runcor foot- ball ground on January 21 alter the Oldham-Rana:ern match. George Molvneux, 11Front-street, It spectator, 0...4 summoned for assaulting Joseph Owens, one of thel Oldham forwrods. Mr. Livaker,solic:tor, said that at the conclusion of the gent. between Runcorn and Oldham Owens was kicked as he WWI leaving the Irwell-lane enclosure. Ile remonstrated, and was struck twice, being knocked down. The tumult followed a friendly game, and it would be very detrimental to national sport if this sort of thing was not stopped. I Owens gave evidence.. He said he wan knocked I senseless, and found he laid a black eye when he came I to himself. Ilts lip was also out. The Chai MI. (De. ltl'Dougall) made several imp ti r les with regard to Go prosecution. ~- ..</p>
        <p>Itlr. I.lnelter geld It was undertaken by the Runcorn Football Club end not on the euthority of the Northern Union.</p>
        <p>—Tit-a—Chairman told the Cen;ll did not wish a court of justice to he used to settle squabble, of this sort. Alfred Leyland, an old player of the Runcorn Club, wail also summoned. In this Case Fred Smith and George Smith spoke to seeing Leyland !alike Owes, twice, knocking him down. The bench took the unusual course of adjourning both cases till the end of the football season-51.y next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>STARTLING WARRINGTON EXPLOSION</head>
        <p>STARTLING WARRINGTON EXPLOSION</p>
        <p>Seven Men Injured at Monks' Hall's.</p>
        <p>A sensation was caused about ft o'clock on Thursday morning in Liverpool•road. Warrington, as the result of an explosion which occurred at the iron works of Mestere. Monks, Hall and Co. A terrific report was heard, and this was shortly followed by the blowing up of a huge spelter bath, ventral tons of rnolten metal Lring Hung abont in all directions to the danger of the workmen. Fortunately the majority of the workmen were enabled to gain cover and prote, tion from the scalding apelter. but seven men received burns about the arms and faces, and were immediately pent to the local Infirmary for treatment. Later it transpired 'hat their injuries were only of , minor character, and after being medically attended they were able to proceed to their homes. The names of the injured men are George Mains. lit, Elliastreet. neck. Harry Ellis: 25, Hesketh-street. hands and face.</p>
        <p>John Kirkshaw, I. Ilaydock-btreet, arm and font. John Lawrence, 44, Haydock-at tem, head and arm Thomns Johnton, Bramhall.atreet, head and arm.</p>
        <p>Jcdin Weston, 11, Jonea-street, arm and neck. Harry Atkinson, 11, Ernest-street, fate arm Itad leg.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>THEY COST SO LiTTLE, DO SO MUCH FOR SKIN AND HAIR.</head>
        <p>THEY COST SO LiTTLE, DO SO MUCH FOR SKIN AND HAIR.</p>
