        <p>[ALL RIONTS RISEIVED.I</p>
        <p>A CLOUD OF SUSPICION.</p>
        <p>Br BURFORD DELANNOY Author of " The Garden Court .Mystery," " Be- tween Ine Lines." " The Margate Murder Mystery," " A Woman at Bay." &amp;e.</p>
        <p>" The daughter of a curate!" my mete raved. "A wretched ninety pounds a year curate! You are mad !" _</p>
        <p>He glared contemptuously it*, he spoke. I answered not a word, knowing his temper— and knowing my own. For all that. it W. as much as I could do to keep the brake applied. "Do you think for one moment"•—his fist was used on the table by way of emphasis— that I would give my consent to such a miserable alliance? Do as I tell you or take the consequences. Love!" The word was exploded scornfully. "This is the twentieth ' century! That sort of nonsense wail early Victorian. Money and position are the things which count nowadays." He paused, that he might take breath. Still I was able to control myself. But it did not work well. A soft answer may posseso wrath. ' turning properties, but silence, in this in- stance, seemed to be an addition of so much fuel to the fire. _</p>
        <p>"I have set my heart." he almost shouted in the heat of his temper. "on your marrying your cousin, Lady Geraldine." " And." I answered. forced to speak at last, "I have told you that I absolutely refuse to do no."</p>
        <p>You ahnolutely refuse! Have you thought of the poverty-stricken misery into which 'love in likely to leud you? Of what your future in likely' to be with a millstone round your neck in the shape of thin penniless pauper of a—" "Uncle—"</p>
        <p>"Silence, air! Let me lia.ah—whftt probably be the laat apeech you will ever hear me utter."</p>
        <p>This was too much. He wns pnitively threatening me. The brake was losing its power. "This girl, whose face in her fortine," ia resumed. "is no doubt chuckling in lit • sleeve !at the thought of having entrapped a rich man's heir! My lawyer. to-morrow, unlenn you change your mind, shall draw up a codicil which will leave you penniless." "If you only knew—"</p>
        <p>"If-:-I—onl,—knew! Good Heaven., boy, don', I know.? Isn't it my knowledge which prompts me to speak as I do? Ilave I lived sixty years in the world without knowim what women are? I put the question to yon once for all. Frank: Will you 'give up thie milk. and-water chit? Will you do as I ask yon?" There was hesitation over my answer—in no way due to any intention of failing in with his views. I loved Millie far too well for that. But he was in d^adly cermet. I understood then my own utter helpless- ness. My uncle, Sir Peter. a bachelor, had promised hie favourite sister on her death-bed to look after her boy, and—let me be just and record here the fact—right faitnfully had he fulfilled his promise. Too well, I n a sense. For I had not been permitted to ,study for a profeeeion, or acquire knowledge likely to be of practical benefit if it came to fighting the world. There wee no need for it. my uncle said; he made me a liberal allowance, and when he died I was to inhe it all. What more did I want?</p>
        <p>Hot-headed and impetuoue. my uncle was one of the beet men in the wort I. I woe keep- ing that fact before me as I -mdeavoured to ehape an .aneaer.</p>
        <p>But quick tempera run in die family. Sir Peter's abuse of the girl I loved incensed me. As to marrying Lady Geraldine. I could have laughed at the mere thought—.had I been in a laughing mood. "Come. sir. your answer?" _</p>
        <p>- -......,.. Ilia flat on the blue again, be way of emphasis. A glare, such an might bays been expected from a tiger. and—w4.ll. I think the look really proved the last straw. ' "If there were not anotiter woman in the 1, world," I said, heatedly, "I wouldn't marry Lady Geraldine! I bate her—with an intent- arty as great as is my love for Millie Lorain.!''</p>
        <p>It wan an injudicious speech; it proved another "Inst. straw." My uncle sprang to his feet. and ?Lurked to the d,or.</p>
        <p>"Outside my house!" he cried, and his qui"ering forefinger pointed the way. "And never darken its doors again—unless you come toack to tell me that poverty has taught you oommon sense. Go!" Looking back, it all smacks of cheap melo- drama, which, in calmer moments, we should both have laughed over heartily. As it won. I (latighed in what I intended to be a scornful, !mocking manner—after tha manner of the viilain of the piece—and, turning on my heel, walked out of the room, whistling. My uncle's threats had not penetrated the armour of my anger. I felt that I knew him. Ile would come round in a day or two—or a week or so, any way. Ho had grown to rely on me; I was his companion, his confidant; he could not do without me.</p>
        <p>! So. hoeing 'left' i•te house. I walked away— loralked until the shades of night fell. I made '•ip my mind to go to town and spend a week therm, till Sir Peter roe tiled me and eximiissed sorrow for what he had said about Millie. ..... •</p>
        <p>_ . „.. -.....- The. sound of my ovrn laughter at thought of giving up Millie brought me down to the level of common eenee. Where was I? I looked arrnind for familiar landniarks. The moon happe!..sing to come from behind in clond at the moment 1 saw where I was-Barling- ton Crces-roaddk Nearly eightieilee from Immo! A good walker, and to ierustaincd. I had covered the distance with on noticing it. It was nine o'clock. Feeling in) my pockets. I discovered , that I had but a fel• shillings there. ecarcely suffieient for my fare o London. What a fool I had erm to loave the home like that When, too.c it would have been no now to go op to my raevi room and take from a drawer the notes an gold which rested there. Nine o'clock! Two ho re' sharp welkin^ would carry me 19:ek to th Hall. If I mule obtain my money. I should . the 11.90 up to London easii Could I manage to got in own room? That won poesil opened on to the lawn by The key of the lattor wan I Turning. I retraced my st, spot of rain, and a glance whence it fell, was a movet nee wet through before 1 had . ! Illock. 1 ,ading. thou lit</p>
