        <p>But I scarcely heard wl, shaken the entire building. I Aitken to have thought fir auytl. I had etumbled over. That non. and quiet, filled me with horror that did not move!</p>
        <p>Stooping. I kit about eautioumly tuan'a body ! My band ettooonteret</p>
        <p>wet—and n.arm-La.:l;ti-ei,--kie—a my breath cam, and went in lb Fear held me in MAI!</p>
        <p>Staggering to my feet. I stumblo • gas hravket and jerked the cort Tent,WV v,a reeted on .what lay</p>
        <p>acrealiacJ wV. wflici rang</p>
        <p>(141u8C. It .as My allele JEWJWCI to MS heart! . . .</p>
        <p>The lolood-shained ahirt.frout ehewad the location of to, w•Ri..d; the deadly stillness of OW bony—all was eloquent of the worst. Half-drtwaed, wholly affrighted, the ser- vants tumbled into the room. The butler had inure sense thou net of them put to- geti‘er; be went straight to the tel., hone and stag up the local doctor and the polioe.tation. So surgeon's verdict wee needed. The old man was dead. In the profundity of my grief I took no notice of the lixdui with which I was being favoured. But at lust it was borne Into me that I was suspected of murdering my undo!</p>
        <p>When the police arrived, the servants spoke of the quarrel that evening—of how I had been turned from the Hall and forbidden to enter its doors again. I listened with that far-away feeling one experieneve in a dream. The officers were soon infected with the suspicion. There was. too, the evidence of their eyes. The accused was wet, muddy. dis- hevelled, buntline—and with bloodstained hands! .</p>
        <p>On the floor lay the weapon with which the murder had been committed. On the wall was the shape—made by the sun—chars' it had hung for years. They looked upon it as a "red-handed" capture. 31y isolated position—they all stood away from me as far as possible--was, I think, the first thing to bring are to consciousness 14 what was happening. When the police at last 6pok2. I mine out of my stupor and pro- tested my innocence. I might la well have paved my breath. On every face there eat expressed' firm conviction of my guilt. Bewildered, stunned. dazed, I offered no re• sistance alien, presently, an officer'. hand was placed on my shoulder, and the sergeant said:</p>
        <p>" Frank Corylon. I arrest you on suspicion of having murdered Sir Peter Nunes." Apart from the natural horror and grief at my uncle's death and my own detention for causing it, the dominating feeling in me* was one of wonder—What would Millie think? That question was welf.propounded a thou- sand timew in the days that followed. . . . .</p>
        <p>The family lawyer' whose faith in me -- Heaven bleen him !—never wavered, wae un• able to cope with the matter. Convinced of my innocence, he lacked the ability to prove it. end urged me to employ s private detec- tive. It was necemiary. he eaid—on the prin- ciple of vetting a thief to catch a thief—to fight the conelueion at which the police had arrived.</p>
        <p>A private detective named Watson Ward wan retained. I hod heard of him an being a ahrewd. keen, observant needle of a man. When he came to interview me in the prisum, I 'maw rather dinappMided Ut him. I could see nothing of the nharpneen and craftimen which I had concluded would be pert and Parcel of hi. eqinpnient. Before the flninh, ',weever, I wan taught the truth of the pro- verb concerning the mistake of trusting to appearances.</p>
        <p>I am Watson Ward. I pick up the thread of the narrative at thin point end carry it to the end. My line in inventigst‘on. I may add that ..,hen I drop o h..: I usually bring up a finh. That savourn of egotism, you think? Well, people don't get reputations without de- nerving them, no o rule, tin they! Any way, if there in a knotty criminal problem knock. ing &amp;snit. it gen,,rilly finds its way towards me. Why, even Sootland Yard itself— But that .would. hatching tale out of school.. "</p>
        <p>What Fronk Ca;ion tdd me in Mir- borough Gaol was easy to believe. He was hot-headed. but there was not a trace of vice in him. It became clear that the only way to p:ove his innocence was to obtain evideno• of someone else's guilt. "Someone else" wanted finding. The fo- cused man wan unable to furnish mu much .4 a clue as would have filled a hollow tooth. My work seemed cut out with a large pair of Sell- nor,. After going over the Hall. and inter- viewing the servants there. I trained back to London. TLere I went to the shipping office of which the dead man had loon the head; asked for and interviewed the bead clerk; had quite a long tall with him...</p>
        <p>He'wris very --tiorry for young Maater Frank --so much so, that I thought the sorrow over' done. You n put too much colour on a pic- ture, you know. Youth, he said, had hot and was apt to lcse its head at times. too. tie , toting man one in love—mill the aweeteat and prettiest woman in the world. I pricked up en' errs at that. The head c;erk was not too old to be it target for one of Cupid's darts. And the most honest of men sometimes :all victims to the gneen-eyed MON- Lter.</p>
        <p>"I Its,. seen this young man ii gaol." I "laid. "What do you think—guilty or not guilty.. as they say to juries?" "Sot guilty." _ _</p>
        <p>"You r'speAr confidently." I observed, for the quiet assurance of the negative had sur• prised me. "Doubtless eoufs.lenee with a •oundation. Dv you suspect someone else?" •' Yes." The affirmatiNe uttered 1111 rein, '..antly as before Ire had spoken promptly. "I am sorry to say I do. If I am correct, the murder arts committed to cover up another Irrime." This gores in•er•st,ng. me!" . . . . .</p>
        <p>" the offiee- he;* has bren in • turm.dl. Two cheipies hail loon forged. Sus- picion fell on all in the afire. To nie was Ile- Oted the taiali of investigation. At but the inquiry narrowed down—to the one person I</p>
        <p>"A clerk here?" "Yes. Almost a junior—Edgar Loraine." " Loraine! Surely that is the name of the girl the accused man intended to marry?" "Alas ! yes. The boy it her brotber. good-enough lad when he first filled a stool here, but—it is the old story—bad companions. I have found it all Oct. Betting. card playing. steavy lowies, and, then, to pay hi. 'debts of honour', he dishimoured by forging other people's names to two cheques." "A had business."</p>
        <p>'As the head clerk echoed my words he raised his hands. " That is not a fitting description. His father is a clergy. man—this will break his heart. The sister, too. Millie—"</p>
        <p>"Hes the boy bolted?" No. This --he picked up a letter which rested on his desk—" is excusing his absence front the office. on the ground of illness." " A trick to enable hint to escape?" "No. I sent to verify the twat...tent. Be was found ill enough." . . - .</p>
        <p>"At his lodginr? Let US go at once •nd eee for ourselves. - -</p>
        <p>We did; burrowed from the Bank to Shen. herd's Bush. In that district the youngster had rooms. We found him in them right enough. He sins as pale as a ghost, and un- doubtedly ill. Usually, when I lock into a man's fare. I am able to read it; e•-, are difficult things to foree falsehood into.• But thin boy's expres- sion puzzled me. He we, a fair-haired. blue-eyed slip of a 2.armit:stL hint hivsetsistizot,so tl,ze ,rl,l.ta-akd yscillating nature. plainly. As to an ability to commit murder, I could not tell. I introduced myself as a detective. notin; Nat he went even whiter than he hail hikm on entrance—although that was. of course. no sl proof. Whcn I accused him, he vehe- 'y denied that he had been out of Los. the night id the murder. baffled Inc. Suspicion about the head 'rived. There seemed to be a desire 'art to shield the man in gaol. and yet cc to fix tit,' crime on the junior clerk, nding the stain. I interviewed the but drew a Hank. Her lodgers, abe d hatch-keys; they might come in Ott a dozen times a ch.y without her bit the wiser. heeame necessary to prosecute my in- lirther afield. As a result of them, I, apologised to the head clerk. He I if the investigation clean enough. indness of heart, I suppose, that made o shield the boy. ter it was who. had used the dagger ant slept at his lodgings ow tile night urder. That was lie nuirmer one I wn—although be was artful enough ,nd in bed by the eersant there toe ling. journeyed down the night before to on, three stiles from the Hall. and )y a train which called there in the fling. Arrived in town. he let him. he house at the time when the early</p>
        <p>morning milkmen were on their rounds—the evidence of one of the latter clinched that part of the story. It wee a bad condition of affairs. Bad to tell the prisoner, woree to tell the father, and might bane the effect of making the girt give up her lover. But, then, that en/ mere r.hti- ment. One has no right to allow ouch a thing to enter into businem. . . . .</p>
        <p>Be the time I completed my inquiries the boy had bolted. That rather fouled the course I was on. Not that I cared so much about tho guilt 7 man eneaping_-1 wae retained for the defence, you see. But to enable me to prove the accused moo's, intwx,nce. I wanted just five minutes with the guilty one. If in the course of that interview I obtained a written confession, it would mean tae speedy release of my client. quick work means good pay—and I may remark that I am not in the detective business :or my health. With a view to cheering the prisoner a hit, I went down to the gaol where he wee con- fined. To my huge astonishment. instead of rejoicing at what I had to sac. he !wended shout the boy more than be had done about. himeelf!</p>
        <p>..... "Thin disgraee," ho maid, " will break the heart of the girl who hi oil that is deareet on earth to me. Better that I 0114,111,1 Colltillllo to suffer_ rather than thot second grief should email her. She must never know the truth."</p>
        <p>They knew 'tn. ;at the gaol, and had given me the privilege usually accorded to lawyer. only; that in to say. my interview with the prisoner was taking place in the solicitors' room. To that, in the midst of our conversa- tion. came the gaol governor. ." Congratulations. Mr. Carylon," he said, enhancing to his captive and shaking bends. " A lime Office warrant has just arrived— for your release!"</p>
        <p>"Sly releae.e?'' "The really guilty man haa at last con- feeperl to the crime.'</p>
        <p>A shiver lan over me—attic to disappoint- ment! 1 had been forkstalled by the offivial police. 'they. I thought. had got kohl of the boy. Hirt confession bad been laid before the authorities. This was the freedom I had counted on procuring nowelf. Then I heard all the' governor knew. The boy was lying in St. Thomas's Hospital, at the point of death. There had been an acei• dent; a magistrate had been summoned to the hospital and taken his dying depositi.an. We journeyed to London by the next up train. At the hospital it did not need the nurse's fingers to her lips to make us cautious. If ever death was writt,n on a face it appeared on that of the bov who lay there —with a complexion almost the colour of the pillow his head rested on. I believed, after all, that my theory was the correct one: the boy had taken fright and made a bolt for it. But, lying there, he said differently: that his conscience had pricked him; that he was going straight to Scotland Yard to give himself up, rather than be haunted by the thought that another man was suffering for his crime. A motor-'hus skidded Jost as he was crossing the road; there was a jam between—but there, an account of what happened would not make nice reading. The confession gave an account of the forgery of the two cheques; of how the head clerk had, practically, discevered his guilt. The hesitation between the ideas of suicide and an appeal to the head partner, who had given him the situation two years before, for his father's—the curate of the parish—sake. Particulars of the meeting with Sir Peter Nunes just outside the latter's house•, the invitation to ent.r; the discussion which fol- lowed; the malice where mercy had been looked for.</p>
        <p>The Baronet had seized on she boo's guilt as a handle with which to humiliate and shame the Loraine family. He threatened to prosecute, and hound the curate and his adventuress daughter—he called her worse— out of the place. l'hey were twins, it appeared—the brother and sister—and the love existing between them was deep. One word leads to another— no words no quickly as hot ones. Those used by the bov's employer cut deeply. When the Baronet found that him abuse of the girl had a rousing effect he continued it. But he went too far. For Aim!,lf the lad was ready to bow his head; but when his sister's fame was assailed the blood went to his head —he saw red.</p>
        <p>'The—xi-mg-WI: on the wall—the steel of which flashed brightly in the gsslialit—caught his eye. It rieornea to him that it wan there as an instrument of vengeance. The next in. !Want it was in his hand, and in the moment after it was buried in Sir Peter Nancy's heart.</p>
        <p>Of all nature's Y•wa, self•preservation in Old firnt. Never for • moment imagining that his friend and prospective hrother-indaw• was likely to be accused of the crime, the noir- lerer sought safety in flight.</p>
        <p>A glad -light came into the dying lad's. eyes as they rested on his visitor—for he had eyes Inlv for Frank Carylon. . . .</p>
        <p>You're free, Fi'ank," he said, in a voice chat was little more than a whisper. "I'm glad you came. Directly I heard that you— but you know, don't yoi:! It's good to feel your hand and know that you forgive me foe the trouble I gave you." "That's all right, laddie." Frank spoke gruffly, pressing the other's hand. ." Vo.s never meant to wrong me." . . . . .</p>
        <p>" But I did. And I am norm If I hadn't been such a cow ant I should have ended m• life rather than go don n to Sir Peter's ana beg for mercy. I wanted time—wanted to say that if he are me that I would pay back every penny. But he wouldn't give me the chance, and he said things about—" '•' ' • "</p>
        <p>I limos., !addle. 1 hauve heard." " Millie, Frank. I want you to—to pro- mise about her."</p>
        <p>'That she shall never know—l mean about the disgrace. Death covers things, doesn't it? The dad—poor old dad !—I suppose he must. But if you can keep it from Millie, do." Frank promised. And, somehow, he was able to keep his word. The girl was so cut up over the lons of her brother that she had no thought sf reading papers. After the funeral she had a serious illness, and spent six months in the South of France.</p>
        <p>....... ......—. When she came back to England the whole affair was !a the limbo of the pant. She came back, too, with a changed name. For out there—where the orange blossoms grow as profusely no bllsckberries do with us—she be- came Ilia. Frank Carylon. The game of life Is a curious one, isn't it? The detective sometimes holds strong hands. bat the Fell Sergeant always hay; the deadly trump up his sleeve. When he tabs a hand in the game the other players may a; well put tPiety cards on the table. It is the 1.11 of the curtain.</p>
        <p>ITris END.I</p>
