      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. C. ROUCHLLY A Remarkable Old Warringtonian</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. C. ROUCHLLY A Remarkable Old Warringtonian</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>HELPED TO BUILD THE 'JOHN TAYLEUR.''</p>
        <p>Be the death of Mr. George Roughley, on Monday, the memory of the terrible wreck of the Warrington ship, "John Tayleur" hat been revived, for he was at the time a ship's carpenter and helped to build the ill-fated vessel. He was born in Warring- ton over 80 years ago and was educated at the Bluecoat School, leaving there when 12 years of age. He was then apprenticed to Messrs. Clare Pi Ridgway as a carpenter and after serving the usual period of seven years, he found employment with the firm of Messrs. Tayleur, engineers, whose works were on the banks of the Mersey, just behind Messrs. Crosfield's Soap Works. In 1850 the firm conceived the ambitious pro- ject of developing a new industry—nothing less than the building of iron ocean going vessels. About that time the dis- covery of gold in Australia occasioned a rush to the gold fields, and passenger ships were in great demand. In 1852 the "John Tayleur' was planned and began to rise in the stocks, a long well designed and graceful vessel of about 2,000 tons burden. It was intended for her to be a steamer, but as the engine builders were exceed- ingly busy and Messrs. Tayleur did not want to wait she was converted into a three-masted sailing ship. As a ship of that type she was the largest of her time, and there was naturally considerable pride that the town was to give to the world a record vessel. October 4th, 1853, was the day fixed for the launching and the river banks presented an animated appearance as the hour of the great event approached. It is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>LATE MR. U. ROUGULE'</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. U. ROUGULE'</p>
        <p>no wonder that expectations ran high, for the success of this first iron vessel would mean an important industry for Warrington. When the time arrived for the launching, Mr. Roughley was seated in a small boat in the river, and when he helped to loosen the slips the vessel took a dive into the river, which caused such a swell that the craft in which he was situated toppled over and he was precipitated into the water. He was not the only sufferer, for the water was so disturbed that it splashed straight up the banks and covered the spectators from head to foot. On January 19th, 1854, the vessel started from Liverpool with 600 souls on board and but for the fact that the emolu- ments offered to Mr. Roughley were in- sufficient he would have gone out on her maiden voyage as ship's carpenter. Two days afterwards the emigrant ship—the pride of Warrington—was wrecked on the mast of Ireland, and 400 people lost their lives. Of the 10Q women on board, only three of them were left to tell the story.</p>
        <p>Undeterred by this sad event Mr. Roughly a few months later enrolled on a vessel and had a safe journey to Australia, coming back to Ceylon and India. At Madras the passengers had to land by means of serf boats, which was ruat then considered as disagreeable as now. After an absence of three years he returned to England, was married, and employed at the place in which he learned his trade. Some 50 years ago he along with several others—Mr. William Barlow, of Bewsey street, was one of them —founded a branch of the Order of Druids, which was then in its infancy. He passed ough all the offices, and his efforts were atly appreciated. He was the oldest mber of the Mermaid Lodge, trustees of Warrington Order of Druids, Widows and Orphans Fund, Hope Collecting Society, and a member of the Committee Medical Association. He retired from business about 29 years ago, and spent his latter years with his son-in-law and daughter (Mr. and Mrs. J. B. Wood) of 1 Chester-road. TI-LE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Thursday after- noon, the first part of the service was held in St. James' Church (at which the deceased was a regular attendant) and was conducted by the Revs. H. B. Firth and E. M. Swift. The interment was in the Warrington Ceme- tery, where a goodly number gathered as a mark of respect to the deceased. Among those present were Mr. &amp; Mrs. J. B. Wood (son-in-law and daughter) and children, Messrs. J. Roughley (cousin), W. Lee, A. Cook, N. Cook, R. Cook, J. Cotterill, T. Thornley (nephews), Mrs. Wood, Messrs. F. Rathbone, T. Boscow, and Dr. Martin. Among those who represented the Druids were Messrs. J. Bibby, R. Lees, J. Jackson, H. Kent, PuciPl, W. Wood, F. 'Sennett, Clough, Pointer, Caldwell, W. Bibby, Ray, Hankey, Allen, and Perdue. The wreaths received included " The Com- mittee and Members of the Order of Druids," the Trustees of the Widow and Orphans' Fund, and the Committee and Members of the Hope Collecting Society. Mr. Stephens was the undertaker.</p>
