      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>MR. WHITTON AND MISS BUTTON.</head>
        <p>MR. WHITTON AND MISS BUTTON.</p>
        <p>Photo.</p>
        <p>On Ssturday last at Christ Church, Latchford, the wedding took place if tt illiam Arthur Whitton, Lethbridge, Alberta, Canada, and Mies Christina Hutton, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Robert Hutton, of Fir Frore,- Latchford, Cheshire The ceremony was perfoimed by the Rev. G. C. Briggs, in the presence of a large congregation.</p>
        <p>The chancel was artistically decorated with tulips. other white thwers, foliage and palms. Mr. T. L. Fry was at the organ and while the guests were assembling he gave a abort recital. The items included" Minuet in B" Smart),"( Sunset Melody" (Vincent), and" Communion" (Jules Griaon . As tbe bride walked up the mule to the altar the clngrega- lion ruing "The voice that breathed o'er Eden." The Psalm " fleati Omnes" was chanted by the choir at the conclusion of the ceremony, and the hymn " 0 perfect love" followed. Mr. Fry played "Praeludium Is " (Mendel...he) while the bride and bridegroom sign d the register, and the happy pair left the church to the arable of Meidelasnlin's Weoding March.</p>
        <p>The bride who was given away by her father was eharmirgly attired in a Princess gown of white : oriental satin with court train. The bodice was trimmed with white silk net, and pearl trimming, and the skirt with orange blos,om and ornaments made ~f the same satin. A pretty drapery was Mso arranged at the side of the skirt. The court train which was square was prettily trimmed with chiffon, orange blossom, and white heather and caught at the left shoulder. A sheaf of longillorum Lillies WaS carried tied with broad satin ribbons. The bridesmaids, Miss Janet Hutton and Miss</p>
        <p>Margaret Hutton, sisters of the bride, wore charming dresses of pale blue oriental satin trimmed with net chiffon, and inserti, ir to match. Large picture bats of blue satin to match the gowns were worn, trimmed with black velvet ruching oral large roses of a beautiful pink shade introduced on the right side. They carried bouquets of pale pink tulips tied with soft satin ribbons, and wore turquoise earrings, gibs of tbe bridegroom. The bride's mother was handsomely attired in a gown of hello chiffon velour embroidered, and trimmed passementerie in shades of mauve and green. Yoke and sleeves of gold Met net, veiled alencon lace and chiffon. A hat of the same shade was worn, and she carried a bouquet of entrant, rose,, arid perms violets. Mr. David D. Cousin, Alloa, N.B. acted am he'd man. Later in the afternoon Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Whitton left for the South Coast, prior to their departure for Canada. The bride's going away dress was a beautiful shade of tomato chiffon velvet, with the bodice trimmed with flue gold braid and embroidery, and ivory and black lace introduced. The skirt was prettily arranged with cross draperies and little pipings of black satin. A large black picture hat trimmed with plumes was worn with silver grey fox furs. A large number of guests from Burton nod Warrington attern'ed the reception which was he'd at " Hr Grove," after the ceremony. Numerous and handsome presents were received. The Winmarleigh Cafe Co. supplied the wedding cake, and catered for the guests. Mr. and Mrs. Whitton left Liverpool fir Canada yesterday afterncwo per the S.S. • Empress of 'Britain."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TWELVE LICENCES TO BE OBJECTED TO</p>
        <p>No Singing Licences for Picture Palaces. There was comparatively little business of import- ance at the annual licensing sessions which were held at the Warrington Borough Police Court yesterday morning. The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) pre- sided and the other magisiates present were :—The DeFury Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), Aldermen Smethurst. Pierpoint, Bennett and Bolton, Councillors Roberts, Wilkinson and Jackson Holmes, Messrs. J. W. Hayward, H. Thornton, Edwin Woods, Pilling, T. Pemberton, W. T. Williams, S. Wright, W. Roberta, A. J. Jolley, T. Locker and W. Furness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>TR ANSFER.</head>
        <p>A TR ANSFER.</p>
        <p>On the application °Mfr. Browne, the licence of the Albion Hotel was transferred to Sarah Jenks, widow of the late licensee.</p>
        <p>LICENCES TO BE OBJECTED TO.</p>
        <p>- The Cleik announced that the whole of the alehouse licence* would be renewed, with the excep- tion of the following, which would be held over until the adjourned sessions in order that notices of objection might be given :—King's Arms, flutter- market-street; Britannia, Scotland-road; Waggon and Horse., Butterroarket-aireet ; Higher Angel, Buttermarket-street ; Royal Arms, Bank-etreet ; Bowling Green, Liverpool-road. Mr. Browne : I notice they are all Messrs. Greenall's houses. I appear for the firm. Mr. Steel : I appear on behalf of the Licensed Victnalleon Association and represent the tenants. The beerhouse licences were renewed with the exception of the Railway Tavern, Bank Quay and Factory lane •, Filecutterie Arm., Fennel-street ; Shakespeare, Pinner's-brow ; and the Brewer'. Arms, which were also adjourned. Two off-licences at 1 and 3, Jones' Building., and 40, Kerfoot-street, were also deferred until the adjourned sessions. MUSIC LICENCES APPLIED FOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne applied on behalf of the proprietora of the Park Picture Palace and Central Picture Palace for the restrictions with regard to music to be with- drawn. Ile pointed oat that in several other town. the magistrates allowed not only instrumental music but ringing to describe the Pictures on the screen. At , present the only sousie which the proprietors of these places of entertainment were allowed to provide was that of a pianoforte. It was ocmplained that continu- ous looking at the pictures wearied the eye, and an interval of ten minates for a song Cr instrumeetal selection would do no harm end afford the necessary relief.</p>
      </div>
