        <p>for private or public gatitoringt and to hold " long nights" in connection with is class's twice a year. There was only one vacant night each week on which the rooms could be used otlaerwb4 than for the classes.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY COMPANIES i NO THE COWPENSATION LE \ Y. Mr. C. E. Pinfold of the Cheshire L:nes Railway Company, and Mr. R. L. Mason, on beLlf of the London and North-Western Railway, applied, for a reduction of their contribution to the compen titan levy with regard to their refreshment comas. Ur. Plnfald pointed out that during the past you the sale of intoxicants at the refreshment rooms on the , C.L.C. showed a decrease of £33 10s. 11d. or three per cent, food an iticreese of three per cent, minerals an increase of oue per cent, and cigars a decrease of 11 per cent, the total takings showing a decrease of £2l. He submitted that the comoany tee entitled to an allowance of more than ote-third which was the present concession made by the Bench to railway companies—particularly having regard to the fact that they could never hope to get an ything from tha Icompensation fund. 4 Mr. Mason said figures which were taken last year for the whole of the refreshment rooms oei the L. and N.-W. Railway showed that thesale of intoxivants was leas than that of fool and non- intoxicants. The Chief Constable said the p slice heel no objets- tics to singing licences being granted for the picture palaces. . THE DECISIO6. / The magistrates retired, and J. their return the Mayor aenounced that Mr. Br ne's applicatioa for music licence. was refused as it also those of the railway companies with re ad to the refreshment rooms. Mr. Steel's applica on for the Independent Labour Party was granted, be hours being from 8.30 ' p.m. to 3.3 i a.m. The a lication on behalf of Mr. Boyle was refused, and :easional extensions would al before. have to TLerthe applieddjouroedliCoera@L'eg sessions were fixed for March 10th, THE CHIEF .ONSTABLE'S REPORT. In the course of a forth anneal report to the licensing justices et Chief Constable states that the number t.f lice ce-holders in the borough of all classes is 102. hese figures show • decrease of six as compared ilk last veer, the lapsed licenses being as follow, viz - Big Morse Shoe, Horeemarketetreet ; Lower Seth Stara, Bridge-street ; Rose and Crown, Bridge-str ; Bird-in-Hand, Bank-street ; and the off-1 ices. , 11, Brick-street and 5. Horsema rket-street The nun rof licencee is equal to one in every 3316 inhabits ts. Whit reference to the drunkennew, thete b. an increa of 99 is the number of persons proceeded against as compared milk the previous tear. The figures for the pest five yearn are as follows:- 19 ' ... 470 males and 114 females ... Total-584 1 .1 ... 384 ~ 1441 „ ... Total-483 _ ... 235 „ 73 „ ... Total-308 907 ... 220 „ 110 „ ... Total-330 1906 ... 235 „ 98 „ ... Total-333 Of the 584 persons proceeded spinet 419 were • eats of the borough, and 16.1 straagere to the There is an increase of 71 is the number of prosecuted for drunkenness@ during the year red with the the figures of the previous oaths. ye of the week on which drunkenness are as follow : —Sundae. 61 : Monday, ill ; Wednesday, 41; llursday. 32 ; Friday ay, rea. 'lle largest number of cue., 00, Bridge-street, Buttertnarketetreet coming . while Winwick-street and Horsemarket- -34 and 31 respectively. stirs table gives the number of persons inst for drunkenness in ten northern riding Warrington) for the year ended st, 1910. Only Middlesburgh is higher ton with a percentage of 1368 per 1,000 : Warriogton s figure is 900 ; St. Helens 316; and Huddersfield (the 1°,4801 89. 14 clubs registered in Ma Bon:Nigh, all r to have been mtiefactorily cosducted r, as no complaints have been made to inst any of them. par three licensees were proceeded flews, via :—John Stirrup, Traveller's, use, Fennel-street. for selling intoxi- to a drunken person; Fined the mote rum 21st, 1910. William Adams Ray, skimmers, Pinner's Brow, for selling iquor to a drunken person ; Fined 1041.</p>
