      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>AGED AND INFIRM. Opening of New Buildings at</head>
        <p>THE AGED AND INFIRM. Opening of New Buildings at</p>
        <p>tile Workhouse</p>
        <p>The nen block of buildings n Inch has been erected at the Warrington Workhouse for the accommodation of aged and infirm in- mates was formerly opened on Thursday afternoon by Mr. George Mather (chairman of the Board). The occasion was rightly regarded as marking an epoch in the ad- ministration of the Poor Law in the War- rington Union, and a large number of Guardians, Rural District Councillors, and other public' men assembled to witness the ceremony. The inmates of the Workhouse were also interested onlookers, and the band ot the Padgate Cottage Homes played selections.</p>
        <p>The cost of the new building (including furniture) is about C. 6,000. The new block consists of six wards and three day rooms, :giving a total accommodation of 114 beds.' There is Nothing in the way of external ornamentation, utility and economy having been the chief considerations. The building is constructed on the pavilion system, with alternative fire-proof staircases at each end. It is three storeys high, with basements and sub-basements, the latter being for greater convenience in carrying electric light, water, and heating pipes. The building has been erected by Mr. Harry Fairclough, to the designs of Messrs. Wm. and Segar Owen. The proceedings at the opening were very brief. Mr. Segar Owen said it was the architects' proud privilege on such occasions to hand to the opener of the building a little souvenir, and he had pleasure in hand- ing Mr. Mather a gold key, which had been specially designed by his brother, Mr. Geoffrey Owen. Explaining the purpose of the new block, Mr. Owen said it was to , accommodate those who were not qualified for the Infirmary, but who were too infirm to be in the House. The building was planned as part of a whole scheme. For, the time being it would accommodate both male and female inmates, but ultimately it would become a female ward alone, and a corresponding male ward would be built on the vacant land immediately opposite. Mr. Mather, in opening the building, said he hoped it might prove a hay2n of rest to old people who through no fault of their own had to seek shelter in the Workhouse (Applause). The key presented to Mr. Mather is of 15ct. gold, the handle being in the form of a wreath, with amethyst set in centre. The body of the key is octagonal in shape, and the wards are farmed of the figures 1911 in pierced work. The key, which has been made by Messrs. Eustance &amp; Co. Ltd., is lin miniature and can be used as a scarf brooch. LUNCHEON AT THE LION HOTEL.</p>
        <p>After the opening ceremony Mr. Mather entertained a large company of Guardians, officials and others at luncheon at the Lion Hotel. The loyal toasts were submitted from the chair and enthusiastically honoured.</p>
        <p>The Chairman proposed " sucjcess to the new building," and said, he believed the building would give satisfaction to the rate- payers, the Guardians, and even to the Local Government Board. Its occupants would have every care and attention at the hands of Mr. and Mrs. Awbery, Dr. Hodg- kinson, Miss Biggin, and Dr. Taylor, in all of whom the Guardians had implicit con- fidence (Applause). Mr. E. L. Bent, in responding, offered some criticisms of the building, chiefly on the ground that there were so many steps and staircases as to make it inconvenient for old people. He did not blame the archi- tects, who had followed out their instruc- tions, but he placed a great deal of the responsibility on the shoulders of the Local Government Board, who had the last deal- ings with the plans. He also considered the site was unsuitable.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Rushworth also responded and s.id the old people under the care of the G rdians were exceedingly well cared for, as was evidenced by the fact that very fe had taken advantage of the old age p)nsion. Mr. A. J. Jollev proposed "the Local Government Board," which, although not a popular institution, had, he considered, played an important and valuable part in the public life of the country. The reason for the undisguised feeling and hostility often shown towards the Local Government Board was accounted for by the fact that any body which had to exercise a super- vising, checking influence was bound to come into conflict occasionally with the people who were supervised and controlled. Mr. Arthur 11. Lowry, Local Government Board Inspector, responded and took occa- sion to reply to criticisms which had been levelled at a new order of the Board with regard to administration of out-relieving. He pointed out that, in spite of what had been said to the contrary, it left the powers of the Guardians to give outdoor relief to widows and children exactly what they were at the present moment. The order in- volved no initiative, no " bolt from the blue," on the part of the Local Govern- ment Board, but was merely the extension to Unions throughout the country of a policy which had been found to pay best in the most progressive Unions. Mr. W. Flanagan proposed " The Visi- tors," and Mr. T. J. Lee, in responding, said the new building was much more comfort- able than many of the homes of Warrington front which the old people came. I Alderman Bennett also responded. He ex- pressed the opinion that the new building might have been made a little more attrac- tive outside. Public men were apt to overlook the fact that there was some- thing to be considered besides mere utility, the beauty itself was in a sense utility, and that by pleasing the eye and appealing to the fine instincts of the human race, they were not wasting money but spending it' fn the best way possible. Mr. A. Brooks, who proposed the health of "The Building Committee," pointed out that the Local Government Board excised several ornamental features and insisted upon the building being extremely plain. Mr. W. Furness, responde.d. to the toast. . .</p>
        <p>The toast of " The architect and con- tractor " was proposed by Mr. William Houghton, and responded to by Mr. S. Owen and Mr. H. Fairclough. . .</p>
        <p>The health of the chairinan was enthusi- astically honoured on the proposition of Alderman James Evans, seconded by the Rev. T. R. Matthews.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
