      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHARITES.</head>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHARITES.</p>
        <p>Warrington District No 4.</p>
        <p>Tbe annual no-o-ttng of thn bova D.strict was held lo th•Coogregatiomal schoolroom, Aabtou-la-Makerfield on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Th.Cooilmu called to order shoat 3 p.m. by 800 C S Fellows, D C The roll of officers and repre- sentatives wee celled, wb.o 12 oflioars and I representa- tives responded to the call, only ODG representative from the Hope of Bortonnood being absent. . . . . .</p>
        <p>A notice 4 motion from Charity tent to amend rule six, clause three, was defeated.</p>
        <p>A report f rom • .tit-committ., appointed to Mai. 9 on effort to open a branch of the order at West-street, Goopel Hall, Warrington, was accepted, and left in the hand, of the committee to carry out. Bro. B. W LKIR, U.S J.T., presented his report of the work done in the juvenile tents daring the past year. The returns shooed an incur., of over 200 jnyeeiles makieg a total memberebip of 1991 member. and total fund., £524 11.. id. which was considered very satisfactory. Hope of Sankey tent wss the winner of the district challense, Devil and Block abovring the highest peicentage of new membem during 1910.</p>
        <p>Bro. T. WINSTANLEV, OS., In bin report, stated that one matter for congratulation wee that tbe drink bill of thin country was atilt on the down grade, nod although locally it vow hardly perceivable, he did not think it required much of an oi.timiat to my that exceseive and even moderate drinking we on the &amp;atm, and the general tone of the people wee to look at tbe drink evil Iron) the Temperance work leg point of view. One matter called for great regret end some action both collectively se an Order, end separately as toots and districts, and that was the growth of clubs regletered for the sale of drink. He had no hesitation In eitying that they were worse than public- houses. (Applause.) The very least that should be done was to place them in the same ' position se licensed hoe.. In regard to bents of opening sod closing sod police teipervision. Okpplreme4 The ennual balance sheet was promoted which showed the District Sick fend to be £3,413 Oe. 9.1 ,an incr.). of £3OO during the year District funeral fund £2762 Ils. Id., being an increase) of £250, Manage- ment funds In 4s. 110. d. making total foods of the District £G736 16s. al.l. The investments were as follows Warrington Saving Bank, £360 IS., lid. Mortgage securitie• £lOl2, Land and Buildings £5242 IS.. 7d, Invested with the Board of Directors £199. Cult In Treasurers bands LIS ts. 21d. The auditors Bro. Ft Fernley and B. Warburton reported that the books and accounts were highly witisfactury. The election of officers took place which resulted in the following being elected ;—Bro. J E Gee, 1)12 It; Bro. J R Barker, DI) II Bro. D Hamblett, Levitt.: Bro. SI Fernley, Guardian, Bro CS Fellones, l' I) C and Juvenile Dletrict Superintendent ; Bro. T Winstenley, DS ; Bro. T Morey, T ; trostees, Bros. J Shaw, E Walkden and N' Wright ; auditors, Bro. B Warburton and T Length..; property collectors, Wigan property, Bro. T Winstonley ; Earlestown pro- perty, B S Fellow.); Golboroe property, it Has.; Edge Green property, J Sproeton. _ _</p>
        <p>There were six nominations for two reprseentativen to the H. M. C. to be held at Southport neat August The two representatives elected wens Bros. 4. E. Gs. land 4. R. Barker.</p>
        <p>Au levitation to bold th• text district meths, at Earlestovrn lois annoyed. An excellent tea *as provided in the schoolroom and efterivards • vote of thanks can ably moved by Bro. S. Fernley and seconded by Bro. D.Hamblett foe the pro- vision made for the comfort of tbe titters and repres- entative,</p>
        <p>-71;i; WU, suitably ackaosiodged by Wimtaaley and %I ism Corless.</p>
        <p>During the past quarter they had a net innrsuis of 11 member. making • total increase for the year of 63, and comprising 21 *dolt tents in the diatrict with a menthership of 1130. The rotors. in conneetion with the valuation of the district required by the Registrar of Friendly Soeietiee were well in heed, and they were I looking forward with every eatisfection that the sick I and funeral funds of the district would show ssoo • better position of aolveney time in the 1963 valuation.</p>
