        <head>MR CROSFIELD AT BILLIARDS Match With a Yorkshire Professional.</head>
        <p>MR CROSFIELD AT BILLIARDS Match With a Yorkshire Professional.</p>
        <p>Mr. A 11. Croali,.: : • Warringtoa Liberal Club on Monday e‘t.lic.g t•Dgageti in a match at billiards with George Nelson, a well.known Yorks professional. There was a large gathering of members of the club to witness the encounter, and Mr. Crosfield bad a very cordial welcome. Before mitering upon the match. Mr. Croafield briefly eddreesed the company. Be said that during the peat ten veers he had not played ten games at billiards. That all arose from taking np an active political career. Billiards got the go-by and if Mr. Nelson valued his career at billiards he must beware of tackling • constituency. (Laughter.) " Asa matter of fact" continued Mr..Crosfield " now that my brother and I have taken a bootie together at Lodge-lane, one might have a cluince of working ups little bit of the old form at billiards, as we shall have an excellrot table there and therefore if Mr. Nelson comes to Warrington in the future. I might be ebb, to put up a rather better game for him. This' evening I am prepared to be thor uglily well laid-out. (Liughterd I have no doubt I shall he even more severely IA out on Wednesday evoning—lhmghteo —in the debate which, in a rash moment,l have under- taken. In fact, with this double prospect of a good drubbing, I never in my life felt more entirely like in Limb being led to the slaughter." (Much laughter) Mr. Crosfield concluded by expressing the hope that under the circumstance% his friends would make allowance for lack of practice and more than indifferent form at</p>
        <p>--7111; professional conceded Ilr.Croefield 2.01 start out cr 100sed succeeded in defeating him without much trouble. Nelson's highest break was o 000( 71 aud ILa secured several of 0).</p>
