      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE "The Face it the Window.'</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE "The Face it the Window.'</p>
        <p>There are no half measures al, ,• 'Hie Face at the Window." It is a French ruelodt.,,a ~f the most sensational. blood-curdling type, and al a .•,,,aieriamace is highly popular with the " gods " who have been demonstrative to a degree unusual even with them. To do the author justice, it mu.t be,0111,11.1 that the play is ingeniously conreive.l and cleverly worked out, excitement being piled upon excitement in a way which rivets the attention. 'I he duel of wits between he Loop, the master criminal, and Paul alone, a sort of French Ilawkshaw, the detective, is fascinating to the observer who is prepared for any curious development. But the most case- hardened play-goer is unpleasantly thrilled by the Vied wail and mysterious ai.pearances of the ghastly "face at the window," which is always the harbinger of tragedy. Messrs. F. Slariott Watson and James Umber's company gives a thoroughly realistic and convincing performance. Mr. W. Haughton Macauley displays conspicuou. talent as The Chevalier, alias he Loup, his " make up" lining the moat villainous imaginable, and he succeeds in making himself so heartily detested as to draw forth hisses from the gallery—a compliment' which the . .</p>
        <p>actor most must appreciate. Mr. T. W. Ounecombe portrays Paul Gouffet, the indomitable detective. se to the manner born. Miss Constance Glenalyn is a charming Marie Bresson, the Banker's 'daughter, and quickly enlisted the sympathim of th •UdiYOCIf. All the other part. ars in good bands special praise being due to Mr. J. Weston (Henry de Breeson, the banker . Mr. Richard i /liver (Lucian Cortier), Mr. E. Cheater (Peter Pottleberry). and M Bessie Franks (Ilabette) the too mat-amused hein responsible for the humorous element. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
